":\"### 16.2 The build\"},{\"line_number\":926,\"content\":\"### 16.3 Browser flags for headless WebGPU\"},{\"line_number\":940,\"content\":\"### 16.4 Renderer boot\"},{\"line_number\":958,\"content\":\"### 16.5 The ink filter (run exactly as written, on both backends)\"},{\"line_number\":1038,\"content\":\"### 16.6 Materials\"},{\"line_number\":1067,\"content\":\"### 16.7 Rapier\"},{\"line_number\":1085,\"content\":\"### 16.8 Pad stub for tests\"},{\"line_number\":1101,\"content\":\"## 17. Testing\"},{\"line_number\":1147,\"content\":\"## 18. Build order\"},{\"line_number\":1167,\"content\":\"## 19. Finishing: About screen, exit interview, catalogue\"},{\"line_number\":1191,\"content\":\"## 20. Brief coverage checklist\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"2e522565-f181-4a93-8064-dc13772d3bf4"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_3","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a massive game implementation task.\n\nThe goal plan must capture observable outcomes from the stage, plan, and task files. Acceptance criteria should be small and grouped by related mechanics. Architecture details belong in PLAN.md itself, not in the goal plan. However, PLAN.md is extremely prescriptive about file names, exact mechanics, and values, which creates tension with the goal plan writer's instruction to avoid prescribing the solution.\n\nThe contract is PLAN.md plus TASK.md. Vague criteria risk a verifier accepting a game that violates the plan. Copying every detail into criteria would make the goal unfinishable. The instruction requires keeping acceptance criteria small and satisficing, never an exhaustive conjunction.\n\nA single criterion may group several closely-related mechanics into one checkable outcome. Unrequested features belong under Non-goals. The game's defining mechanics are already specified in PLAN.md, so the criteria should fold those core systems directly.\n\nThe hand-in requirements from TASK.md are now explicit. The deliverables include a dev subfolder, a single HTML file in the root, PROGRESS.md, an exit-interview.json with the exact schema, and an About/Credits screen containing Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback.\n\nThe game title is randomly selected from fifty brainstormed titles and stored only in identity.js. Verifiers must know the naming pattern without being locked to a specific title. The source title is \"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\" from TASK.md. The filename and folder name follow a strict template that embeds this source title literally.\n\nTASK.md lists three hardware constraints that must be emulated: no physical controller, no touch device, and no human listener. The game must therefore use stub controllers, Playwright profiles for touch input, and OfflineAudioContext for audio.\n\nAdditional hardware constraints from TASK.md include no second player, no real iPhone, no TV, and no display for runs. The game must run on two browser pages, use a WebKit profile, and capture 1920×1080 screenshots. xvfb is required for headed runs. Headless Firefox cannot support WebGL2, so that path is abandoned. WebGPU screenshots may be blank, but PLAN.md guarantees they work with five GPU flags enabled. Gamepad audio unlock and rumble cannot be proven or tested beyond guards. The hub harness tests are part of the computer-based deliverables.\n\nThe local hub harness is downloaded from GitHub and runs locally, not as a remote CI. It serves as the gate for computer-based deliverables.\n\nFor visual or interactive games, the harness cannot drive end-to-end gameplay. Criteria must anchor on static or structural fallbacks instead of requiring human play or observation. The artifact must exist. Pure logic units must be exercised by real unit tests. Every browser-loaded script must provably load.\n\nThe entry-point launch check requires Playwright to probe for a headless browser, serve and load the page, assert zero page errors, verify the render surface matches its target dimensions, confirm the surface is substantially filled, validate that driven input produces the expected visible change, and capture a screenshot.\n\nThe launcher may be unable to run here, so captured evidence of that failure is acceptable. PLAN.md §16.3 confirms the hub's GPU flags enable pixel readback and frame drawing. A successful readback that returns blank or partial data is the app's output and must be fixed rather than falling back. Software rendering is slow at 260–410 ms per frame, so the launch check should use test.step instead of waiting on frames.\n\nThe acceptance criteria will be three to five gating outcomes grouped by defining mechanics. The first group covers the core surgical loop: beat clock, vitals and timer, eight tools plus syringe and defib with their rules, grades and ranks, composure gifts, difficulty and checkpoints, and assist.\n\nThe remaining three criteria cover content, presentation and controls, and multiplayer. Content requires seven surgical strains, operations one through seven plus Last Patient and Grand Rounds, story scenes with required beats, and an original cast. Presentation and controls require Three.js R180 WebGPU with ink and cel shaders, Rapier physics, synthesized Web Audio, a gamepad-first ten-foot UI, glyphs, touch notices, attract mode, and dev mode. Multiplayer requires an AI Hub shared-world of one to four players plus couch co-op, with the hub harness passing. The deliverables criterion covers the single HTML file, exit-interview.json, about screen, PROGRESS.md, dev folder, and green tests. This yields five gating outcomes. No single holistic end-to-end gate is written.\n\nEach criterion must be atomic and independently checkable from its own start state. The first criterion is the shipped package and identity: a single self-contained HTML file exists in the work folder root, named with the chosen title, source title, and timestamp, built from a matching dev subfolder.\n\nThe first criterion now specifies concrete loading and structural checks. The HTML must load on file:// and http://127.0.0.1 without external script or font URLs. It must embed the unmodified AI Hub session client once with a SHA-256 matching the release, include required hub meta tags, and expose `window.__game`. The title appears only in the identity module. The deliverables criterion also requires exit-interview.json beside the HTML with the required shape, PROGRESS.md, and an About/Credits screen showing the four feedback blocks and parsing `#about-data`.\n\nThe surgical simulation criterion requires the headless engine to implement the beat clock, vitals and timer, scoring grades and ranks, all ten tools including defib, composure gifts, difficulty checkpoints, and consult effects. Node tests must drive the shipped simulator directly rather than re-implementing it. Determinism is enforced by seeding with identical inputs and rejecting any Math.random usage. Data lint checks ensure drape window coverage, full tool and gift inclusion, and no \"good\" in Bea's lines outside the final scene.\n\nThe operations and story criterion requires all seven REQUIEM strains to behave per the plan. Operations one through seven, Last Patient, and Grand Rounds must exist as data and be completable.\n\nBot playthroughs on Normal must succeed each operation with a completion time of forty-five to seventy-five percent of the timer, minimum vitals of thirty-five, and rank at least A. Last Patient completes without timer or rank constraints.\n\nSloppy bot playthroughs on Easy must achieve rank at least C. Idle playthroughs must fail every operation except Last Patient.\n\nThe full story order through Last Patient and Grand Rounds must complete in the simulator with one to four seats. The narrative must contain the required scenes and beats, span two hundred fifty to three hundred fifty lines, and use original characters without banned source names.\n\nThe fifth criterion covers the playable shell. The browser must boot on WebGPU and WebGL2 with zero page errors, a non-blank render at the intended size, and a substantially filled surface. Input must drive the surgical tip and tools via pad, mouse, or keys; prompts must follow the last active device; an Xbox glyph option must be present; touch-only mode must display the exact \"Controller or keyboard required\" notice plus Settings, omitting touch controls; and attract mode must start after ten seconds of idle time and exit on any input without saving. Dev mode must be reachable from pause with tools and live sliders that modify the simulation and reset it. Audio must use synthesized tracks, sound effects, and voices through OfflineAudioContext with a default music gain of 0.10, and no external audio files may be used.\n\nSave data must survive a reload. The UI playthrough must reach Title, Main Menu, Story, Chart, Operation, Report, and Credits. Screens must include fail, free play, lobby, settings, and about.\n\nCouch co-op requires two pads with independent tips and scoring. The hub harness must pass for two-player, three-player bad network, four-player bad network, host-leave, server, and four-player without WebGPU flags. It must show `window.__hubCheck.pass === true` and no `[AIHub]` console lines. Standalone mode must use an Enter/Space sequence, leave `AIHub.active` false, leave seeded randomness untouched, and capture a non-blank screenshot. Shared-world seats one to four must display own cards, and host-authoritative snapshots must stay under size limits.\n\nThe verification plan maps directly to the five gating criteria. Visual games require unit tests for pure logic and browser script loads that expose a window global while avoiding Node globals. The built HTML must be a module script with its bundle inlined so it works from file:// without network imports.\n\nA gating launch check must run on the real entry path. It serves the HTML, loads it, asserts zero page errors, checks drawing dimensions and a substantially filled canvas, confirms driven input produces visible change, and captures a screenshot. This check must pass more than once for consistent success.\n\nBecause the repository is greenfield with no existing tests, the verification plan should add a gating step that exercises the test command `node tests/run-all.mjs` as specified in PLAN.md.\n\nThe gating step must implement the tests and run them, capturing output to the scratch folder. The plan must preserve every must-have term from TASK.md verbatim, including Three.js R180, WebGPU, stylized cel-shaded ink filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, single HTML file, code-based audio, Animalese or Simlish voices, and default music gain of ten percent.\n\nThe plan should describe deliverables as outcomes rather than fixed file paths. The `node tests/run-all.mjs` command is the implementer's responsibility under PLAN.md, so the verifier must accept that exact path as the proof mechanism.\n\nThe goal plan must not freeze the implementer's architecture. PLAN.md is the design document, and the goal plan is a separate contract for what was supposed to happen. If the implementer diverges from PLAN.md's required systems, that is a failure. Internal file names specified by PLAN.md are part of the required outcomes.\n\nThe test harness must be a single command that prints only failures and a one-line count.\n\nTitle selection must be a playful riff chosen by a real random number generator after brainstorming fifty options. Originality must avoid any source IP names.\n\nPhysical DualSense rumble, real iOS Safari, a second human player, Firefox WebGL2, gamepad-only audio unlock proof, FPS optimization, internet multiplayer outside the hub, and performance targets are explicitly out of scope.\n\nWebGPU software rendering is slow and may require up to five minutes. Earlyoom can kill browsers if memory is exhausted. Source filenames may contain colons and spaces, but Linux tolerates them. The title is chosen by a random number generator after brainstorming fifty options, so deliverable names depend on that result. WebGPU screenshots must use the exact five flags from PLAN.md §16.3; incorrect flags produce blank output. Headless Playwright runs are acceptable. The hub client version may differ from the 1.4.0 hash if PLAN.md specifies a newer release.\n\nScope is enormous, so the plan must group criteria into three to five gating outcomes. Budget shortening is PLAN.md's implementer guidance, not a non-goal. Required systems must not be listed as non-goals.\n\nSource IP names such as GUILT and Healing Touch are constraints, not non-goals. The vertical slice is the demo, not a full-length franchise. Touch controls are explicitly forbidden. Custom player names, chat, emotes, and power-ups are out of scope.\n\nThe work folder, a new dev subfolder, the root HTML, exit-interview.json, PROGRESS.md, npm dependencies for Three.js, Rapier, esbuild, Playwright, and fonts, plus the hub session.js and harness from GitHub are the required deliverables.\n\nThe implementation will follow PLAN.md §15 and §18. The simulation will be pure logic testable in Node without Three.js, DOM, or audio. The view layer will reconcile state. A single command will run the tests.\n\nThe plan will condense the build order into a three-to-eight step task checklist. Every criterion will be tagged as gating or evidence in the verification plan.\n\nThe browser gating check must run twice. It asserts a substantially filled canvas with correct dimensions, visible change from driven input, and a screenshot saved under the scratch folder.\n\nThe harness verifiers will audit real in-repo tests plus captured run output under the scratch folder.\n\nThe `node tests/run-all.mjs sim` command is the first gating criterion. It must print only failures and a one-line count, exit zero, and observe determinism, tool rules, strain behaviors, bot invariants such as vitals above thirty-five and rank at least A, sloppy bot behavior, idle failure, full story order, grand rounds one through four, data lint, snapshot size limits, and no Math usage.\n\nThe browser gating step runs `node tests/run-all.mjs browser` and captures output to `{SCRATCH}/browser.log`. It verifies WebGPU and WebGL2 boot, no page errors, non-blank screenshots, input distances, prompts, dev sliders, a ten-second attract mode, the exact touch notice text, audio that is not silent when offline, music at ten percent gain, save-and-reload behavior, about fields, couch two pads, UI playthrough screens, and packaging checks.\n\nThe hub gating step runs `node tests/run-all.mjs hub` and captures output to `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`. It validates all five hub scenarios plus a four-player WebGL2 test and the standalone sequence. It asserts `window.__hubCheck.pass === true` and no `[AIHub]` console lines.\n\nThe launch check must run twice with Playwright using the five GPU flags. It asserts canvas and render size match the viewport, a high painted fraction, and driven input changing tip position in `__game.state()`. Two screenshots are saved to `{SCRATCH}`. If the launcher cannot run, the plan falls back to sim tests and structural checks. A blank readback is treated as a failure that must be fixed.\n\nA fifth gating step covers static checks on the delivered files. It verifies that exit-interview.json parses and contains required keys, that the HTML hub metas appear in exact attribute order, that the bundle is a single file with no external scripts, that the client hash is present, that PROGRESS.md exists, that banned source strings are absent, that the title appears only in identity, and that no TODO or FIXME or placeholder or stub remains in the source. Some of these checks overlap with the browser packaging test, but duplicating them is acceptable if the verification plan is the procedure. The plan must not become a huge duplicate of PLAN §17.\n\nTests must drive shipped functions rather than reimplement logic. The `window.__game` and `?test=1` hooks are required observable state from PLAN.md, so they are valid testable interfaces. The goal writer must avoid setting bars that can only be met by building a verifier oracle.\n\nThe launch check must exercise the real HTML entry point, not merely test hooks. A non-empty canvas is insufficient; the operative observable is a substantially painted field that advances game state when input is applied. The boot sequence must display \"Scrubbing in…\" before reaching the title screen. Criterion wording is being tightened so a small-model verifier can check it directly.\n\nThe work folder must contain PROGRESS.md, a root HTML file, and a dev subfolder. The HTML filename and folder name share the same playful Title prefix plus the fixed suffix from PLAN §19. exit-interview.json must parse as one JSON object with sourceInformation, testingFeedback, engineFeedback, promptFeedback, and aiHub. The aiHub block requires usage \"added on import\", type Game, genre Simulation, source \"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\", series \"Trauma Center\", and players \"1–4\".\n\nThe Title prefix is chosen by brainstorming fifty options and a real RNG. The logo tolerates any length. The title string appears in no other source file.\n\nThe HTML must open from disk or localhost with inlined scripts and fonts, no external URLs. It must contain exactly one unmodified AI Hub session client whose SHA-256 matches the vendored release. Hub meta tags must appear in the exact order and content of PLAN §14.6, including no `hub:webgpu=required` attribute. The catalogue must list title, description, theme-color, icon, type, players, and source. An About/Credits screen must exist with a `#about-data` JSON block holding Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback.\n\nThe package criterion is dense but remains a single checkable deliverable. Criterion 2 will cover the simulation.\n\nThe simulation runs headless at 1/60 with a seeded RNG and no Math.random. It implements the beat clock, vitals, timer, and miss failure. Grades are FLAWLESS, CLEAN, ROUGH, or MISS. Ranks are S, A, B, C, or D. The wheel tools, syringe, defibrillator, Hush, and Sforzando follow PLAN §4 rules. Difficulty multipliers, phase checkpoints that restore vitals above sixty and timer above ninety seconds, Bea's Notes after two failures, and consult effects are included.\n\nCriterion 2 now requires identical seeds and inputs to produce the same state hash twice. Snapshot messages at the busiest four-seat Grand Rounds moment must stay under 1100 bytes for the unreliable snapshot and under 16 KB for the keyframe. Data lint checks that touchable spawns sit inside the drape ellipse, the teaches union covers eight wheel tools, syringe, defibrillator, both gifts, and all seven strains, and no Bea line contains \"good\" except the final scene. Criterion 3 will cover bot-playable content.\n\nThe demo must include the original operations Broken Glass, Growth Spurt, Adagio, Staccato, Legato, Fermata, Coda, Last Patient, and Grand Rounds. The seven strains behave as specified. Story scenes S0 through S16 contain must-have lines and 250 to 350 lines in the source's soap-opera tone. The named original cast appears and banned source names are absent.\n\nA bot using only simulation state on Normal must complete each operation with a clear time of 45 to 75 percent of the timer. Minimum vitals must stay at least thirty-five and rank must be at least A. Par scores come from that bot.\n\nA sloppy bot on Easy must finish at rank C or higher. Idle input fails every operation except Last Patient. Story order through Last Patient and Grand Rounds completes at one, two, three, and four seats. Phases are self-contained so skip-to-phase works. Criterion 4 will cover browser presentation, input, and shell.\n\nThe browser entry point renders a Three.js r180 operative field with cel-shading and ink outlines. It uses WebGPU when the five §16.3 flags are active, otherwise WebGL2. The page must show zero errors and a substantially filled render surface at target size. Rapier physics drives pulls such as forceps tension and tray pile. All music, sound effects, and voices are generated by Web Audio with no external audio files; the music bus defaults to gain 0.10.\n\nGamepad, keyboard, and mouse all drive the same screens. Tip movement, the eight-way tool wheel, analog use, and prompts follow the last active device. PlayStation glyphs appear by default; Xbox mode is selectable in Settings.\n\nTouch-only devices show exactly \"Controller or keyboard required\" and a working Settings button. No touch controls are present. Attract mode still runs on those devices.\n\nAttract mode starts after ten seconds of idle input on the title, menu, free-play, and touch-notice screens. Any input exits it without writing a save.\n\nDev mode is reachable from the pause menu with standard tools and live tuning sliders that change play immediately and reset to documented defaults. Save and load preserve progress.\n\nA scripted UI playthrough must reach Title, Main Menu, Story, Chart, Operation, Report, and Credits.\n\nCriterion 5 covers multiplayer. The hub connects in shared-world mode for one to four seats with an own card. Without a hub the game starts standalone. The host runs the simulation; clients render snapshots. The own tip stays local. Two pads can join for Couch Grand Rounds with independent tips and dual scoring.\n\nThe real hub harness passes with two players. Three and four player sessions run at 150±50 ms and 5% loss including a leave. Host-leave and server copy also pass. The four-player WebGL2 case repeats successfully. Each run shows `__hubCheck.pass === true` and no `[AIHub]` console lines.\n\nStandalone mode opens the HTML. A scripted Enter, Space, Enter, Space sequence throws nothing. `AIHub.active` is false. `Math.random` is untouched. The screenshot is not blank.\n\nSession rules ban titles menus attract mode dev mode and saves inside a session. Pause is settings only. The simulation never pauses. `finish()` runs only on the host. It returns standings scores won stats and awards.\n\nThe verifier might nitpick missing juice items. §9.7 lists art and animation specifics as non-optional polish. The defining mechanics are the surgical loop. The named thing is this specific plan's game, not generic surgery.\n\nJuice, busts, ink filter, and audio character will be folded into Criterion 4 as presentation requirements. The operating view must include the ink filter, toon organs, instrument meshes, grade stamps, Hush wash, and defib flash. Story scenes must use 3D busts and the must-have staging. The public-domain Dies Irae motif must serve as Voss's signature.\n\nFont size rules are structural checks against the CSS rem scale rather than visual tests. Screenshots serve as evidence for these requirements.\n\nCriterion 4 must also include every tuning ID from the dev mode table, each with live sliders and a documented reset.\n\nThe exhaustive slider table will be referenced as \"the tuning values named in PLAN §13\" rather than listed in full. PLAN.md remains the source of truth for defaults and ranges.\n\nA risk is noted: the software WebGPU path can run 260–410 ms per frame, which may trigger earlyoom and kill browsers. The hub waits up to 300 seconds, so only one browser should be launched.\n\nIf the hub harness fails from memory pressure, the failure must be captured but the criterion stays gating. The client hash may not match 1.4.0 if upstream moved; verify against the RELEASES file as PLAN says. A colon in a filename is forbidden. Playwright headless mode needs no DISPLAY variable; xvfb is only for headed runs. Gamepad audio unlock and rumble hardware cannot be proven here, so test the guard and the OfflineAudioContext path and note the gap. Firefox WebGL2 is unavailable and is not required. FPS optimization and performance budgets are non-goals.\n\nSelf-hosted internet multiplayer outside the AI Hub is not required. Touch gameplay controls are excluded. Real DualSense hardware, iOS Safari, audible listening tests, and a second human are not required. The source IP names Derek Stiles, GUILT, Healing Touch, and Caduceus are forbidden. Playable Opus 2 is a post-credits tease, not a playable operation, so it is a non-goal. The authoring document docs/AUTHORING.md must be created per PLAN §18 step 9 and §20.\n\nCriterion 1 must include an authoring note explaining how to add operations, strains, story scenes, and tuning values. This is a PLAN deliverable.\n\nPROGRESS.md is STAGE.md, not PLAN.\n\nThe implementer must place feedback in both the chat reply and the About screen. The goal plan should require that the About screen contains the answers and that exit-interview.json holds the same answers. The chat reply is the implementer's final message.\n\nThe goal is a code-change task. The plan will be short and testability-focused, using PLAN §15 and §18 as guidance rather than contract. Simulation and view will remain separate. A condensed eight-step checklist ending with test and evidence will guide implementation. Because the objective names a named game, defining mechanics must be researched first.\n\nThe OBJECTIVE does not name Trauma Center, so no web research is needed. PLAN already encodes the defining mechanics and explicitly rejects several original elements, such as star drawing and XS rank. Adding those rejected mechanics would violate the rule that the OBJECTIVE's explicit words always win. The filename is being double-checked against TASK.\n\nThe filename template uses square brackets as matrix placeholders, not literal characters. The source title Trauma Center: Second Opinion must appear verbatim inside those brackets.\n\nThe filename must not include the square brackets. The source title should appear verbatim, including spaces and the colon, because TASK requires it and Linux allows them; no sanitization is needed unless it breaks. The work folder root holds the HTML and exit-interview.json, while development files go in the subfolder.\n\nThe single HTML is copied to the work folder root from dist per PLAN. The plan will be tightened to stay short and concrete for small models, grouping related mechanics into criteria without dropping core requirements. Each verification step needs an explicit action and its expected observations. The harness must run the launch more than once. Screenshot review at 1920×1080 is evidence for ten-foot legibility; PLAN says tests cannot judge this, so it is a visual check and fix.\n\nLaunch with pixel fill is gating because the environment can perform it. The test suite must print only failures and a one-line count; noise causes the brief to fail. The plan file is ready to be written.\n\nThe write tool will create the plan file at the session path. Criterion 3’s bot time window is kept as a gating item because PLAN §17 lists it as an explicit done condition.\n\nClear time is gated at 45% to 75% of the timer per PLAN. Last Patient has no HUD, no timer, and cannot fail; it completes automatically. Grand Rounds scaling applies counts × (1 + 0.5 × (players−1)) and HP × (1 + 0.25 × (players−1)). Criterion 3 will ban Trauma Center clones. Browser launch verification requires a non-blank screenshot and a painted canvas area covering most of the viewport, not merely a single pixel readback.\n\nViewport dimensions must match the set viewport to catch stale renderer sizes. Driven input verification requires both a state change and a visible pixel difference; for 3D scenes, the tip position change is the reliable observable. If WebGPU readback is blank despite the five flags, that is an application bug to fix rather than a fallback.\n\nA risk is added: TASK.md machine notes list a shorter WebGPU flag set that PLAN §16.3 says loses the device, so the five-flag recipe from PLAN must be used instead.\n\nThe plan will use file:// for the launch because the bundle is inlined and WebGPU supports it. The hub harness will run an in-process HTTP server on 127.0.0.1 port 0; this is part of the hub tests, not a separate product. The document will include an eight-item checklist from PLAN §18, an implementation approach, and a risks section.\n\nCheckboxes are forbidden outside the task checklist. Criteria will be numbered and concrete, with one checkable outcome each. Verification steps map one-to-one to criteria. Simulation tests must assert the rules directly; the verification step requires adding those assertions and running the suite so it exits zero with captured output.\n\nTests must not hardcode expected values or mock the unit under test. The bot playthrough computes results from the simulation, so tests should assert invariants such as rank, time band, and vitals rather than golden hashes of whole operations. A determinism test can compare two runs against each other instead of using a hardcoded hash. Snapshot byte size is a valid assertion. PROGRESS.md updates should be treated as an ongoing task step.\n\nThe title screen logo must be generated dynamically to fit the chosen title rather than using a fixed bitmap. Criterion 1 will require that the title is an original playful riff, not the source title, and appear only in the identity module. Verification can confirm identity.js exists and the title is not a source trademark. The implementer need not prove they brainstormed fifty titles.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"PLAN.md is the spec. I’ll turn its required outcomes into a short gating plan, without freezing how the game is built."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-1c60f8eb-755f-412f-83ff-75bc16ed519d-6","name":"write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6%2Fwork/01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710/goal/plan-01a1aa56-6405-7d60-8230-1a9d7b05a19c.md","content":"# Plan: Build the surgical-action demo PLAN.md specifies, and hand in every artifact TASK.md and STAGE.md require\n\n## Goal kind\ncode-change\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n1. Hand-in package. The work folder contains `PROGRESS.md`, a parsable `exit-interview.json` beside the game HTML (one object, every TASK.md key filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, `aiHub.type` Game, `aiHub.genre` Simulation, `aiHub.source` `Trauma Center: Second Opinion`, `aiHub.series` `Trauma Center`, `aiHub.players` `1–4`, controls and tags as PLAN §19), a dev subfolder named `{Title}-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230`, and one root file `{Title}-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230.html`. `{Title}` is an original playful riff on the source (50 titles brainstormed, then one picked with a real RNG), shown on the title screen, and stored so no other source file contains that string. The HTML is one self-contained file that runs from `file://` and from `http://127.0.0.1` (scripts and fonts inlined; no external script or font URLs). It includes exactly one unmodified AI Hub session client (SHA-256 equals the released hash for the vendored version), the §14.6 hub meta tags in that exact `name`-then-`content` order and wording (no `hub:webgpu=required`), catalogue tags (`title` split on the dash, description, `theme-color` `#17707A`, inline SVG icon, `hub:type` Game, `hub:players` `1–4`, `hub:source` `Trauma Center: Second Opinion`), and an About/Credits screen whose `#about-data` JSON shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback with PLAN §19’s fixed values. The same four blocks are in the finishing chat reply. Own source has no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, or `stub`. A short authoring note explains how to add an operation, a strain, a story scene, and a tuning value.\n\n2. Surgical simulation. A headless sim (no renderer, DOM, or audio) steps at fixed 1/60 from seeded RNG only (`Math.random` absent from sim code). It implements the beat clock (`bpm` rises as vitals fall; beat drives tissue, ECG, strains, music tempo); vitals, timer, and miss failures; grades FLAWLESS/CLEAN/ROUGH/MISS, combo, and ranks S/A/B/C/D titled Chief of Surgery / Attending / Resident / Intern / Malpractice Suit Pending (never XS, Bad, Good, or Cool); all eight wheel tools plus syringe and defibrillator with the PLAN §4 rules (including analog use, nicks, tears, clamp-on-beat, defib gold band, clamps off before the end); Hush and Sforzando; Intern/Resident/Attending multipliers; phase checkpoints (retry restores vitals ≥60, timer ≥90s, checkpoint score) and optional Bea’s Notes after two failures of the same phase; consults (careful vs aggressive). Same seed and inputs yield the same state hash twice. At the busiest 4-seat Grand Rounds moment the unreliable snapshot is ≤1100 bytes and the keyframe ≤16 KB. Data lint: every touchable spawn lies inside the drape ellipse, `teaches` covers the eight wheel tools, syringe, defib, both gifts, and all seven strains, and no Bea line contains “good” except the final scene.\n\n3. Demo content a bot can finish. Playable vertical slice: operations Broken Glass, Growth Spurt, Adagio, Staccato, Legato, Fermata, Coda, Last Patient, and Grand Rounds, with strains Adagio, Staccato, Tremolo, Legato, Crescendo, Fermata, and Coda behaving as in PLAN §5, original cast and setting (Port Calloway, St. Alder, Ward Zero, Ren, Bea, Bell, Pip, Sorrento, Ines, Voss/REQUIEM/the Hush — never Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Caduceus, Delphi, GUILT or its strain names, Healing Touch, star drawing, or Hope Hospital). Story scenes S0–S16, 250–350 lines, must-have lines present, scenes skippable. Phases are data and `skipTo` works. On Normal, a bot that reads only sim state clears each operation (Last Patient only has to complete: no timer, no rank, cannot fail) in 45–75% of that op’s timer, minimum vitals ≥35, rank ≥A; `parScore` is taken from that bot. A sloppy bot (noise and reaction delay) on Easy clears at rank ≥C. No input fails every op except Last Patient. Story order through Last Patient, and Grand Rounds at 1, 2, 3, and 4 seats (counts and strain HP scale as PLAN §6), all complete.\n\n4. Playable presentation, input, and shell. The shipped page boots a Three.js r180 scene with cel-shaded/toon materials, the ink/halftone filter, code-built organs, instruments, and strains, and Rapier-backed pulls, on WebGPU under PLAN §16.3’s five flags and on WebGL2 without them, with zero page errors. The render surface’s drawing size equals the requested size and is substantially filled (not a blank or a few pixels). Pad (standard mapping), keyboard, and mouse drive the same screens: tip, eight-way wheel, analog use, hover-moves-focus, prompts follow the last device (`data-prompt-set`; PlayStation glyphs default, Xbox via Settings). All music, effects, and Animalese/Simlish voices are synthesized (no audio files); music-bus gain defaults to 0.10. Touch-only (`(any-pointer: fine)` false and no pad) shows exactly “Controller or keyboard required” plus a tappable Settings button, no touch controls, and attract mode still runs. Attract mode starts after 10 s of wall-clock idle on Title, Main Menu, Free Play, and the touch notice, loops a cheated play of many ops, and any input returns to the previous screen without saving. Dev mode opens from pause (hidden in hub sessions) with the PLAN §13 tools and live sliders (current value, default tick, per-page and global reset) that change the sim immediately. Progress survives reload outside a session. A scripted UI playthrough reaches Title, Main Menu, Story, Chart, Operation, Report, and Credits; Fail, Free Play, Lobby, Settings, About, and the touch notice are reachable. Missing graphics shows a friendly screen instead of crashing.\n\n5. Multiplayer, hub and couch. With no hub the page starts the standalone game (`AIHub.active` false, `Math.random` untouched). In a session it uses shared-world, seats 1–4, own card: straight into Grand Rounds (no title, menus, attract, story, dev, or saves); pause is Settings only and the sim keeps running; network only through the hub; host alone runs the sim (including a viewless server copy) on a timer not tied to animation frames; clients render snapshots; the local tip is instant; `finish()` is host-only and reports standings, scores, won, stats, and awards. Couch Grand Rounds: up to four seats on one screen; two pads join, move two tips independently, and both score. The real hub harness passes all of: 2 players; 3 players and 4 players at ~150 ms ±50 ms and 5% loss; host-leave; server copy; and the 4-player case again on WebGL2 — each with `__hubCheck.pass === true` and no console line starting with `[AIHub]`. The standalone harness sequence (Enter, Space, Enter, Space) throws nothing and the shot is not blank.\n\n## Verification plan\n1. gating: Add or update in-repo tests that import the shipped sim (no reimplementation, no mocking the sim, no hardcoded full-state oracles) and assert criterion 2 and 3: each tool and strain rule, determinism by comparing two runs, snapshot byte caps, data lint, banned names, bot Normal/Easy/idle invariants, story order, Grand Rounds at 1–4 seats. Run the one shipped command for the sim suite (PLAN §17: `node tests/run-all.mjs sim`). It must print only failures plus a one-line count and exit 0. Save the log to `{SCRATCH}/sim.log`.\n2. gating: Browser suite (`node tests/run-all.mjs browser`) drives the shipped page, including `?test=1` hooks that call into the real sim, and asserts criterion 4: WebGPU boot with the five flags `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`, WebGL2 boot without them, WebKit boot, zero page errors, non-blank shots, pad/mouse/key input (full right stick for 1 s moves the tip by `tipSpeed` ±5%), prompt set and Xbox glyphs, dev-mode slider changes the sim and reset restores defaults, attract at 10 s ±1 s and exit without a save, Pixel 7 and iPhone 15 profiles show the exact touch notice and a working Settings tap, OfflineAudioContext output is non-silent for every track, effect, and voice, music gain 0.10, no audio-file references, save survives reload, About fields and `#about-data` parse, two stub pads score in couch play, UI playthrough `state().reached` contains Title, Main Menu, Story, Chart, Operation, Report, and Credits roll, packaging checks from criterion 1. Exit 0; failures-only output. Save `{SCRATCH}/browser.log`.\n3. gating: Hub suite (`node tests/run-all.mjs hub`) copies the built page into the real harness from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` and runs every row in criterion 5 (1280×860, up to 300 s, `body[data-complete=\"true\"]`, `__hubCheck.pass === true`, no `[AIHub]` console line), plus the on-its-own Enter/Space sequence. Exit 0. Save `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\n4. gating: Launch the real root HTML twice, from `file://` or `http://127.0.0.1` (bind `127.0.0.1` on an OS-picked port; confirm `window.__game` exists before trusting the page). Use the five flags above. Each run: zero page errors; canvas drawing buffer equals the requested size; painted fraction is high (a blank or speck fails — do not treat a successful blank readback as a missing tool); a driven input (stub pad or mouse) changes the on-screen tip or HUD, visible in a second capture. Save `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. Both runs must agree. If Playwright cannot start at all, save that error to `{SCRATCH}/launch.log` and rely on steps 1–3; a started browser that returns an empty buffer is a failure to fix.\n5. evidence: At 1920×1080, capture Title, Main Menu, a story shout, calm operation, danger, Hush, each strain, Report, Settings, About, the session card, one upright HUD portrait, and one upright cut-in under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Use them only to fix ten-foot type, ink, and overlap; they do not gate if steps 1–4 pass.\n6. gating: Parse `exit-interview.json`; confirm the root HTML and dev-folder names; confirm the client hash and that the hub banner string appears once; confirm own source (not the bundle) has no banned source names, no `Math.random` in the sim, and no TODO/FIXME/placeholder/stub. Record results in `{SCRATCH}/package.log`.\n\n## Non-goals\n- FPS targets, frame-time optimization, or lowering scene detail to chase performance.\n- Playable post-credits “Opus 2”, online play outside AI Hub, custom names, chat, or emotes.\n- Touch-screen gameplay controls, a second desktop layout, or Firefox WebGL2 (headless Firefox here has no WebGL2).\n- Hardware rumble feel, a human listener, real iOS Safari, or a physical DualSense (stub the pad; guard a missing vibration actuator).\n- Source-game IP, star-drawing specials, or the source’s grade names.\n\n## Assumed scope\nGreenfield work folder (`PLAN.md`, `STAGE.md`, `TASK.md` only). New dev subfolder plus root HTML, `PROGRESS.md`, and `exit-interview.json`. Dependencies installed inside the dev folder: `three@0.180.0`, `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3`, `esbuild@0.28.2`, `playwright@1.63.0`, `@fontsource/bangers`, `@fontsource/barlow-condensed`. Hub `session.js` and harness files downloaded from `michaelcrosato/ai-hub` `lib/hub`. Browsers already on the machine; do not run `playwright install`. Tests and screenshots stay inside the dev folder; command logs copied to `{SCRATCH}`.\n\n## Implementation approach\nFollow PLAN.md for rules and content; the layout there is guidance so the sim stays testable, not a reason to reject an equivalent structure. Keep the operation sim pure (JSON state, seeded RNG, fixed 1/60, Rapier in field space) so Node tests and the bot drive it with no browser. Let the view, audio, and DOM reconcile that state or a decoded snapshot, never own the rules. Build operations, strains, and scenes as data. Ship one command that runs sim, then browser, then hub, and prints only failures and a count. Inline the browser bundle so `file://` works. Use the five WebGPU flags from PLAN §16.3, not the shorter set in TASK.md. One browser at a time. Stop only processes this work started, by PID.\n\n## Task checklist\n- [ ] Scaffold the dev folder, identity (RNG title), tuning defaults, package, inlined single-file build, vendored hub client, and a test command that prints only failures and a count.\n- [ ] Implement the headless sim: beat, vitals, scoring, tools, gifts, checkpoints, and Op 1; bot clears Op 1; sim tests green.\n- [ ] Render the operative field (toon, ink, Rapier juice, HUD, pad/mouse/keys) so Op 1 is playable through the report on WebGPU and WebGL2.\n- [ ] Add remaining tools, Hush, Sforzando, all seven strains, ops 2–7, Last Patient, and Grand Rounds; bot invariants hold.\n- [ ] Add story scenes, busts, procedural music, effects, and gibberish voices (music gain 0.10).\n- [ ] Add menus, chart, free play, settings, glyphs, touch notice, save, About, credits, attract mode, and dev mode.\n- [ ] Add couch co-op and hub session (card, snapshots, host/server rules) until every hub harness run passes.\n- [ ] Calibrate `parScore`, write the authoring note, fill About and `exit-interview.json`, copy the HTML to the root name, and save sim, browser, hub, and two launch captures under `{SCRATCH}`.\n\n## Risks / Contradictions\n- TASK.md’s shorter WebGPU flags drop the device after the first frame on this machine; PLAN §16.3’s five flags are the ones that draw. A blank readback with those five flags is an app bug, not an excuse to skip the launch check.\n- Software WebGPU is ~260–410 ms/frame and earlyoom kills extra browsers. Hub runs may need the full 300 s budget. Run one browser. If the harness process itself cannot start, capture that log; do not substitute a mocked hub result.\n- The required HTML and folder names include spaces and a colon (`Trauma Center: Second Opinion`). Keep that literal pattern; Linux allows it.\n- The released hub-client hash may no longer be the 1.4.0 value in PLAN §16.2 if upstream moved. Hash the vendored file against `RELEASES` in `scripts/lib/hub-client.mjs` and require a match; do not edit the client.\n- Pad-only audio unlock and real rumble cannot be proven here. Cover synthesis with `OfflineAudioContext` and a missing-actuator guard; say so in Testing Feedback.\n- Headless Firefox has no WebGL2 here. Do not block on it. WebKit WebGL2 and Chromium WebGPU plus WebGL2 are the bar.\n"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7626,"output_tokens":13035,"cache_read_input_tokens":42496,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"cbd258d8-2df2-4f1f-8cd0-37da9460734f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-1c60f8eb-755f-412f-83ff-75bc16ed519d-6","content":"{\"type\":\"SearchReplace\",\"EditsApplied\":{\"old_string\":\"\",\"new_string\":\"# Plan: Build the surgical-action demo PLAN.md specifies, and hand in every artifact TASK.md and STAGE.md require\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. Hand-in package. The work folder contains `PROGRESS.md`, a parsable `exit-interview.json` beside the game HTML (one object, every TASK.md key filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, `aiHub.type` Game, `aiHub.genre` Simulation, `aiHub.source` `Trauma Center: Second Opinion`, `aiHub.series` `Trauma Center`, `aiHub.players` `1–4`, controls and tags as PLAN §19), a dev subfolder named `{Title}-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230`, and one root file `{Title}-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230.html`. `{Title}` is an original playful riff on the source (50 titles brainstormed, then one picked with a real RNG), shown on the title screen, and stored so no other source file contains that string. The HTML is one self-contained file that runs from `file://` and from `http://127.0.0.1` (scripts and fonts inlined; no external script or font URLs). It includes exactly one unmodified AI Hub session client (SHA-256 equals the released hash for the vendored version), the §14.6 hub meta tags in that exact `name`-then-`content` order and wording (no `hub:webgpu=required`), catalogue tags (`title` split on the dash, description, `theme-color` `#17707A`, inline SVG icon, `hub:type` Game, `hub:players` `1–4`, `hub:source` `Trauma Center: Second Opinion`), and an About/Credits screen whose `#about-data` JSON shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback with PLAN §19’s fixed values. The same four blocks are in the finishing chat reply. Own source has no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, or `stub`. A short authoring note explains how to add an operation, a strain, a story scene, and a tuning value.\\n\\n2. Surgical simulation. A headless sim (no renderer, DOM, or audio) steps at fixed 1/60 from seeded RNG only (`Math.random` absent from sim code). It implements the beat clock (`bpm` rises as vitals fall; beat drives tissue, ECG, strains, music tempo); vitals, timer, and miss failures; grades FLAWLESS/CLEAN/ROUGH/MISS, combo, and ranks S/A/B/C/D titled Chief of Surgery / Attending / Resident / Intern / Malpractice Suit Pending (never XS, Bad, Good, or Cool); all eight wheel tools plus syringe and defibrillator with the PLAN §4 rules (including analog use, nicks, tears, clamp-on-beat, defib gold band, clamps off before the end); Hush and Sforzando; Intern/Resident/Attending multipliers; phase checkpoints (retry restores vitals ≥60, timer ≥90s, checkpoint score) and optional Bea’s Notes after two failures of the same phase; consults (careful vs aggressive). Same seed and inputs yield the same state hash twice. At the busiest 4-seat Grand Rounds moment the unreliable snapshot is ≤1100 bytes and the keyframe ≤16 KB. Data lint: every touchable spawn lies inside the drape ellipse, `teaches` covers the eight wheel tools, syringe, defib, both gifts, and all seven strains, and no Bea line contains “good” except the final scene.\\n\\n3. Demo content a bot can finish. Playable vertical slice: operations Broken Glass, Growth Spurt, Adagio, Staccato, Legato, Fermata, Coda, Last Patient, and Grand Rounds, with strains Adagio, Staccato, Tremolo, Legato, Crescendo, Fermata, and Coda behaving as in PLAN §5, original cast and setting (Port Calloway, St. Alder, Ward Zero, Ren, Bea, Bell, Pip, Sorrento, Ines, Voss/REQUIEM/the Hush — never Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Caduceus, Delphi, GUILT or its strain names, Healing Touch, star drawing, or Hope Hospital). Story scenes S0–S16, 250–350 lines, must-have lines present, scenes skippable. Phases are data and `skipTo` works. On Normal, a bot that reads only sim state clears each operation (Last Patient only has to complete: no timer, no rank, cannot fail) in 45–75% of that op’s timer, minimum vitals ≥35, rank ≥A; `parScore` is taken from that bot. A sloppy bot (noise and reaction delay) on Easy clears at rank ≥C. No input fails every op except Last Patient. Story order through Last Patient, and Grand Rounds at 1, 2, 3, and 4 seats (counts and strain HP scale as PLAN §6), all complete.\\n\\n4. Playable presentation, input, and shell. The shipped page boots a Three.js r180 scene with cel-shaded/toon materials, the ink/halftone filter, code-built organs, instruments, and strains, and Rapier-backed pulls, on WebGPU under PLAN §16.3’s five flags and on WebGL2 without them, with zero page errors. The render surface’s drawing size equals the requested size and is substantially filled (not a blank or a few pixels). Pad (standard mapping), keyboard, and mouse drive the same screens: tip, eight-way wheel, analog use, hover-moves-focus, prompts follow the last device (`data-prompt-set`; PlayStation glyphs default, Xbox via Settings). All music, effects, and Animalese/Simlish voices are synthesized (no audio files); music-bus gain defaults to 0.10. Touch-only (`(any-pointer: fine)` false and no pad) shows exactly “Controller or keyboard required” plus a tappable Settings button, no touch controls, and attract mode still runs. Attract mode starts after 10 s of wall-clock idle on Title, Main Menu, Free Play, and the touch notice, loops a cheated play of many ops, and any input returns to the previous screen without saving. Dev mode opens from pause (hidden in hub sessions) with the PLAN §13 tools and live sliders (current value, default tick, per-page and global reset) that change the sim immediately. Progress survives reload outside a session. A scripted UI playthrough reaches Title, Main Menu, Story, Chart, Operation, Report, and Credits; Fail, Free Play, Lobby, Settings, About, and the touch notice are reachable. Missing graphics shows a friendly screen instead of crashing.\\n\\n5. Multiplayer, hub and couch. With no hub the page starts the standalone game (`AIHub.active` false, `Math.random` untouched). In a session it uses shared-world, seats 1–4, own card: straight into Grand Rounds (no title, menus, attract, story, dev, or saves); pause is Settings only and the sim keeps running; network only through the hub; host alone runs the sim (including a viewless server copy) on a timer not tied to animation frames; clients render snapshots; the local tip is instant; `finish()` is host-only and reports standings, scores, won, stats, and awards. Couch Grand Rounds: up to four seats on one screen; two pads join, move two tips independently, and both score. The real hub harness passes all of: 2 players; 3 players and 4 players at ~150 ms ±50 ms and 5% loss; host-leave; server copy; and the 4-player case again on WebGL2 — each with `__hubCheck.pass === true` and no console line starting with `[AIHub]`. The standalone harness sequence (Enter, Space, Enter, Space) throws nothing and the shot is not blank.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: Add or update in-repo tests that import the shipped sim (no reimplementation, no mocking the sim, no hardcoded full-state oracles) and assert criterion 2 and 3: each tool and strain rule, determinism by comparing two runs, snapshot byte caps, data lint, banned names, bot Normal/Easy/idle invariants, story order, Grand Rounds at 1–4 seats. Run the one shipped command for the sim suite (PLAN §17: `node tests/run-all.mjs sim`). It must print only failures plus a one-line count and exit 0. Save the log to `{SCRATCH}/sim.log`.\\n2. gating: Browser suite (`node tests/run-all.mjs browser`) drives the shipped page, including `?test=1` hooks that call into the real sim, and asserts criterion 4: WebGPU boot with the five flags `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`, WebGL2 boot without them, WebKit boot, zero page errors, non-blank shots, pad/mouse/key input (full right stick for 1 s moves the tip by `tipSpeed` ±5%), prompt set and Xbox glyphs, dev-mode slider changes the sim and reset restores defaults, attract at 10 s ±1 s and exit without a save, Pixel 7 and iPhone 15 profiles show the exact touch notice and a working Settings tap, OfflineAudioContext output is non-silent for every track, effect, and voice, music gain 0.10, no audio-file references, save survives reload, About fields and `#about-data` parse, two stub pads score in couch play, UI playthrough `state().reached` contains Title, Main Menu, Story, Chart, Operation, Report, and Credits roll, packaging checks from criterion 1. Exit 0; failures-only output. Save `{SCRATCH}/browser.log`.\\n3. gating: Hub suite (`node tests/run-all.mjs hub`) copies the built page into the real harness from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` and runs every row in criterion 5 (1280×860, up to 300 s, `body[data-complete=\\\"true\\\"]`, `__hubCheck.pass === true`, no `[AIHub]` console line), plus the on-its-own Enter/Space sequence. Exit 0. Save `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\\n4. gating: Launch the real root HTML twice, from `file://` or `http://127.0.0.1` (bind `127.0.0.1` on an OS-picked port; confirm `window.__game` exists before trusting the page). Use the five flags above. Each run: zero page errors; canvas drawing buffer equals the requested size; painted fraction is high (a blank or speck fails — do not treat a successful blank readback as a missing tool); a driven input (stub pad or mouse) changes the on-screen tip or HUD, visible in a second capture. Save `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. Both runs must agree. If Playwright cannot start at all, save that error to `{SCRATCH}/launch.log` and rely on steps 1–3; a started browser that returns an empty buffer is a failure to fix.\\n5. evidence: At 1920×1080, capture Title, Main Menu, a story shout, calm operation, danger, Hush, each strain, Report, Settings, About, the session card, one upright HUD portrait, and one upright cut-in under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Use them only to fix ten-foot type, ink, and overlap; they do not gate if steps 1–4 pass.\\n6. gating: Parse `exit-interview.json`; confirm the root HTML and dev-folder names; confirm the client hash and that the hub banner string appears once; confirm own source (not the bundle) has no banned source names, no `Math.random` in the sim, and no TODO/FIXME/placeholder/stub. Record results in `{SCRATCH}/package.log`.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- FPS targets, frame-time optimization, or lowering scene detail to chase performance.\\n- Playable post-credits “Opus 2”, online play outside AI Hub, custom names, chat, or emotes.\\n- Touch-screen gameplay controls, a second desktop layout, or Firefox WebGL2 (headless Firefox here has no WebGL2).\\n- Hardware rumble feel, a human listener, real iOS Safari, or a physical DualSense (stub the pad; guard a missing vibration actuator).\\n- Source-game IP, star-drawing specials, or the source’s grade names.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\nGreenfield work folder (`PLAN.md`, `STAGE.md`, `TASK.md` only). New dev subfolder plus root HTML, `PROGRESS.md`, and `exit-interview.json`. Dependencies installed inside the dev folder: `three@0.180.0`, `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3`, `esbuild@0.28.2`, `playwright@1.63.0`, `@fontsource/bangers`, `@fontsource/barlow-condensed`. Hub `session.js` and harness files downloaded from `michaelcrosato/ai-hub` `lib/hub`. Browsers already on the machine; do not run `playwright install`. Tests and screenshots stay inside the dev folder; command logs copied to `{SCRATCH}`.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md for rules and content; the layout there is guidance so the sim stays testable, not a reason to reject an equivalent structure. Keep the operation sim pure (JSON state, seeded RNG, fixed 1/60, Rapier in field space) so Node tests and the bot drive it with no browser. Let the view, audio, and DOM reconcile that state or a decoded snapshot, never own the rules. Build operations, strains, and scenes as data. Ship one command that runs sim, then browser, then hub, and prints only failures and a count. Inline the browser bundle so `file://` works. Use the five WebGPU flags from PLAN §16.3, not the shorter set in TASK.md. One browser at a time. Stop only processes this work started, by PID.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Scaffold the dev folder, identity (RNG title), tuning defaults, package, inlined single-file build, vendored hub client, and a test command that prints only failures and a count.\\n- [ ] Implement the headless sim: beat, vitals, scoring, tools, gifts, checkpoints, and Op 1; bot clears Op 1; sim tests green.\\n- [ ] Render the operative field (toon, ink, Rapier juice, HUD, pad/mouse/keys) so Op 1 is playable through the report on WebGPU and WebGL2.\\n- [ ] Add remaining tools, Hush, Sforzando, all seven strains, ops 2–7, Last Patient, and Grand Rounds; bot invariants hold.\\n- [ ] Add story scenes, busts, procedural music, effects, and gibberish voices (music gain 0.10).\\n- [ ] Add menus, chart, free play, settings, glyphs, touch notice, save, About, credits, attract mode, and dev mode.\\n- [ ] Add couch co-op and hub session (card, snapshots, host/server rules) until every hub harness run passes.\\n- [ ] Calibrate `parScore`, write the authoring note, fill About and `exit-interview.json`, copy the HTML to the root name, and save sim, browser, hub, and two launch captures under `{SCRATCH}`.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- TASK.md’s shorter WebGPU flags drop the device after the first frame on this machine; PLAN §16.3’s five flags are the ones that draw. A blank readback with those five flags is an app bug, not an excuse to skip the launch check.\\n- Software WebGPU is ~260–410 ms/frame and earlyoom kills extra browsers. Hub runs may need the full 300 s budget. Run one browser. If the harness process itself cannot start, capture that log; do not substitute a mocked hub result.\\n- The required HTML and folder names include spaces and a colon (`Trauma Center: Second Opinion`). Keep that literal pattern; Linux allows it.\\n- The released hub-client hash may no longer be the 1.4.0 value in PLAN §16.2 if upstream moved. Hash the vendored file against `RELEASES` in `scripts/lib/hub-client.mjs` and require a match; do not edit the client.\\n- Pad-only audio unlock and real rumble cannot be proven here. Cover synthesis with `OfflineAudioContext` and a missing-actuator guard; say so in Testing Feedback.\\n- Headless Firefox has no WebGL2 here. Do not block on it. WebKit WebGL2 and Chromium WebGPU plus WebGL2 are the bar.\\n\",\"tool_output_for_prompt\":\"The file /home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6%2Fwork/01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710/goal/plan-01a1aa56-6405-7d60-8230-1a9d7b05a19c.md has been created.\",\"tool_output_for_prompt_concise\":\"The file /home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6%2Fwork/01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710/goal/plan-01a1aa56-6405-7d60-8230-1a9d7b05a19c.md has been created.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6%2Fwork/01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710/goal/plan-01a1aa56-6405-7d60-8230-1a9d7b05a19c.md\",\"edits\":{\"details\":[{\"old_string\":\"\",\"old_line\":1,\"new_string\":\"# Plan: Build the surgical-action demo PLAN.md specifies, and hand in every artifact TASK.md and STAGE.md require\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. Hand-in package. The work folder contains `PROGRESS.md`, a parsable `exit-interview.json` beside the game HTML (one object, every TASK.md key filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, `aiHub.type` Game, `aiHub.genre` Simulation, `aiHub.source` `Trauma Center: Second Opinion`, `aiHub.series` `Trauma Center`, `aiHub.players` `1–4`, controls and tags as PLAN §19), a dev subfolder named `{Title}-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230`, and one root file `{Title}-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230.html`. `{Title}` is an original playful riff on the source (50 titles brainstormed, then one picked with a real RNG), shown on the title screen, and stored so no other source file contains that string. The HTML is one self-contained file that runs from `file://` and from `http://127.0.0.1` (scripts and fonts inlined; no external script or font URLs). It includes exactly one unmodified AI Hub session client (SHA-256 equals the released hash for the vendored version), the §14.6 hub meta tags in that exact `name`-then-`content` order and wording (no `hub:webgpu=required`), catalogue tags (`title` split on the dash, description, `theme-color` `#17707A`, inline SVG icon, `hub:type` Game, `hub:players` `1–4`, `hub:source` `Trauma Center: Second Opinion`), and an About/Credits screen whose `#about-data` JSON shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback with PLAN §19’s fixed values. The same four blocks are in the finishing chat reply. Own source has no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, or `stub`. A short authoring note explains how to add an operation, a strain, a story scene, and a tuning value.\\n\\n2. Surgical simulation. A headless sim (no renderer, DOM, or audio) steps at fixed 1/60 from seeded RNG only (`Math.random` absent from sim code). It implements the beat clock (`bpm` rises as vitals fall; beat drives tissue, ECG, strains, music tempo); vitals, timer, and miss failures; grades FLAWLESS/CLEAN/ROUGH/MISS, combo, and ranks S/A/B/C/D titled Chief of Surgery / Attending / Resident / Intern / Malpractice Suit Pending (never XS, Bad, Good, or Cool); all eight wheel tools plus syringe and defibrillator with the PLAN §4 rules (including analog use, nicks, tears, clamp-on-beat, defib gold band, clamps off before the end); Hush and Sforzando; Intern/Resident/Attending multipliers; phase checkpoints (retry restores vitals ≥60, timer ≥90s, checkpoint score) and optional Bea’s Notes after two failures of the same phase; consults (careful vs aggressive). Same seed and inputs yield the same state hash twice. At the busiest 4-seat Grand Rounds moment the unreliable snapshot is ≤1100 bytes and the keyframe ≤16 KB. Data lint: every touchable spawn lies inside the drape ellipse, `teaches` covers the eight wheel tools, syringe, defib, both gifts, and all seven strains, and no Bea line contains “good” except the final scene.\\n\\n3. Demo content a bot can finish. Playable vertical slice: operations Broken Glass, Growth Spurt, Adagio, Staccato, Legato, Fermata, Coda, Last Patient, and Grand Rounds, with strains Adagio, Staccato, Tremolo, Legato, Crescendo, Fermata, and Coda behaving as in PLAN §5, original cast and setting (Port Calloway, St. Alder, Ward Zero, Ren, Bea, Bell, Pip, Sorrento, Ines, Voss/REQUIEM/the Hush — never Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Caduceus, Delphi, GUILT or its strain names, Healing Touch, star drawing, or Hope Hospital). Story scenes S0–S16, 250–350 lines, must-have lines present, scenes skippable. Phases are data and `skipTo` works. On Normal, a bot that reads only sim state clears each operation (Last Patient only has to complete: no timer, no rank, cannot fail) in 45–75% of that op’s timer, minimum vitals ≥35, rank ≥A; `parScore` is taken from that bot. A sloppy bot (noise and reaction delay) on Easy clears at rank ≥C. No input fails every op except Last Patient. Story order through Last Patient, and Grand Rounds at 1, 2, 3, and 4 seats (counts and strain HP scale as PLAN §6), all complete.\\n\\n4. Playable presentation, input, and shell. The shipped page boots a Three.js r180 scene with cel-shaded/toon materials, the ink/halftone filter, code-built organs, instruments, and strains, and Rapier-backed pulls, on WebGPU under PLAN §16.3’s five flags and on WebGL2 without them, with zero page errors. The render surface’s drawing size equals the requested size and is substantially filled (not a blank or a few pixels). Pad (standard mapping), keyboard, and mouse drive the same screens: tip, eight-way wheel, analog use, hover-moves-focus, prompts follow the last device (`data-prompt-set`; PlayStation glyphs default, Xbox via Settings). All music, effects, and Animalese/Simlish voices are synthesized (no audio files); music-bus gain defaults to 0.10. Touch-only (`(any-pointer: fine)` false and no pad) shows exactly “Controller or keyboard required” plus a tappable Settings button, no touch controls, and attract mode still runs. Attract mode starts after 10 s of wall-clock idle on Title, Main Menu, Free Play, and the touch notice, loops a cheated play of many ops, and any input returns to the previous screen without saving. Dev mode opens from pause (hidden in hub sessions) with the PLAN §13 tools and live sliders (current value, default tick, per-page and global reset) that change the sim immediately. Progress survives reload outside a session. A scripted UI playthrough reaches Title, Main Menu, Story, Chart, Operation, Report, and Credits; Fail, Free Play, Lobby, Settings, About, and the touch notice are reachable. Missing graphics shows a friendly screen instead of crashing.\\n\\n5. Multiplayer, hub and couch. With no hub the page starts the standalone game (`AIHub.active` false, `Math.random` untouched). In a session it uses shared-world, seats 1–4, own card: straight into Grand Rounds (no title, menus, attract, story, dev, or saves); pause is Settings only and the sim keeps running; network only through the hub; host alone runs the sim (including a viewless server copy) on a timer not tied to animation frames; clients render snapshots; the local tip is instant; `finish()` is host-only and reports standings, scores, won, stats, and awards. Couch Grand Rounds: up to four seats on one screen; two pads join, move two tips independently, and both score. The real hub harness passes all of: 2 players; 3 players and 4 players at ~150 ms ±50 ms and 5% loss; host-leave; server copy; and the 4-player case again on WebGL2 — each with `__hubCheck.pass === true` and no console line starting with `[AIHub]`. The standalone harness sequence (Enter, Space, Enter, Space) throws nothing and the shot is not blank.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: Add or update in-repo tests that import the shipped sim (no reimplementation, no mocking the sim, no hardcoded full-state oracles) and assert criterion 2 and 3: each tool and strain rule, determinism by comparing two runs, snapshot byte caps, data lint, banned names, bot Normal/Easy/idle invariants, story order, Grand Rounds at 1–4 seats. Run the one shipped command for the sim suite (PLAN §17: `node tests/run-all.mjs sim`). It must print only failures plus a one-line count and exit 0. Save the log to `{SCRATCH}/sim.log`.\\n2. gating: Browser suite (`node tests/run-all.mjs browser`) drives the shipped page, including `?test=1` hooks that call into the real sim, and asserts criterion 4: WebGPU boot with the five flags `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`, WebGL2 boot without them, WebKit boot, zero page errors, non-blank shots, pad/mouse/key input (full right stick for 1 s moves the tip by `tipSpeed` ±5%), prompt set and Xbox glyphs, dev-mode slider changes the sim and reset restores defaults, attract at 10 s ±1 s and exit without a save, Pixel 7 and iPhone 15 profiles show the exact touch notice and a working Settings tap, OfflineAudioContext output is non-silent for every track, effect, and voice, music gain 0.10, no audio-file references, save survives reload, About fields and `#about-data` parse, two stub pads score in couch play, UI playthrough `state().reached` contains Title, Main Menu, Story, Chart, Operation, Report, and Credits roll, packaging checks from criterion 1. Exit 0; failures-only output. Save `{SCRATCH}/browser.log`.\\n3. gating: Hub suite (`node tests/run-all.mjs hub`) copies the built page into the real harness from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` and runs every row in criterion 5 (1280×860, up to 300 s, `body[data-complete=\\\"true\\\"]`, `__hubCheck.pass === true`, no `[AIHub]` console line), plus the on-its-own Enter/Space sequence. Exit 0. Save `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\\n4. gating: Launch the real root HTML twice, from `file://` or `http://127.0.0.1` (bind `127.0.0.1` on an OS-picked port; confirm `window.__game` exists before trusting the page). Use the five flags above. Each run: zero page errors; canvas drawing buffer equals the requested size; painted fraction is high (a blank or speck fails — do not treat a successful blank readback as a missing tool); a driven input (stub pad or mouse) changes the on-screen tip or HUD, visible in a second capture. Save `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. Both runs must agree. If Playwright cannot start at all, save that error to `{SCRATCH}/launch.log` and rely on steps 1–3; a started browser that returns an empty buffer is a failure to fix.\\n5. evidence: At 1920×1080, capture Title, Main Menu, a story shout, calm operation, danger, Hush, each strain, Report, Settings, About, the session card, one upright HUD portrait, and one upright cut-in under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Use them only to fix ten-foot type, ink, and overlap; they do not gate if steps 1–4 pass.\\n6. gating: Parse `exit-interview.json`; confirm the root HTML and dev-folder names; confirm the client hash and that the hub banner string appears once; confirm own source (not the bundle) has no banned source names, no `Math.random` in the sim, and no TODO/FIXME/placeholder/stub. Record results in `{SCRATCH}/package.log`.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- FPS targets, frame-time optimization, or lowering scene detail to chase performance.\\n- Playable post-credits “Opus 2”, online play outside AI Hub, custom names, chat, or emotes.\\n- Touch-screen gameplay controls, a second desktop layout, or Firefox WebGL2 (headless Firefox here has no WebGL2).\\n- Hardware rumble feel, a human listener, real iOS Safari, or a physical DualSense (stub the pad; guard a missing vibration actuator).\\n- Source-game IP, star-drawing specials, or the source’s grade names.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\nGreenfield work folder (`PLAN.md`, `STAGE.md`, `TASK.md` only). New dev subfolder plus root HTML, `PROGRESS.md`, and `exit-interview.json`. Dependencies installed inside the dev folder: `three@0.180.0`, `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3`, `esbuild@0.28.2`, `playwright@1.63.0`, `@fontsource/bangers`, `@fontsource/barlow-condensed`. Hub `session.js` and harness files downloaded from `michaelcrosato/ai-hub` `lib/hub`. Browsers already on the machine; do not run `playwright install`. Tests and screenshots stay inside the dev folder; command logs copied to `{SCRATCH}`.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md for rules and content; the layout there is guidance so the sim stays testable, not a reason to reject an equivalent structure. Keep the operation sim pure (JSON state, seeded RNG, fixed 1/60, Rapier in field space) so Node tests and the bot drive it with no browser. Let the view, audio, and DOM reconcile that state or a decoded snapshot, never own the rules. Build operations, strains, and scenes as data. Ship one command that runs sim, then browser, then hub, and prints only failures and a count. Inline the browser bundle so `file://` works. Use the five WebGPU flags from PLAN §16.3, not the shorter set in TASK.md. One browser at a time. Stop only processes this work started, by PID.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Scaffold the dev folder, identity (RNG title), tuning defaults, package,