{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_5","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Reading STAGE.md to begin the current task.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"I'll start by reading the plan and STAGE.md so I know exactly what has to be finished."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-7d748341-699e-4b1a-ba16-8c17cc8ce3ed-0","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6%2Fwork/01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710/goal/plan.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-7d748341-699e-4b1a-ba16-8c17cc8ce3ed-1","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/STAGE.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-7d748341-699e-4b1a-ba16-8c17cc8ce3ed-2","name":"list_dir","input":{"target_directory":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":11269,"output_tokens":268,"cache_read_input_tokens":10112,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"12ab2f67-1ce2-4bf0-94a9-db7b356f6fcc"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-7d748341-699e-4b1a-ba16-8c17cc8ce3ed-0","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→# Plan: Build the surgical-action demo PLAN.md specifies, and hand in every artifact TASK.md and STAGE.md require\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. Hand-in package. The work folder contains `PROGRESS.md`, a parsable `exit-interview.json` beside the game HTML (one object, every TASK.md key filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, `aiHub.type` Game, `aiHub.genre` Simulation, `aiHub.source` `Trauma Center: Second Opinion`, `aiHub.series` `Trauma Center`, `aiHub.players` `1–4`, controls and tags as PLAN §19), a dev subfolder named `{Title}-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230`, and one root file `{Title}-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230.html`. `{Title}` is an original playful riff on the source (50 titles brainstormed, then one picked with a real RNG), shown on the title screen, and stored so no other source file contains that string. The HTML is one self-contained file that runs from `file://` and from `http://127.0.0.1` (scripts and fonts inlined; no external script or font URLs). It includes exactly one unmodified AI Hub session client (SHA-256 equals the released hash for the vendored version), the §14.6 hub meta tags in that exact `name`-then-`content` order and wording (no `hub:webgpu=required`), catalogue tags (`title` split on the dash, description, `theme-color` `#17707A`, inline SVG icon, `hub:type` Game, `hub:players` `1–4`, `hub:source` `Trauma Center: Second Opinion`), and an About/Credits screen whose `#about-data` JSON shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback with PLAN §19’s fixed values. The same four blocks are in the finishing chat reply. Own source has no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, or `stub`. A short authoring note explains how to add an operation, a strain, a story scene, and a tuning value.\\n\\n2. Surgical simulation. A headless sim (no renderer, DOM, or audio) steps at fixed 1/60 from seeded RNG only (`Math.random` absent from sim code). It implements the beat clock (`bpm` rises as vitals fall; beat drives tissue, ECG, strains, music tempo); vitals, timer, and miss failures; grades FLAWLESS/CLEAN/ROUGH/MISS, combo, and ranks S/A/B/C/D titled Chief of Surgery / Attending / Resident / Intern / Malpractice Suit Pending (never XS, Bad, Good, or Cool); all eight wheel tools plus syringe and defibrillator with the PLAN §4 rules (including analog use, nicks, tears, clamp-on-beat, defib gold band, clamps off before the end); Hush and Sforzando; Intern/Resident/Attending multipliers; phase checkpoints (retry restores vitals ≥60, timer ≥90s, checkpoint score) and optional Bea’s Notes after two failures of the same phase; consults (careful vs aggressive). Same seed and inputs yield the same state hash twice. At the busiest 4-seat Grand Rounds moment the unreliable snapshot is ≤1100 bytes and the keyframe ≤16 KB. Data lint: every touchable spawn lies inside the drape ellipse, `teaches` covers the eight wheel tools, syringe, defib, both gifts, and all seven strains, and no Bea line contains “good” except the final scene.\\n10→\\n3. Demo content a bot can finish. Playable vertical slice: operations Broken Glass, Growth Spurt, Adagio, Staccato, Legato, Fermata, Coda, Last Patient, and Grand Rounds, with strains Adagio, Staccato, Tremolo, Legato, Crescendo, Fermata, and Coda behaving as in PLAN §5, original cast and setting (Port Calloway, St. Alder, Ward Zero, Ren, Bea, Bell, Pip, Sorrento, Ines, Voss/REQUIEM/the Hush — never Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Caduceus, Delphi, GUILT or its strain names, Healing Touch, star drawing, or Hope Hospital). Story scenes S0–S16, 250–350 lines, must-have lines present, scenes skippable. Phases are data and `skipTo` works. On Normal, a bot that reads only sim state clears each operation (Last Patient only has to complete: no timer, no rank, cannot fail) in 45–75% of that op’s timer, minimum vitals ≥35, rank ≥A; `parScore` is taken from that bot. A sloppy bot (noise and reaction delay) on Easy clears at rank ≥C. No input fails every op except Last Patient. Story order through Last Patient, and Grand Rounds at 1, 2, 3, and 4 seats (counts and strain HP scale as PLAN §6), all complete.\\n\\n4. Playable presentation, input, and shell. The shipped page boots a Three.js r180 scene with cel-shaded/toon materials, the ink/halftone filter, code-built organs, instruments, and strains, and Rapier-backed pulls, on WebGPU under PLAN §16.3’s five flags and on WebGL2 without them, with zero page errors. The render surface’s drawing size equals the requested size and is substantially filled (not a blank or a few pixels). Pad (standard mapping), keyboard, and mouse drive the same screens: tip, eight-way wheel, analog use, hover-moves-focus, prompts follow the last device (`data-prompt-set`; PlayStation glyphs default, Xbox via Settings). All music, effects, and Animalese/Simlish voices are synthesized (no audio files); music-bus gain defaults to 0.10. Touch-only (`(any-pointer: fine)` false and no pad) shows exactly “Controller or keyboard required” plus a tappable Settings button, no touch controls, and attract mode still runs. Attract mode starts after 10 s of wall-clock idle on Title, Main Menu, Free Play, and the touch notice, loops a cheated play of many ops, and any input returns to the previous screen without saving. Dev mode opens from pause (hidden in hub sessions) with the PLAN §13 tools and live sliders (current value, default tick, per-page and global reset) that change the sim immediately. Progress survives reload outside a session. A scripted UI playthrough reaches Title, Main Menu, Story, Chart, Operation, Report, and Credits; Fail, Free Play, Lobby, Settings, About, and the touch notice are reachable. Missing graphics shows a friendly screen instead of crashing.\\n\\n5. Multiplayer, hub and couch. With no hub the page starts the standalone game (`AIHub.active` false, `Math.random` untouched). In a session it uses shared-world, seats 1–4, own card: straight into Grand Rounds (no title, menus, attract, story, dev, or saves); pause is Settings only and the sim keeps running; network only through the hub; host alone runs the sim (including a viewless server copy) on a timer not tied to animation frames; clients render snapshots; the local tip is instant; `finish()` is host-only and reports standings, scores, won, stats, and awards. Couch Grand Rounds: up to four seats on one screen; two pads join, move two tips independently, and both score. The real hub harness passes all of: 2 players; 3 players and 4 players at ~150 ms ±50 ms and 5% loss; host-leave; server copy; and the 4-player case again on WebGL2 — each with `__hubCheck.pass === true` and no console line starting with `[AIHub]`. The standalone harness sequence (Enter, Space, Enter, Space) throws nothing and the shot is not blank.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: Add or update in-repo tests that import the shipped sim (no reimplementation, no mocking the sim, no hardcoded full-state oracles) and assert criterion 2 and 3: each tool and strain rule, determinism by comparing two runs, snapshot byte caps, data lint, banned names, bot Normal/Easy/idle invariants, story order, Grand Rounds at 1–4 seats. Run the one shipped command for the sim suite (PLAN §17: `node tests/run-all.mjs sim`). It must print only failures plus a one-line count and exit 0. Save the log to `{SCRATCH}/sim.log`.\\n2. gating: Browser suite (`node tests/run-all.mjs browser`) drives the shipped page, including `?test=1` hooks that call into the real sim, and asserts criterion 4: WebGPU boot with the five flags `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`, WebGL2 boot without them, WebKit boot, zero page errors, non-blank shots, pad/mouse/key input (full right stick for 1 s moves the tip by `tipSpeed` ±5%), prompt set and Xbox glyphs, dev-mode slider changes the sim and reset restores defaults, attract at 10 s ±1 s and exit without a save, Pixel 7 and iPhone 15 profiles show the exact touch notice and a working Settings tap, OfflineAudioContext output is non-silent for every track, effect, and voice, music gain 0.10, no audio-file references, save survives reload, About fields and `#about-data` parse, two stub pads score in couch play, UI playthrough `state().reached` contains Title, Main Menu, Story, Chart, Operation, Report, and Credits roll, packaging checks from criterion 1. Exit 0; failures-only output. Save `{SCRATCH}/browser.log`.\\n20→3. gating: Hub suite (`node tests/run-all.mjs hub`) copies the built page into the real harness from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` and runs every row in criterion 5 (1280×860, up to 300 s, `body[data-complete=\\\"true\\\"]`, `__hubCheck.pass === true`, no `[AIHub]` console line), plus the on-its-own Enter/Space sequence. Exit 0. Save `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\\n4. gating: Launch the real root HTML twice, from `file://` or `http://127.0.0.1` (bind `127.0.0.1` on an OS-picked port; confirm `window.__game` exists before trusting the page). Use the five flags above. Each run: zero page errors; canvas drawing buffer equals the requested size; painted fraction is high (a blank or speck fails — do not treat a successful blank readback as a missing tool); a driven input (stub pad or mouse) changes the on-screen tip or HUD, visible in a second capture. Save `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. Both runs must agree. If Playwright cannot start at all, save that error to `{SCRATCH}/launch.log` and rely on steps 1–3; a started browser that returns an empty buffer is a failure to fix.\\n5. evidence: At 1920×1080, capture Title, Main Menu, a story shout, calm operation, danger, Hush, each strain, Report, Settings, About, the session card, one upright HUD portrait, and one upright cut-in under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Use them only to fix ten-foot type, ink, and overlap; they do not gate if steps 1–4 pass.\\n6. gating: Parse `exit-interview.json`; confirm the root HTML and dev-folder names; confirm the client hash and that the hub banner string appears once; confirm own source (not the bundle) has no banned source names, no `Math.random` in the sim, and no TODO/FIXME/placeholder/stub. Record results in `{SCRATCH}/package.log`.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- FPS targets, frame-time optimization, or lowering scene detail to chase performance.\\n- Playable post-credits “Opus 2”, online play outside AI Hub, custom names, chat, or emotes.\\n- Touch-screen gameplay controls, a second desktop layout, or Firefox WebGL2 (headless Firefox here has no WebGL2).\\n- Hardware rumble feel, a human listener, real iOS Safari, or a physical DualSense (stub the pad; guard a missing vibration actuator).\\n30→- Source-game IP, star-drawing specials, or the source’s grade names.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\nGreenfield work folder (`PLAN.md`, `STAGE.md`, `TASK.md` only). New dev subfolder plus root HTML, `PROGRESS.md`, and `exit-interview.json`. Dependencies installed inside the dev folder: `three@0.180.0`, `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3`, `esbuild@0.28.2`, `playwright@1.63.0`, `@fontsource/bangers`, `@fontsource/barlow-condensed`. Hub `session.js` and harness files downloaded from `michaelcrosato/ai-hub` `lib/hub`. Browsers already on the machine; do not run `playwright install`. Tests and screenshots stay inside the dev folder; command logs copied to `{SCRATCH}`.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md for rules and content; the layout there is guidance so the sim stays testable, not a reason to reject an equivalent structure. Keep the operation sim pure (JSON state, seeded RNG, fixed 1/60, Rapier in field space) so Node tests and the bot drive it with no browser. Let the view, audio, and DOM reconcile that state or a decoded snapshot, never own the rules. Build operations, strains, and scenes as data. Ship one command that runs sim, then browser, then hub, and prints only failures and a count. Inline the browser bundle so `file://` works. Use the five WebGPU flags from PLAN §16.3, not the shorter set in TASK.md. One browser at a time. Stop only processes this work started, by PID.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Scaffold the dev folder, identity (RNG title), tuning defaults, package, inlined single-file build, vendored hub client, and a test command that prints only failures and a count.\\n40→- [ ] Implement the headless sim: beat, vitals, scoring, tools, gifts, checkpoints, and Op 1; bot clears Op 1; sim tests green.\\n- [ ] Render the operative field (toon, ink, Rapier juice, HUD, pad/mouse/keys) so Op 1 is playable through the report on WebGPU and WebGL2.\\n- [ ] Add remaining tools, Hush, Sforzando, all seven strains, ops 2–7, Last Patient, and Grand Rounds; bot invariants hold.\\n- [ ] Add story scenes, busts, procedural music, effects, and gibberish voices (music gain 0.10).\\n- [ ] Add menus, chart, free play, settings, glyphs, touch notice, save, About, credits, attract mode, and dev mode.\\n- [ ] Add couch co-op and hub session (card, snapshots, host/server rules) until every hub harness run passes.\\n- [ ] Calibrate `parScore`, write the authoring note, fill About and `exit-interview.json`, copy the HTML to the root name, and save sim, browser, hub, and two launch captures under `{SCRATCH}`.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- TASK.md’s shorter WebGPU flags drop the device after the first frame on this machine; PLAN §16.3’s five flags are the ones that draw. A blank readback with those five flags is an app bug, not an excuse to skip the launch check.\\n50→- Software WebGPU is ~260–410 ms/frame and earlyoom kills extra browsers. Hub runs may need the full 300 s budget. Run one browser. If the harness process itself cannot start, capture that log; do not substitute a mocked hub result.\\n- The required HTML and folder names include spaces and a colon (`Trauma Center: Second Opinion`). Keep that literal pattern; Linux allows it.\\n- The released hub-client hash may no longer be the 1.4.0 value in PLAN §16.2 if upstream moved. Hash the vendored file against `RELEASES` in `scripts/lib/hub-client.mjs` and require a match; do not edit the client.\\n- Pad-only audio unlock and real rumble cannot be proven here. Cover synthesis with `OfflineAudioContext` and a missing-actuator guard; say so in Testing Feedback.\\n- Headless Firefox has no WebGL2 here. Do not block on it. WebKit WebGL2 and Chromium WebGPU plus WebGL2 are the bar.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→# Plan: Build the surgical-action demo PLAN.md specifies, and hand in every artifact TASK.md and STAGE.md require\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. Hand-in package. The work folder contains `PROGRESS.md`, a parsable `exit-interview.json` beside the game HTML (one object, every TASK.md key filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, `aiHub.type` Game, `aiHub.genre` Simulation, `aiHub.source` `Trauma Center: Second Opinion`, `aiHub.series` `Trauma Center`, `aiHub.players` `1–4`, controls and tags as PLAN §19), a dev subfolder named `{Title}-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230`, and one root file `{Title}-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230.html`. `{Title}` is an original playful riff on the source (50 titles brainstormed, then one picked with a real RNG), shown on the title screen, and stored so no other source file contains that string. The HTML is one self-contained file that runs from `file://` and from `http://127.0.0.1` (scripts and fonts inlined; no external script or font URLs). It includes exactly one unmodified AI Hub session client (SHA-256 equals the released hash for the vendored version), the §14.6 hub meta tags in that exact `name`-then-`content` order and wording (no `hub:webgpu=required`), catalogue tags (`title` split on the dash, description, `theme-color` `#17707A`, inline SVG icon, `hub:type` Game, `hub:players` `1–4`, `hub:source` `Trauma Center: Second Opinion`), and an About/Credits screen whose `#about-data` JSON shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback with PLAN §19’s fixed values. The same four blocks are in the finishing chat reply. Own source has no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, or `stub`. A short authoring note explains how to add an operation, a strain, a story scene, and a tuning value.\\n\\n2. Surgical simulation. A headless sim (no renderer, DOM, or audio) steps at fixed 1/60 from seeded RNG only (`Math.random` absent from sim code). It implements the beat clock (`bpm` rises as vitals fall; beat drives tissue, ECG, strains, music tempo); vitals, timer, and miss failures; grades FLAWLESS/CLEAN/ROUGH/MISS, combo, and ranks S/A/B/C/D titled Chief of Surgery / Attending / Resident / Intern / Malpractice Suit Pending (never XS, Bad, Good, or Cool); all eight wheel tools plus syringe and defibrillator with the PLAN §4 rules (including analog use, nicks, tears, clamp-on-beat, defib gold band, clamps off before the end); Hush and Sforzando; Intern/Resident/Attending multipliers; phase checkpoints (retry restores vitals ≥60, timer ≥90s, checkpoint score) and optional Bea’s Notes after two failures of the same phase; consults (careful vs aggressive). Same seed and inputs yield the same state hash twice. At the busiest 4-seat Grand Rounds moment the unreliable snapshot is ≤1100 bytes and the keyframe ≤16 KB. Data lint: every touchable spawn lies inside the drape ellipse, `teaches` covers the eight wheel tools, syringe, defib, both gifts, and all seven strains, and no Bea line contains “good” except the final scene.\\n10→\\n3. Demo content a bot can finish. Playable vertical slice: operations Broken Glass, Growth Spurt, Adagio, Staccato, Legato, Fermata, Coda, Last Patient, and Grand Rounds, with strains Adagio, Staccato, Tremolo, Legato, Crescendo, Fermata, and Coda behaving as in PLAN §5, original cast and setting (Port Calloway, St. Alder, Ward Zero, Ren, Bea, Bell, Pip, Sorrento, Ines, Voss/REQUIEM/the Hush — never Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Caduceus, Delphi, GUILT or its strain names, Healing Touch, star drawing, or Hope Hospital). Story scenes S0–S16, 250–350 lines, must-have lines present, scenes skippable. Phases are data and `skipTo` works. On Normal, a bot that reads only sim state clears each operation (Last Patient only has to complete: no timer, no rank, cannot fail) in 45–75% of that op’s timer, minimum vitals ≥35, rank ≥A; `parScore` is taken from that bot. A sloppy bot (noise and reaction delay) on Easy clears at rank ≥C. No input fails every op except Last Patient. Story order through Last Patient, and Grand Rounds at 1, 2, 3, and 4 seats (counts and strain HP scale as PLAN §6), all complete.\\n\\n4. Playable presentation, input, and shell. The shipped page boots a Three.js r180 scene with cel-shaded/toon materials, the ink/halftone filter, code-built organs, instruments, and strains, and Rapier-backed pulls, on WebGPU under PLAN §16.3’s five flags and on WebGL2 without them, with zero page errors. The render surface’s drawing size equals the requested size and is substantially filled (not a blank or a few pixels). Pad (standard mapping), keyboard, and mouse drive the same screens: tip, eight-way wheel, analog use, hover-moves-focus, prompts follow the last device (`data-prompt-set`; PlayStation glyphs default, Xbox via Settings). All music, effects, and Animalese/Simlish voices are synthesized (no audio files); music-bus gain defaults to 0.10. Touch-only (`(any-pointer: fine)` false and no pad) shows exactly “Controller or keyboard required” plus a tappable Settings button, no touch controls, and attract mode still runs. Attract mode starts after 10 s of wall-clock idle on Title, Main Menu, Free Play, and the touch notice, loops a cheated play of many ops, and any input returns to the previous screen without saving. Dev mode opens from pause (hidden in hub sessions) with the PLAN §13 tools and live sliders (current value, default tick, per-page and global reset) that change the sim immediately. Progress survives reload outside a session. A scripted UI playthrough reaches Title, Main Menu, Story, Chart, Operation, Report, and Credits; Fail, Free Play, Lobby, Settings, About, and the touch notice are reachable. Missing graphics shows a friendly screen instead of crashing.\\n\\n5. Multiplayer, hub and couch. With no hub the page starts the standalone game (`AIHub.active` false, `Math.random` untouched). In a session it uses shared-world, seats 1–4, own card: straight into Grand Rounds (no title, menus, attract, story, dev, or saves); pause is Settings only and the sim keeps running; network only through the hub; host alone runs the sim (including a viewless server copy) on a timer not tied to animation frames; clients render snapshots; the local tip is instant; `finish()` is host-only and reports standings, scores, won, stats, and awards. Couch Grand Rounds: up to four seats on one screen; two pads join, move two tips independently, and both score. The real hub harness passes all of: 2 players; 3 players and 4 players at ~150 ms ±50 ms and 5% loss; host-leave; server copy; and the 4-player case again on WebGL2 — each with `__hubCheck.pass === true` and no console line starting with `[AIHub]`. The standalone harness sequence (Enter, Space, Enter, Space) throws nothing and the shot is not blank.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: Add or update in-repo tests that import the shipped sim (no reimplementation, no mocking the sim, no hardcoded full-state oracles) and assert criterion 2 and 3: each tool and strain rule, determinism by comparing two runs, snapshot byte caps, data lint, banned names, bot Normal/Easy/idle invariants, story order, Grand Rounds at 1–4 seats. Run the one shipped command for the sim suite (PLAN §17: `node tests/run-all.mjs sim`). It must print only failures plus a one-line count and exit 0. Save the log to `{SCRATCH}/sim.log`.\\n2. gating: Browser suite (`node tests/run-all.mjs browser`) drives the shipped page, including `?test=1` hooks that call into the real sim, and asserts criterion 4: WebGPU boot with the five flags `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`, WebGL2 boot without them, WebKit boot, zero page errors, non-blank shots, pad/mouse/key input (full right stick for 1 s moves the tip by `tipSpeed` ±5%), prompt set and Xbox glyphs, dev-mode slider changes the sim and reset restores defaults, attract at 10 s ±1 s and exit without a save, Pixel 7 and iPhone 15 profiles show the exact touch notice and a working Settings tap, OfflineAudioContext output is non-silent for every track, effect, and voice, music gain 0.10, no audio-file references, save survives reload, About fields and `#about-data` parse, two stub pads score in couch play, UI playthrough `state().reached` contains Title, Main Menu, Story, Chart, Operation, Report, and Credits roll, packaging checks from criterion 1. Exit 0; failures-only output. Save `{SCRATCH}/browser.log`.\\n20→3. gating: Hub suite (`node tests/run-all.mjs hub`) copies the built page into the real harness from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` and runs every row in criterion 5 (1280×860, up to 300 s, `body[data-complete=\\\"true\\\"]`, `__hubCheck.pass === true`, no `[AIHub]` console line), plus the on-its-own Enter/Space sequence. Exit 0. Save `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\\n4. gating: Launch the real root HTML twice, from `file://` or `http://127.0.0.1` (bind `127.0.0.1` on an OS-picked port; confirm `window.__game` exists before trusting the page). Use the five flags above. Each run: zero page errors; canvas drawing buffer equals the requested size; painted fraction is high (a blank or speck fails — do not treat a successful blank readback as a missing tool); a driven input (stub pad or mouse) changes the on-screen tip or HUD, visible in a second capture. Save `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. Both runs must agree. If Playwright cannot start at all, save that error to `{SCRATCH}/launch.log` and rely on steps 1–3; a started browser that returns an empty buffer is a failure to fix.\\n5. evidence: At 1920×1080, capture Title, Main Menu, a story shout, calm operation, danger, Hush, each strain, Report, Settings, About, the session card, one upright HUD portrait, and one upright cut-in under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Use them only to fix ten-foot type, ink, and overlap; they do not gate if steps 1–4 pass.\\n6. gating: Parse `exit-interview.json`; confirm the root HTML and dev-folder names; confirm the client hash and that the hub banner string appears once; confirm own source (not the bundle) has no banned source names, no `Math.random` in the sim, and no TODO/FIXME/placeholder/stub. Record results in `{SCRATCH}/package.log`.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- FPS targets, frame-time optimization, or lowering scene detail to chase performance.\\n- Playable post-credits “Opus 2”, online play outside AI Hub, custom names, chat, or emotes.\\n- Touch-screen gameplay controls, a second desktop layout, or Firefox WebGL2 (headless Firefox here has no WebGL2).\\n- Hardware rumble feel, a human listener, real iOS Safari, or a physical DualSense (stub the pad; guard a missing vibration actuator).\\n30→- Source-game IP, star-drawing specials, or the source’s grade names.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\nGreenfield work folder (`PLAN.md`, `STAGE.md`, `TASK.md` only). New dev subfolder plus root HTML, `PROGRESS.md`, and `exit-interview.json`. Dependencies installed inside the dev folder: `three@0.180.0`, `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3`, `esbuild@0.28.2`, `playwright@1.63.0`, `@fontsource/bangers`, `@fontsource/barlow-condensed`. Hub `session.js` and harness files downloaded from `michaelcrosato/ai-hub` `lib/hub`. Browsers already on the machine; do not run `playwright install`. Tests and screenshots stay inside the dev folder; command logs copied to `{SCRATCH}`.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md for rules and content; the layout there is guidance so the sim stays testable, not a reason to reject an equivalent structure. Keep the operation sim pure (JSON state, seeded RNG, fixed 1/60, Rapier in field space) so Node tests and the bot drive it with no browser. Let the view, audio, and DOM reconcile that state or a decoded snapshot, never own the rules. Build operations, strains, and scenes as data. Ship one command that runs sim, then browser, then hub, and prints only failures and a count. Inline the browser bundle so `file://` works. Use the five WebGPU flags from PLAN §16.3, not the shorter set in TASK.md. One browser at a time. Stop only processes this work started, by PID.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Scaffold the dev folder, identity (RNG title), tuning defaults, package, inlined single-file build, vendored hub client, and a test command that prints only failures and a count.\\n40→- [ ] Implement the headless sim: beat, vitals, scoring, tools, gifts, checkpoints, and Op 1; bot clears Op 1; sim tests green.\\n- [ ] Render the operative field (toon, ink, Rapier juice, HUD, pad/mouse/keys) so Op 1 is playable through the report on WebGPU and WebGL2.\\n- [ ] Add remaining tools, Hush, Sforzando, all seven strains, ops 2–7, Last Patient, and Grand Rounds; bot invariants hold.\\n- [ ] Add story scenes, busts, procedural music, effects, and gibberish voices (music gain 0.10).\\n- [ ] Add menus, chart, free play, settings, glyphs, touch notice, save, About, credits, attract mode, and dev mode.\\n- [ ] Add couch co-op and hub session (card, snapshots, host/server rules) until every hub harness run passes.\\n- [ ] Calibrate `parScore`, write the authoring note, fill About and `exit-interview.json`, copy the HTML to the root name, and save sim, browser, hub, and two launch captures under `{SCRATCH}`.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- TASK.md’s shorter WebGPU flags drop the device after the first frame on this machine; PLAN §16.3’s five flags are the ones that draw. A blank readback with those five flags is an app bug, not an excuse to skip the launch check.\\n50→- Software WebGPU is ~260–410 ms/frame and earlyoom kills extra browsers. Hub runs may need the full 300 s budget. Run one browser. If the harness process itself cannot start, capture that log; do not substitute a mocked hub result.\\n- The required HTML and folder names include spaces and a colon (`Trauma Center: Second Opinion`). Keep that literal pattern; Linux allows it.\\n- The released hub-client hash may no longer be the 1.4.0 value in PLAN §16.2 if upstream moved. Hash the vendored file against `RELEASES` in `scripts/lib/hub-client.mjs` and require a match; do not edit the client.\\n- Pad-only audio unlock and real rumble cannot be proven here. Cover synthesis with `OfflineAudioContext` and a missing-actuator guard; say so in Testing Feedback.\\n- Headless Firefox has no WebGL2 here. Do not block on it. WebKit WebGL2 and Chromium WebGPU plus WebGL2 are the bar.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6%2Fwork/01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710/goal/plan.md\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"# Plan: Build the surgical-action demo PLAN.md specifies, and hand in every artifact TASK.md and STAGE.md require\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. Hand-in package. The work folder contains `PROGRESS.md`, a parsable `exit-interview.json` beside the game HTML (one object, every TASK.md key filled, `usage` exactly `added on import`, `aiHub.type` Game, `aiHub.genre` Simulation, `aiHub.source` `Trauma Center: Second Opinion`, `aiHub.series` `Trauma Center`, `aiHub.players` `1–4`, controls and tags as PLAN §19), a dev subfolder named `{Title}-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230`, and one root file `{Title}-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230.html`. `{Title}` is an original playful riff on the source (50 titles brainstormed, then one picked with a real RNG), shown on the title screen, and stored so no other source file contains that string. The HTML is one self-contained file that runs from `file://` and from `http://127.0.0.1` (scripts and fonts inlined; no external script or font URLs). It includes exactly one unmodified AI Hub session client (SHA-256 equals the released hash for the vendored version), the §14.6 hub meta tags in that exact `name`-then-`content` order and wording (no `hub:webgpu=required`), catalogue tags (`title` split on the dash, description, `theme-color` `#17707A`, inline SVG icon, `hub:type` Game, `hub:players` `1–4`, `hub:source` `Trauma Center: Second Opinion`), and an About/Credits screen whose `#about-data` JSON shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback with PLAN §19’s fixed values. The same four blocks are in the finishing chat reply. Own source has no `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, or `stub`. A short authoring note explains how to add an operation, a strain, a story scene, and a tuning value.\\n\\n2. Surgical simulation. A headless sim (no renderer, DOM, or audio) steps at fixed 1/60 from seeded RNG only (`Math.random` absent from sim code). It implements the beat clock (`bpm` rises as vitals fall; beat drives tissue, ECG, strains, music tempo); vitals, timer, and miss failures; grades FLAWLESS/CLEAN/ROUGH/MISS, combo, and ranks S/A/B/C/D titled Chief of Surgery / Attending / Resident / Intern / Malpractice Suit Pending (never XS, Bad, Good, or Cool); all eight wheel tools plus syringe and defibrillator with the PLAN §4 rules (including analog use, nicks, tears, clamp-on-beat, defib gold band, clamps off before the end); Hush and Sforzando; Intern/Resident/Attending multipliers; phase checkpoints (retry restores vitals ≥60, timer ≥90s, checkpoint score) and optional Bea’s Notes after two failures of the same phase; consults (careful vs aggressive). Same seed and inputs yield the same state hash twice. At the busiest 4-seat Grand Rounds moment the unreliable snapshot is ≤1100 bytes and the keyframe ≤16 KB. Data lint: every touchable spawn lies inside the drape ellipse, `teaches` covers the eight wheel tools, syringe, defib, both gifts, and all seven strains, and no Bea line contains “good” except the final scene.\\n\\n3. Demo content a bot can finish. Playable vertical slice: operations Broken Glass, Growth Spurt, Adagio, Staccato, Legato, Fermata, Coda, Last Patient, and Grand Rounds, with strains Adagio, Staccato, Tremolo, Legato, Crescendo, Fermata, and Coda behaving as in PLAN §5, original cast and setting (Port Calloway, St. Alder, Ward Zero, Ren, Bea, Bell, Pip, Sorrento, Ines, Voss/REQUIEM/the Hush — never Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Caduceus, Delphi, GUILT or its strain names, Healing Touch, star drawing, or Hope Hospital). Story scenes S0–S16, 250–350 lines, must-have lines present, scenes skippable. Phases are data and `skipTo` works. On Normal, a bot that reads only sim state clears each operation (Last Patient only has to complete: no timer, no rank, cannot fail) in 45–75% of that op’s timer, minimum vitals ≥35, rank ≥A; `parScore` is taken from that bot. A sloppy bot (noise and reaction delay) on Easy clears at rank ≥C. No input fails every op except Last Patient. Story order through Last Patient, and Grand Rounds at 1, 2, 3, and 4 seats (counts and strain HP scale as PLAN §6), all complete.\\n\\n4. Playable presentation, input, and shell. The shipped page boots a Three.js r180 scene with cel-shaded/toon materials, the ink/halftone filter, code-built organs, instruments, and strains, and Rapier-backed pulls, on WebGPU under PLAN §16.3’s five flags and on WebGL2 without them, with zero page errors. The render surface’s drawing size equals the requested size and is substantially filled (not a blank or a few pixels). Pad (standard mapping), keyboard, and mouse drive the same screens: tip, eight-way wheel, analog use, hover-moves-focus, prompts follow the last device (`data-prompt-set`; PlayStation glyphs default, Xbox via Settings). All music, effects, and Animalese/Simlish voices are synthesized (no audio files); music-bus gain defaults to 0.10. Touch-only (`(any-pointer: fine)` false and no pad) shows exactly “Controller or keyboard required” plus a tappable Settings button, no touch controls, and attract mode still runs. Attract mode starts after 10 s of wall-clock idle on Title, Main Menu, Free Play, and the touch notice, loops a cheated play of many ops, and any input returns to the previous screen without saving. Dev mode opens from pause (hidden in hub sessions) with the PLAN §13 tools and live sliders (current value, default tick, per-page and global reset) that change the sim immediately. Progress survives reload outside a session. A scripted UI playthrough reaches Title, Main Menu, Story, Chart, Operation, Report, and Credits; Fail, Free Play, Lobby, Settings, About, and the touch notice are reachable. Missing graphics shows a friendly screen instead of crashing.\\n\\n5. Multiplayer, hub and couch. With no hub the page starts the standalone game (`AIHub.active` false, `Math.random` untouched). In a session it uses shared-world, seats 1–4, own card: straight into Grand Rounds (no title, menus, attract, story, dev, or saves); pause is Settings only and the sim keeps running; network only through the hub; host alone runs the sim (including a viewless server copy) on a timer not tied to animation frames; clients render snapshots; the local tip is instant; `finish()` is host-only and reports standings, scores, won, stats, and awards. Couch Grand Rounds: up to four seats on one screen; two pads join, move two tips independently, and both score. The real hub harness passes all of: 2 players; 3 players and 4 players at ~150 ms ±50 ms and 5% loss; host-leave; server copy; and the 4-player case again on WebGL2 — each with `__hubCheck.pass === true` and no console line starting with `[AIHub]`. The standalone harness sequence (Enter, Space, Enter, Space) throws nothing and the shot is not blank.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: Add or update in-repo tests that import the shipped sim (no reimplementation, no mocking the sim, no hardcoded full-state oracles) and assert criterion 2 and 3: each tool and strain rule, determinism by comparing two runs, snapshot byte caps, data lint, banned names, bot Normal/Easy/idle invariants, story order, Grand Rounds at 1–4 seats. Run the one shipped command for the sim suite (PLAN §17: `node tests/run-all.mjs sim`). It must print only failures plus a one-line count and exit 0. Save the log to `{SCRATCH}/sim.log`.\\n2. gating: Browser suite (`node tests/run-all.mjs browser`) drives the shipped page, including `?test=1` hooks that call into the real sim, and asserts criterion 4: WebGPU boot with the five flags `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`, WebGL2 boot without them, WebKit boot, zero page errors, non-blank shots, pad/mouse/key input (full right stick for 1 s moves the tip by `tipSpeed` ±5%), prompt set and Xbox glyphs, dev-mode slider changes the sim and reset restores defaults, attract at 10 s ±1 s and exit without a save, Pixel 7 and iPhone 15 profiles show the exact touch notice and a working Settings tap, OfflineAudioContext output is non-silent for every track, effect, and voice, music gain 0.10, no audio-file references, save survives reload, About fields and `#about-data` parse, two stub pads score in couch play, UI playthrough `state().reached` contains Title, Main Menu, Story, Chart, Operation, Report, and Credits roll, packaging checks from criterion 1. Exit 0; failures-only output. Save `{SCRATCH}/browser.log`.\\n3. gating: Hub suite (`node tests/run-all.mjs hub`) copies the built page into the real harness from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` and runs every row in criterion 5 (1280×860, up to 300 s, `body[data-complete=\\\"true\\\"]`, `__hubCheck.pass === true`, no `[AIHub]` console line), plus the on-its-own Enter/Space sequence. Exit 0. Save `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`.\\n4. gating: Launch the real root HTML twice, from `file://` or `http://127.0.0.1` (bind `127.0.0.1` on an OS-picked port; confirm `window.__game` exists before trusting the page). Use the five flags above. Each run: zero page errors; canvas drawing buffer equals the requested size; painted fraction is high (a blank or speck fails — do not treat a successful blank readback as a missing tool); a driven input (stub pad or mouse) changes the on-screen tip or HUD, visible in a second capture. Save `{SCRATCH}/launch-1.png`, `{SCRATCH}/launch-2.png`, and `{SCRATCH}/launch.log`. Both runs must agree. If Playwright cannot start at all, save that error to `{SCRATCH}/launch.log` and rely on steps 1–3; a started browser that returns an empty buffer is a failure to fix.\\n5. evidence: At 1920×1080, capture Title, Main Menu, a story shout, calm operation, danger, Hush, each strain, Report, Settings, About, the session card, one upright HUD portrait, and one upright cut-in under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Use them only to fix ten-foot type, ink, and overlap; they do not gate if steps 1–4 pass.\\n6. gating: Parse `exit-interview.json`; confirm the root HTML and dev-folder names; confirm the client hash and that the hub banner string appears once; confirm own source (not the bundle) has no banned source names, no `Math.random` in the sim, and no TODO/FIXME/placeholder/stub. Record results in `{SCRATCH}/package.log`.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- FPS targets, frame-time optimization, or lowering scene detail to chase performance.\\n- Playable post-credits “Opus 2”, online play outside AI Hub, custom names, chat, or emotes.\\n- Touch-screen gameplay controls, a second desktop layout, or Firefox WebGL2 (headless Firefox here has no WebGL2).\\n- Hardware rumble feel, a human listener, real iOS Safari, or a physical DualSense (stub the pad; guard a missing vibration actuator).\\n- Source-game IP, star-drawing specials, or the source’s grade names.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\nGreenfield work folder (`PLAN.md`, `STAGE.md`, `TASK.md` only). New dev subfolder plus root HTML, `PROGRESS.md`, and `exit-interview.json`. Dependencies installed inside the dev folder: `three@0.180.0`, `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3`, `esbuild@0.28.2`, `playwright@1.63.0`, `@fontsource/bangers`, `@fontsource/barlow-condensed`. Hub `session.js` and harness files downloaded from `michaelcrosato/ai-hub` `lib/hub`. Browsers already on the machine; do not run `playwright install`. Tests and screenshots stay inside the dev folder; command logs copied to `{SCRATCH}`.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\nFollow PLAN.md for rules and content; the layout there is guidance so the sim stays testable, not a reason to reject an equivalent structure. Keep the operation sim pure (JSON state, seeded RNG, fixed 1/60, Rapier in field space) so Node tests and the bot drive it with no browser. Let the view, audio, and DOM reconcile that state or a decoded snapshot, never own the rules. Build operations, strains, and scenes as data. Ship one command that runs sim, then browser, then hub, and prints only failures and a count. Inline the browser bundle so `file://` works. Use the five WebGPU flags from PLAN §16.3, not the shorter set in TASK.md. One browser at a time. Stop only processes this work started, by PID.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [ ] Scaffold the dev folder, identity (RNG title), tuning defaults, package, inlined single-file build, vendored hub client, and a test command that prints only failures and a count.\\n- [ ] Implement the headless sim: beat, vitals, scoring, tools, gifts, checkpoints, and Op 1; bot clears Op 1; sim tests green.\\n- [ ] Render the operative field (toon, ink, Rapier juice, HUD, pad/mouse/keys) so Op 1 is playable through the report on WebGPU and WebGL2.\\n- [ ] Add remaining tools, Hush, Sforzando, all seven strains, ops 2–7, Last Patient, and Grand Rounds; bot invariants hold.\\n- [ ] Add story scenes, busts, procedural music, effects, and gibberish voices (music gain 0.10).\\n- [ ] Add menus, chart, free play, settings, glyphs, touch notice, save, About, credits, attract mode, and dev mode.\\n- [ ] Add couch co-op and hub session (card, snapshots, host/server rules) until every hub harness run passes.\\n- [ ] Calibrate `parScore`, write the authoring note, fill About and `exit-interview.json`, copy the HTML to the root name, and save sim, browser, hub, and two launch captures under `{SCRATCH}`.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- TASK.md’s shorter WebGPU flags drop the device after the first frame on this machine; PLAN §16.3’s five flags are the ones that draw. A blank readback with those five flags is an app bug, not an excuse to skip the launch check.\\n- Software WebGPU is ~260–410 ms/frame and earlyoom kills extra browsers. Hub runs may need the full 300 s budget. Run one browser. If the harness process itself cannot start, capture that log; do not substitute a mocked hub result.\\n- The required HTML and folder names include spaces and a colon (`Trauma Center: Second Opinion`). Keep that literal pattern; Linux allows it.\\n- The released hub-client hash may no longer be the 1.4.0 value in PLAN §16.2 if upstream moved. Hash the vendored file against `RELEASES` in `scripts/lib/hub-client.mjs` and require a match; do not edit the client.\\n- Pad-only audio unlock and real rumble cannot be proven here. Cover synthesis with `OfflineAudioContext` and a missing-actuator guard; say so in Testing Feedback.\\n- Headless Firefox has no WebGL2 here. Do not block on it. WebKit WebGL2 and Chromium WebGPU plus WebGL2 are the bar.\\n\",\"total_lines\":54}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-7d748341-699e-4b1a-ba16-8c17cc8ce3ed-1","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/STAGE.md\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\\n\",\"total_lines\":1}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-7d748341-699e-4b1a-ba16-8c17cc8ce3ed-2","content":"{\"type\":\"ListDir\",\"Content\":{\"content\":\"- /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/\\n  - PLAN.md\\n  - STAGE.md\\n  - TASK.md\",\"absolute_root_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work\"}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"62f2afef-06dd-4aa1-b854-14776782ad47"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_6","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a massive game development task.","signature":"OAxSagNn+7Ed4jdXHAmdW10ycD5vJvYimXkkA5XbG82JQQHvPh1rUL8wzqDLKBvRtZGJsPorKk9hAKaIaqzRf7XhzF+G8KP2jfm303ICaxzE+Wy4Gt1VVkqTH5halAQ40v3pR7Zxa/JOrYsLJ2jN2a/qCjd9C8tYsjcBletSKeaqkmPWd9yL5E3VnRq4MXddr4r62n9vDKrYxDx9PCus3hiPRTF3D2J4uG8vBobkO2juwoeIn6y4h+2Ts0/biZlXhrnaJ4rZIrQLXUBqEHC4"},{"type":"text","text":"The plan is a full surgical-action game. I'll read PLAN.md and TASK.md next and seed the checklist from them."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-36268023-480f-4557-9f75-5b28aad397d0-3","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/TASK.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-36268023-480f-4557-9f75-5b28aad397d0-4","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/PLAN.md","limit":400}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4127,"output_tokens":173,"cache_read_input_tokens":21376,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"8fd60866-76d6-4c73-b428-a12f043856cd"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-36268023-480f-4557-9f75-5b28aad397d0-3","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→SOURCE: Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Use Three.js R180, WEBGPU, use a stylized cel-shaded / ink filter, rapier 0.19.3 for physics, Web Audio, Gamepad API; The rest of the tech stack is yours to choose.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n20→\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n30→Effort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n40→  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\n50→No script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 23:12:30\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→SOURCE: Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230].html. Dependencies that can be download