grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/strains.js\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":13,\"content\":\"      if (e.kind === 'adagio' && e.stunned <= 0 && e.hp <= 0 && !e.tray && !e.holder) {\"},{\"line_number\":142,\"content\":\"    link.need = !link.cut ? (link.index === e.active ? 'scalpel' : 'wait') : link.tray ? 'done' : 'forceps';\"},{\"line_number\":143,\"content\":\"    if (link.cut && !link.tray && !link.holder) {\"},{\"line_number\":169,\"content\":\"    ent.grabbable = !!(link.cut && !link.tray);\"},{\"line_number\":171,\"content\":\"    ent.need = link.cut ? (link.tray ? 'done' : 'forceps') : (ent.index === e.active ? 'scalpel' : 'wait');\"},{\"line_number\":172,\"content\":\"    if (link.tray) ent.alive = false;\"},{\"line_number\":174,\"content\":\"  if (e.links.length && e.links.every((l) => l.tray)) {\"},{\"line_number\":176,\"content\":\"    e.tray = true;\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"76f4a8c5-1331-4226-a821-1f8628bab578"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_103","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The adagio wake does not clear the tray.\n\nThe adagio that disappeared was likely the grande. It had already been lasered to 0 HP before the first sample, so the logged HP belongs to the old trayed adagio. The grande may still be alive.\n\nThe old adagio likely wakes at pt=10. It may have been trayed when the grande started, or it may have survived extraction.\n\nkillLaser leaves adagio alive at zero HP until trayed, so the phase could not end without traying. The grande was likely trayed by pt=6. A wake on the grande would only occur if it were dropped rather than trayed. The bug is becoming clear.\n\nA trayed adagio still steps. When its stun expires, it may not be trayed, so it wakes.\n\nThe wake HP is 40 times the scaled value. The bot must kill it again. Meanwhile the grande flag causes bleeders. The bot holds forceps while trying to extract a body that wakes and does not clamp because it is stuck carrying.\n\nAdagios are not embedded by default. carry() should drop them into the tray in about two seconds.\n\nThe bot is holding the adagio but never reaches the tray. The carry function only releases when overTray, so the adagio may be stuck in a wall or unreachable position.\n\nAdagio grabbability requires a positive stun value. The bot grabs the adagio during its five-second stun and should reach the tray in about one and a half seconds. If embedded is set, the axis pull may drag the creature into a corner forever. The stun expires while it is held, so the bot keeps holding a woken creature.\n\nThe old adagio may not have been trayed at all. If the phase cleared without it, the bot would grab it from the wrong tray site and cause a forceps mismatch. I will re-examine how the creature phase finishes.\n\nScratches spawned by a trayed adagio land on the tray site because the adagio's position is set to the tray coordinates on release. The bot holds forceps for twenty seconds while open bleeders are active, but it never grabs those scratches.\n\nThe bot ignores tray bleeders because the clamp logic skips entities on the tray site. They still drain vitals, causing infinite damage. Trayed strains must be fully removed from simulation and AI when extracted.\n\nSetting `alive = false` in `releaseItem` when an entity is trayed would fix the stuck simulation. The phase-clear check still passes because it only requires `alive && !tray`. Dead entities are already omitted from exports, so the bot will not see them. Only the actual remove action should set alive to false.\n\nThe coda circle cut never starts. The bot holds the scalpel at the starting point because `started` never becomes true. The simulation only sets `started` when the distance to the start drops below 0.08, but the bot never reaches that threshold.\n\nThe coda cut may not start because the bot is not actually at the first point. If the target is not pts[0], the distance never drops below 0.08 and `started` stays false. The scalpel is held but the path never begins.\n\nThe bot should reach the first coda point and start the cut. If `started` is true but `along` never increases, the bot is not moving. It may be stuck because it thinks it should not advance.\n\nThe bot may see `started` as true while the simulation still treats it as false, or vice versa. The circle's center is pulled toward the entity when within 0.12, but the rim sits 0.22 away so that assist does not apply there. If the bot strays outside the 0.055 polyline tolerance, it would nick and stall without advancing along.\n\nThe circle cut may have no points at all. A spawn wrapper bug could cause rawSpawn to drop the `points` array when creating the entity, so the bot would have no path to follow.\n\nThe fermata circle cut completed successfully, so the spawn path and points array are not the problem. The coda circle is the only one that stalls at zero along. The cause must lie elsewhere.\n\nThe bot may be stopped at a point that is not the true start, so it never reaches the 0.08 threshold to set `started`. Alternatively, the simulation may project the target ahead of the current along value, placing it outside the 0.055 polyline tolerance and preventing the cut from advancing.\n\nA closed-loop projection bug may prevent the coda cut from starting. Because the circle's first and last points are identical, the nearest segment could be the final one with along ≈ length instead of the first one at zero. The bot would then target the wrong point and never reach the 0.08 start threshold.\n\nThe closed-loop projection theory is wrong. If the nearest segment were the final one, along would jump to the full length and the cut would finish instantly, just as it did on fermata. Because coda remains at zero along, the bot is not actually at the start point. It is holding the scalpel away from the circle, so started never becomes true.\n\nThe coda circle may be missing its points array. If the bot sees no points, it holds the scalpel and never moves. This matches the observed behavior exactly. The fermata circle had points and completed successfully, so the cause is unclear.\n\nI will inspect the live spawn path in sim.js to see if circle points are stripped. Fermata's cut may have finished instantly because the bot was already near the crescendo center, not on the circle rim.\n\nFermata's circle was cut properly in under two seconds, so the coda stall is not a timing issue. The bot is stuck and cannot progress the cut. I will log the seat position, first point, and started flag for one coda frame to find the exact cause.\n\nThe circle points are inside the drape, so the tip clamp can reach them. The bot may be stuck in a deadzone: when the distance to the start is below 0.012 but the required speed magnitude is under 0.13, the steer function returns stop early and the bot never closes the gap to trigger started.\n\nThe deadzone theory is wrong. At a distance of 0.05 the speed magnitude is about 0.46, which is well above the 0.13 threshold, so the bot should move. I will now inspect the seat position and the circle's first point during the stall to find the real cause.\n\nThe circle's first and last points are identical, so projectPoly may pick the final segment as nearest and snap along to the full length. I will log a detailed snapshot to verify this. The tray-alive bug is confirmed for adagio.\n\nThe circle segments are small enough to avoid scalpel tolerance issues. The bot and circle share the same aorta site, so actOn is not switching targets. I will write a short probe script, then fix the tray-alive flag, arrest vitals restore, and feeder priority before retesting.\n\nThe scalpel hold threshold is 0.032, which is below the 0.08 start distance, so the bot should begin the cut once it arrives. The real issue may be that `use` is not greater than 0.5 on the arrival frame, causing the bot to hold the scalpel instead of moving. Later frames show `use` at 1.\n\nThe closed-loop projection theory is now the leading explanation. Because the circle's first and last points are identical, `projectPoly` may pick the final segment as nearest while the bot is still moving toward the start. This would snap `along` to the full length and instantly finish the cut. That would explain why fermata completed but coda stalls at zero.\n\nThe bot is likely frozen at the feeder, 0.28 units from the circle center. If the coda circle has no points array, the bot holds the scalpel and never moves, so `along` stays at zero. This matches the stall exactly. Fermata still has points and completed its cut, which raises the question of why coda would be missing them.\n\nThe feeder sits 0.28 units from the circle center, which is 0.06 units outside the 0.055 scalpel tolerance. This explains why the coda cut never starts while fermata's did. If the circle points are missing, the bot would freeze at the feeder and hold the scalpel forever.\n\nI am now inspecting the rawSpawn function in sim.js to see if it copies the circle's points array when creating the entity. The earlier read at offset 760 returned exportState instead of spawn, so I need to locate the actual spawn body.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-350d61e7-cea9-4930-9e6d-080f2b40c2ab-62","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"function rawSpawn|rawSpawn =","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/sim.js"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2833,"output_tokens":4780,"cache_read_input_tokens":139904,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"b76e0574-074e-46b1-8622-aa900846bcf4"}
