      const speed = tun.tipSpeed * sm ** tun.stickCurve * (steady ? tun.steadyFactor : 1);\"},{\"line_number\":549,\"content\":\"  const r = sim.tuning.gelRadius;\"},{\"line_number\":578,\"content\":\"    e.cols = Math.max(1, Math.ceil((x1 - x0) / CELL));\"},{\"line_number\":579,\"content\":\"    e.rows = Math.max(1, Math.ceil((y1 - y0) / CELL));\"},{\"line_number\":591,\"content\":\"      const cx = x0 + (col + 0.5) * CELL;\"},{\"line_number\":592,\"content\":\"      const cy = y0 + (row + 0.5) * CELL;\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/sim.js\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":819,\"content\":\"      tipSpeed: this.tuning.tipSpeed, stickCurve: this.tuning.stickCurve, steadyFactor: this.tuning.steadyFactor,\"},{\"line_number\":821,\"content\":\"      gelRadius: this.tuning.gelRadius,\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/bot.js\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":38,\"content\":\"function magFor(want, tuning, steady) {\"},{\"line_number\":39,\"content\":\"  const cruise = tuning.tipSpeed * (steady ? tuning.steadyFactor : 1);\"},{\"line_number\":41,\"content\":\"  return Math.min(1, ratio ** (1 / tuning.stickCurve));\"},{\"line_number\":44,\"content\":\"function steer(me, tx, ty, want, tuning, opts, steady = false) {\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"65db7cb8-5de1-4452-8e59-66288d8437de"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_148","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Tip speed is 1.9 and stick curve is 1.7. The bot paint speed target is zero.\n\nThe target speed of 0.58 is confirmed as the actual field-units per second when not slowing near the target. The gel path length for a rectangle is being calculated from its adjusted width and height, with rows spaced at 0.12 units.\n\nThe default gel rectangle is 1.72 units wide and 0.86 units tall, requiring eight rows. Its path length is about 14.6 units, so the paint time is roughly 25 seconds.\n\nThe finish gel on broken glass is 12.2 units long and takes about 21 seconds. Combined with the default gel, that is roughly 47 seconds of gel work, leaving about 56 seconds for gameplay. To add 40 more seconds, I need about one large default gel zone plus a medium one. The drape ellipse spans ±1.4 horizontally and ±0.85 vertically.\n\nI need to find the exact lint rule that requires spawns inside the drape ellipse. Gel zones default to position [0,0], which is inside the ellipse, so they likely satisfy that check automatically.\n\nThe bot paints one gel zone at a time, so multiple zones in the same phase are handled sequentially. The default rectangles already achieve roughly 90% coverage, so similar shapes should suffice. I will add extra gel zones and possibly extra wounds to the close and finish phases to pad the total time.\n\nA table shows the time gaps to reach the 60% target for each phase. Broken glass needs about 40 seconds more. Growth needs roughly 121 seconds more. Adagio and staccato need about 100 seconds each. Legato needs about 150 seconds. Fermata needs about 193 seconds. Coda with healAt is near the band but still short by about 5 seconds; removing healAt would leave it about 175 seconds below target.\n\nRaising healAt to about 65 for living coda could help, but it would also increase minimum time. Stacking many gel zones risks vitals draining during long painting sessions. I need to check whether scriptDrain from the crash phase persists across phase changes. Vitals still drain while the timer runs during gel, so broken glass’s minimum of 75 already accounts for that base drain.\n\nBleeder vitals stay clamped during close phases until gel and suture finish, so they do not drain while padding gel. Fermata close also leaves a bleeder unclamped, but it follows the same pattern.\n\nLegato repair completed with four unclamped brain bleeders still alive. The allClampsOff requirement may not actually prevent completion when no bleeders are clamped, so those bleeders could drain vitals during long gel padding and kill the patient. I need to check how legato bleeders spawn and whether they drain during repair.\n\nLegato repair completed in 65 seconds with minimum vitals at 98.5, suggesting the unclamped brain bleeders spawned too late to drain vitals during that short phase. Adding 150 seconds of gel padding would give those bleeders enough time to kill the patient. I need to check stepBleeder and when jerks spawn to confirm.\n\nThe repair objective \"Close the vessel\" matches the closing regex, so the bot skips clamping bleeders during that phase. They remain unclamped and drain vitals throughout the entire repair. The current 65-second repair keeps minimum vitals near 98 because the bleeders spawn too late to cause damage. Adding long gel padding would let those bleeders drain the patient.\n\nPadding legato on the membrane phase avoids the dangerous repair phase. Bleeders spawn during chain and thrash, so they stay short. Membrane gel could reach 150 seconds without killing the patient. Other operations should use early safe phases or close phases with no active drain.\n\nFermata close also leaves an unclamped heart bleeder that drains vitals. The current 59-second close is too short for it to kill the patient, but adding 190 seconds of gel there would let it drain long enough to do so. Padding fermata's open phase instead is safe because it has no strains yet.\n\nGrowth crash applies scriptDrain and sets vitals to 48.\n\nGrowth close should be padded in the open phase instead, since close ends with unclamped bleeders. Adagio grande spawns bleeders during close, so the open and look phases are safer for padding; creature fight scratches may interrupt gel work there. Staccato holes spawn notes that drain vitals if ignored, so the open phase is the right place for extra gel. BG already has 103 seconds of gameplay, so adding one more gel zone to the finish phase should cover the remaining 40-second gap.\n\nCoda is already 252 seconds long, so adding only five seconds of gel should push it into the target band. The closing phase still carries leftover heart and lung bleeders that could drain vitals during a long gel session, but the conductor fight is fragile enough that a small addition is preferable to a large one.\n\nCoda should not receive long gel in its closing phase because leftover bleeders drain vitals during that time. The extra five seconds can instead come from raising healAt to about 66 for living coda, which also raises minimum vitals. The plan is to fix healAt so it only triggers when the patient is alive and raise the landing minimum vitals to at least thirty-five.\n\nA healAt of 64 should land vitals near forty, though the exact value needs testing because extra trips and Hush timing may shift the result. Suture is too slow for large pads, so gel remains the better choice. Multiple gel zones can be stacked in one phase; done.cleared marks all of them as finished.\n\nThe bot paints gel zones one by one, prioritizing by site and distance. Keeping all zones on the same site avoids unnecessary site switching. The default gel rectangle corners both fall inside the drape ellipse, so no lint failure is expected.\n\nA standard PAD gel rectangle fits safely inside the drape ellipse. Eight zones would take roughly 190 seconds, which is a lot of real work.\n\nA wider gel rectangle fits inside the drape ellipse, but eight zones still take about 240 seconds. That overshoots fermata’s 315-second ceiling, so seven zones would be safer. I am recalculating the exact time to confirm.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-89b13241-8b1e-4aef-aa42-5bcc0b41fe34-20","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"vitals\\s*-=|scriptDrain|drainScale|passive","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/sim.js"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-89b13241-8b1e-4aef-aa42-5bcc0b41fe34-21","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"drape|ellipse|1\\.4|touchable|inside","path":"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6%2Fwork/01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710/goal/plan.md"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1539,"output_tokens":3918,"cache_read_input_tokens":58752,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"3ca5cd93-ac75-404e-92b4-f6c87678a2b3"}
