{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_153","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"`when.removed` is true once any entity of that kind exists but none are alive.\n\nTrigger chaining via flags is possible, but `advancePhase` runs triggers before `phaseCleared`. This ordering could cause issues with the cleared check.\n\nA single extra layer works cleanly. The first trigger spawns the next gel zone before phaseCleared runs, so the phase does not clear prematurely. Once that extra gel dies, the phase clears.\n\nFor multiple layers, a second trigger is needed. It can use a flag to avoid firing again.\n\n`triggerMet` evaluates each condition independently. A failed check does not return false; it simply skips that branch. The function only returns false at the end if no condition matched.\n\nTrigger conditions are ORed, not ANDed. A trigger fires if any single condition is met, so combining a flag with `removed` will not work. PhaseTime gates provide a cleaner way to chain multiple layers.\n\nOverlapping gel zones would share a grid and paint together. Each new zone needs a full paint. Spawning at phaseTime 28 and 56 keeps layers separated.\n\nSpawning too early risks overlapping zones that paint together. The phase cannot clear until every gel zone is dead, so the timer must wait for the previous layer to finish first.\n\nGel completion does not set a flag automatically. Modifying the trigger system to support AND conditions would be cleaner but carries more risk. Spacing phaseTime triggers wider than the gel duration creates idle gaps where the bot waits. This is acceptable but less ideal than spawning the next zone immediately after the previous one finishes.\n\nA minimal `when.all` condition would let triggers require both `removed` and a flag simultaneously. The first gel zone trigger could spawn the next layer and set a completion flag, then use `once` to prevent it from firing again.\n\nIf both triggers use `removed` alone, they fire simultaneously on the same frame when the first gel dies and spawn overlapping zones. The second trigger must require both `removed` and a flag set by the first trigger to chain them properly.\n\nThe `when.all` condition is required for the chaining logic to work correctly. The second trigger must wait until the newly spawned gel zone is alive, so it cannot fire immediately after the first gel dies. Once that second gel dies and the completion flag is set, the next layer spawns properly. This confirms that phase clearing will not complete prematurely while intermediate layers are still alive.\n\nA `gelChain` helper is being drafted to generate the trigger arrays for chained gel layers. It defines a zone factory and iterates through the layers, building each trigger's `when` condition. The first layer fires on `removed: 'gelZone'`, while subsequent layers require both `removed` and the prior completion flag via `when.all`.\n\nThe `gelChain` helper is being finalized with a `once: true` flag on each trigger. The first trigger spawns the next layer and sets its completion flag, while the second trigger requires both that flag and the previous gel zone to be removed. This ordering prevents overlapping spawns because the second trigger sees the newly spawned zone as alive when it first evaluates. A bug was spotted: the first trigger's condition is too broad and only checks `removed: 'gelZone'`.\n\nThe `once: true` flag prevents the first trigger from firing again. `when.removed` correctly returns false before any gel is painted because the zone spawns immediately. Existing gel zones can be handled by enlarging a single large rectangle and chaining copies of it.\n\nChained gel layers are safe only in phases without creature threats. The bot will cut first, then spawn the chain. Phase completion waits for the last gel layer to die. Broken glass needs roughly 40 seconds added. One extra layer brings the time to about 130 seconds. Two layers reach about 156 seconds. The target is 144 seconds.\n\nBroken glass needs two extra gel layers. The finish phase will then take about 147 seconds, which sits comfortably inside the 108–180 second band.\n\nGrowth needs roughly 121 more seconds to reach its target of 198. Five extra layers at about 26 seconds each would bring the total to around 207, still safely under the 247-second ceiling.\n\nAdagio needs about 100 seconds added to reach its target of 198. Four extra large gel layers would bring the total to roughly 202, leaving headroom under the 247-second ceiling. The grande phase currently has 100 HP and is already included in the 97-second baseline, so adding more gel layers there should not push the total over the limit.\n\nAdagio will start with three extra gel layers at HP 100, aiming for about 176 seconds. HP 150 may push the total to roughly 210, still inside the band.\n\nStaccato needs about 104 seconds added, bringing the total to around 220.\n\nLegato needs about 156 seconds added, bringing the total to around 222.\n\nThe gel chain in the membrane phase might delay the bleeders.\n\nBleeders spawn in later phases, so the long membrane gel chain will not delay them. Fermata needs about 182 seconds added to reach its target of 252; seven extra layers bring the total to roughly 241. Reverting the scratch healAt change would return Coda to about 167.7 seconds.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-709df113-ef94-4f8b-a293-fd42973bdb0b-28","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"kind === 'bleeder'|clamped","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim","glob":"*.js"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2339,"output_tokens":2960,"cache_read_input_tokens":74240,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"83160a97-5661-4460-85d1-0904ba77449b"}
