comp 5 openB 0 scr 5 hush wash\\nwin 79.50 v 46.0 tool laser xy -0.21 0.07 cd -0.017 comp 5 openB 0 scr 5 hush wash\\nwin 80.00 v 45.7 tool laser xy 0.36 0.14 cd -0.017 comp 5 openB 0 scr 5 hush wash\\nEND true t 170.8 vmin 32.54 fail null\\n(node:31427) Warning: The 'NO_COLOR' env is ignored due to the 'FORCE_COLOR' env being set.\\n(Use `node --trace-warnings ...` to show where the warning was created)\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"command\":\"node \\\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/tmp/grok-goal-c4cb33e83374/implementer/dbg-vmin.mjs\\\" coda\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Sample coda bot during vitals dip\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6%2Fwork/01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710/terminal/call-9549260b-fcd7-47d5-869e-aeb9e3058c38-65.log\",\"total_bytes\":4034}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"f8610951-c7b0-4a66-827f-4e0faccdcb21"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_174","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The task is clear. It is at t=64.5-65.\n\nVitals drop by 6 at 66.0, 68.0, 71.5, and 73.5. The 68.0 drop lands during the syringe trip to IV. Vitals heal to 50.3 at 68.5 and 53.5 at 72.5. They reach 46.2 at 73.5 and 34.8 at 75.5.\n\nThe floor is 33.6 at IV. It is hit before the +15 heal applies. At 76.5 vitals reach 47.5 after healing and a hush inhale. Composure resets to zero. Scratches drain roughly 2.9 per second. At 75.0 vitals are 41.8 and the syringe cooldown is 0.317. The bot still lasers because it has not yet moved to IV.\n\nThe floor is 32.5, the lowest point during travel and hold before injection. The bot starts moving at 75.5 while the cooldown is nearly expired. It reaches the IV at 76.0 with vitals at 33.6. The syringe cooldown drops from 1.3 to 0.3 between 74.0 and 75.0. A high healAt value would make the bot leave immediately when the cooldown hits zero.\n\nThe bot waits for the syringe cooldown to expire before starting its trip. The downbeat strike lands during that travel. To survive, vitals must be at least 44 at the start of the trip. That leaves enough after the -6 and travel drain to reach the IV floor.\n\nThe cooldown only becomes ready once vitals have already dropped to 41, so the bot cannot start its trip earlier. The -6 strike happens because the baton is not killed during the windup; the bot is either at the IV or gelling scratches instead. A fix could be to gel scratches while waiting for the cooldown to reduce drain, or to prevent the downbeat from hitting if the baton is dead.\n\nThe bot beams batons during windup but misses the windup during the IV trip, so every syringe trip risks a downbeat. The fix is to skip syringing during the windup beat and gel scratches instead to reduce drain. Another option is to raise the heal threshold so trips start only when vitals are 55 or higher and the cooldown is ready.\n\nThe syringe action runs before the work list, so it preempts baton killing. This is why trips eat downbeats. The fix is to add a `windup` check that suppresses syringing whenever any baton is in windup. That lets the bot kill the baton first and then syringe on a safe beat.\n\nSuppressing syringing during windup would remove the -6 strike entirely. The bot would then kill the baton first and syringe on a safe beat. Without those downbeats, vitals would stay higher during travel. A trip starting at 42 would land around 39 after scratch drain alone, which is above the 35 floor.\n\nA simpler fix is to abort any active syringe trip the instant a baton enters windup. The bot would then kill the baton and retry on the next safe beat. Windup occurs once every four beats, so it happens roughly once every 2.5 seconds. A syringe trip lasts about 1.5 seconds, meaning it could be interrupted by windup before it even finishes.\n\nA trip can fit in the 1.96-second non-windup window if it starts right after windup. Aborting mid-hold resets the IV hold, so the bot must return to heal. That may not always work within the gap. If it keeps aborting forever, vitals drain to zero and the bot dies. At 92 BPM the bar lasts 2.61 seconds.\n\nAnother approach is to gel scratches aggressively so the slow drain between downbeats is minimal, and kill every baton so no -6 strikes occur. The trace shows the -6 is the fatal blow; scratch drain alone is not enough to kill the bot. If all batons die, only scratch bleed remains, which is roughly two points per second. The bot should gel those five scratches at priority 84, but notes, bleeders, feeders, batons, and syringe trips currently outrank them. Five scratches can accumulate before the bot even reaches the IV.\n\nRaising scratch priority to 92 would let the bot gel them before notes and camping. Existing scratches from the first downbeat had already accumulated, since the bot was not in position at conductor start. The plan is to kill batons during windup to prevent new downbeat scratches, gel existing ones aggressively, and keep vitals around 45–50 with hush.\n\nScratch priority is set to 91. This outranks notes at 88 and feeders at 94, so the bot gels them before grabbing notes or camping. Core and windup remain at 97 and 96. During coda there are no feeders.\n\nGelling five scratches would take about five seconds, but notes burst into spores after only three. The bot should gel scratches only when vitals are below fifty and many are present; otherwise it must grab notes first. The immediate fix is to suppress syringe during windup beats. That change will be tested alone before raising scratch priority.\n\nThe bot is only 0.44 units from the coda at t=75.5, so it needs about 0.8 seconds to beam the baton during windup. That exceeds the 0.65-second window. Aborting the trip would not prevent the downbeat strike.\n\nThe bot camps at the coda because it is already next to the baton. From the IV it is too far away to beam the baton before the windup window closes. The fix is to avoid starting a syringe trip when the bot is too far from the coda to answer the next windup, or only syringe when vitals are critical and the bar is not in its second half.\n\nThe simplest robust fix is to restrict syringe trips to beats 2 and 3, immediately after the windup. The bot would abort if the beat reaches 4, since that is when the next downbeat strike lands. Beat 1 is already taken by the downbeat, and beat 3 is the note cue, so those beats are reserved for core or notes. This keeps the trip inside the safe window without needing to suppress syringing during windup.\n\nThe trace shows travel from the coda to the IV takes about 0.5 seconds, plus a 0.5-second hold, totaling roughly 1.0 second. That fits comfortably inside beats 2 and 3, which last about 1.24 seconds at this tempo. The plan is to pre-emptively syringe whenever vitals are below 55, the bar is on beat 2, and core is not active.\n\nAt low vitals the tempo can rise to 116 BPM, compressing beats to about 0.52 seconds. The safe window of beats two and three shrinks to roughly 1.04 seconds, which still fits a one-second trip.\n\nThe code currently exports `beat.count` rather than `beatInBar`. Computing the bar position from the raw beat count risks an off-by-one error.\n\nThe bot will read `beatInBar` from the exported state. The syringe gate is being drafted so trips only start on beats 2 or 3, or when the coda is not active.\n\nThe gate now combines the windup flag with the beat check. It blocks syringing during any windup and restricts coda trips to beats two and three. If a trip is already underway when the beat reaches four, the next decision aborts it and beams the baton instead.\n\nMissing the heal window once per bar is acceptable if the bot succeeds most bars. The plan is to measure first before raising scratch priority. Coda gel layers will be added for time, and fermata and legato will each get +2. Growth and adagio are already in the band, so they do not need changes. Layer adjustments will wait until after the coda vmin fix to avoid confounding results.\n\nFermata needs two layers to reach its target fraction. Legato needs one layer for margin. The coda gel zone will receive five layers to push total time from 170 seconds toward the 342-second target.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-9ad0aaba-62ab-447d-996f-c383d9857c09-66","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"beatInBar","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/sim.js"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1510,"output_tokens":4239,"cache_read_input_tokens":110976,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"8a20c6b3-ac84-4c61-8fc3-de2eb06c1fe4"}
