{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_188","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The coda moved to lung after HP dropped low enough. The death occurred later at 138 versus 114.\n\nScratch-first delayed healing too much. The healAt 64 policy survived, but adding a messy-syringe gate at 38 caused death. Gelling scratches while new ones kept coming let vitals fall to zero before the bot could heal. I need to keep the healAt 64 behavior and add something that only lifts the one dip.\n\nRestoring healAt 64 without the scratch gate will confirm the 170.8 / 32.5 baseline. A tiny change was considered: applying the stabilizer at the start of the syringe hold when vitals are below 36. That would raise the minimum to 34.8, still under 35. The 34.8 value was already the post-downbeat level at IV arrival.\n\nThe -6 drop happened mid-travel, not during the hold. Preventing the hold drain only saves 2.3 points, leaving a 0.2 point shortfall. The arrival vitals must be raised above 35.\n\nInstant heal on arrival plus one less scratch would raise the minimum to roughly 35.2. A downbeat of 6 points still leaves the floor at 32.5, so reducing it to 3 or 2 points barely clears 35.5 or 36.5. The better path is combining instant healing with fewer scratches. Another option is capping a single downbeat so vitals cannot drop below 36 from one hit, though that requires changing the hurt mechanic.\n\nA global coda cap was rejected as dishonest for a surgical game. The plan requires genuine vitals above 35, not hidden assists. The next step is to restore the exact working policy and add a trace that logs scratch count at the minimum vitals moment. Then gelling will be restricted to cases with four or more scratches and vitals above forty-five, while syringing continues as before otherwise.\n\nLimiting gelling to three or more scratches still leaves the floor at 34.3 or 34.9, which is too low. The only way to clear the dip is to keep the scratch count at zero during the trip. That means gelting every scratch before any syringe trip, while still syringing often enough to survive.\n\nThe previous gelling-first attempt died because downbeats kept spawning new scratches while the bot was off the baton, creating a spiral. To keep the scratch count at zero, the bot must kill every baton so no new ones spawn, then gel the existing one immediately and stay on the batons. Syringing should be limited to vitals below forty.\n\nThe healAt 32 test died at t=113 with high HP and active scratches. The beat-2 policy trace showed HP stuck at 300 because the bot remained at the IV. The healAt 32 run should have stayed and lasered, so the failure is puzzling.\n\nThe healAt 32 run never moved the coda. It barely damaged it at heart HP. The bot should have laserered the core instead of syringing. Windup and core overlap may have caused it to lasered batons instead. Core windows are short and the bot may have been out of position.\n\nThe healAt 32 failure may have been caused by the Hush beam not firing, leaving the bot unable to damage the coda despite its high BPM. I will inspect stepLaser and the coda damage multiplier next, but a structural fix is likely needed.\n\nThe coda fight is too lethal for the Normal bot. Legal tuning knobs include downbeat damage, scratch rate, and healer behavior. A new idea is to start the conductor with vitalsSet 70 instead of 88. The prior 88 run skipped Hush because the gift only fires under 50 vitals, and the unslowed boss killed the patient.\n\nA vitalsSet of 70 would still leave the bot above 50, so Hush would not fire until vitals drop below that threshold. The early downbeats would land at 64 and 58, but the floor of 32 came from a later trip when vitals were already low. Starting higher might delay the dip, or the bot could simply syringe less and die before Hush ever triggers. The summary already showed that 88 kills the patient, so 70 may behave similarly.\n\nA new idea is to give the conductor full composure at the start so Hush fires immediately, before the first downbeat. The bot already enters with vitals near 49, which is below the 50 threshold. The key question is whether downbeat damage counts as a timed event; if it does not, the -6 hit would still land at full strength despite the early Hush.\n\nDownbeat damage is not timed, so Hush does not reduce it. Scratches are reduced by Hush instead. If Hush were active for the entire fight, scratch drain would drop to a quarter and core windows would stay open longer in wall-clock time because the beat is slowed.\n\nThe bot checks its special gift immediately after syringe. If healAt 64 triggers a syringe first, the Hush beam never fires because the special is on cooldown. That explains why the working run never received early Hush protection.\n\nThe working run only received Hush at 76.5 seconds because the bot was always syringing, so the gift check never ran while composure was already at one hundred. The proposed fix is to prioritize the special gift over syringe when composure is one hundred and vitals are below fifty, letting Hush fire before any downbeat damage lands.\n\nThe fix is to check the special gift before the syringe when a coda is active. This lets Hush fire immediately instead of letting syringe trips steal the turn and miss batons.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-eef790c7-527b-4527-9165-ee88375b247c-85","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"special|hushPhase|composure","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/tools.js"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":802,"output_tokens":2735,"cache_read_input_tokens":138624,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"c3ca6709-1d73-42ee-b5cc-4d63538a87b2"}
