lpel* spd 0.26\\n nick development:bare t 54.03 tools scalpel* spd 0.22\\ncoda sloppy seats 1 FAIL:miss miss 12 t 71.0\\n{ 'development:bare': 12 }\\n(node:66910) Warning: The 'NO_COLOR' env is ignored due to the 'FORCE_COLOR' env being set.\\n(Use `node --trace-warnings ...` to show where the warning was created)\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"command\":\"node \\\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/tmp/grok-goal-c4cb33e83374/implementer/miss.mjs\\\" growth-spurt 1 sloppy; node \\\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/tmp/grok-goal-c4cb33e83374/implementer/miss.mjs\\\" grand-rounds 3 normal; node \\\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/tmp/grok-goal-c4cb33e83374/implementer/miss.mjs\\\" coda 1 sloppy\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Log whether nicks are bare or off-line\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6%2Fwork/01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710/terminal/call-9399416a-d414-4fdb-ba33-d9d3cffc7eb6-135.log\",\"total_bytes\":1379}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"01e9bb41-5ca2-457b-92ab-7b5026b910fe"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_218","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Two distinct bugs were identified. The first involves bare nicks during tether or link swipes when the swipe duration exceeds zero.\n\nThe first bug is a 4-second sloppy delay. The fix is to count being near a tether or link as onLine.\n\nThe second bug is line nicks in 3-player games. Two scalpels cut the same incision. One bot leaves the line at speed 0.90 and 0.45. The cut bot overshoots. This may be caused by aim assist from other entities, conflicting claims, or the third player changing guide points.\n\nThe claim logic appears correct: each incision gets only one scalpel. The line nick must therefore stem from something else, such as aim assist or conflicting bot positions.\n\nAim assist may pull the tip toward the nearest guide within 0.12. In multiplayer, extra guides like shards and bleeders could make the nearest target something other than the incision being cut. Solo games have the same guides but fewer bots, so the effect is weaker. Physics interactions between tips were ruled out.\n\nThe shared `along` and `started` state on the incision may cause the second bot to jump off the line when a second scalpel claims it. The logs show two scalpels at t=17.70, yet claims should prevent this. A gel bot does not use a scalpel. The core puzzle remains: why does a bot that cuts fine solo nick when another bot is present?\n\nThe other bot is not an entity, so it cannot affect aim assist. The line nick is per-entity in cutPoly. A solo GR miss of zero confirms the same bot path stays on the line.\n\nIn multiplayer, the second bot is assigned incision 2 from a bad starting position. The approach angle then leaves the line. Solo games avoid this because the first bot claims incision 1 first. The cut logic requires starting within 0.08 of the first point and following the curve. Solo succeeds at this even on tight curves.\n\nThe multi bot may start cutting from a worse angle because it was elsewhere, causing the first frames to drift off the line.\n\nThe fix is to make the cut more conservative: slow down or wait until the tip is back on the line before cutting. The nick occurs when cutting while the tip is slightly off the line, because cutPoly continues using the scalpel while steering toward the ahead point.\n\nThe default scalpel tolerance is 0.055, and the bot steers at 0.48 toward a point 0.12 ahead. On curves or off-start positions this drifts past the line. The fix should be applied inside cutPoly: do not activate the scalpel until the tip is very close to the polyline, and reduce speed if the distance from the line keeps increasing.\n\nA concrete fix is to shorten the lookahead to 0.06 and reduce speed to 0.32. This keeps the tip inside the 0.055 corridor. Solo clear times will increase slightly, which may push operations over their calibrated limits.\n\nSlowing the cut is safe. A few extra seconds won't push the padded time outside the 45–75% band.\n\nThe sloppy nicks are bare swipes during tumor swipes. They are not line nicks. The cut fix won't help here. The fix belongs in stepScalpel.\n\nThe fix for bare nicks in stepScalpel will treat a tether or link as onLine when the scalpel tip stays within 0.12 of the segment during a cut. The 0.12 threshold might be too tight because the swipe offset and noise could push the distance past that limit. A 0.2 radius is being considered instead.\n\nThe onLine threshold for tether and link sever attempts is set to 0.25 fu. The nicking bot moves slowly during the swipe, so distance alone does not prevent the nicks. The multi-player line nick in grand rounds still needs further investigation.\n\nI will log seat positions and incision state during line nicks to diagnose the multiplayer issue. First I will fix bare nicks and retest sloppy. Then I will tackle grand-round line nicks. I suspect the second bot may be cutting the same incision because its `along` value conflicts with the first bot's, causing `cutPoly` to see `!near` and nick.\n\nThe root cause of the multi-player line nick is now clear. stepScalpel runs cutPoly on every incision or circle on the site, not just the one the bot is assigned to. When bot B passes near incision 1 while cutting incision 2, its scalpel tip briefly strays off incision 1's line while still holding use above 0.5. The loop then sees !near on incision 1 and nicks it, even though bot B was correctly cutting incision 2.\n\nThe fix is to add a bare-nick guard in stepScalpel: if the scalpel is cutting but the tip is off the line and speed exceeds 0.15, it nicks. Inside cutPoly, if the tip is not near the polyline, it should immediately return false and only nick once. This confirms the multi-player line nick is a simulation bug where a bot cutting one incision on a site gets falsely nicked for being off another incision on that same site.\n\nSolo grand rounds have zero line nicks. This contradicts the theory that cutPoly checks every incision on a site. The bot must be exiting cutPoly before it can nick the second incision.\n\nThe `!e.started` guard in cutPoly prevents nicks while the incision is still unstarted. Once any bot starts it, other bots cutting elsewhere on that site and not near this incision will nick it. Solo games avoid this because only one bot starts the incision.\n\nThe fix is to restrict line nicks to the seat that actually cut the incision. The `e.nicking` and `e.started` flags are shared across all seats, so a bot that never touched an incision should not be nicked for leaving it. A seat that never came near an incision must not trigger its line nick.\n\nThe fix is to track a per-seat engagement flag. Only the seat that was actually on the incision should be allowed to nick it for leaving the line.\n\nThe per-seat engagement fix is being refined. A seat will track which incision it is actively cutting via `seat.onCut`. Only that seat may nick the line if it leaves while cutting. This prevents false nicks from other bots on the same site, though a bot might miss its own nick if another seat claims the incision first.\n\nThe per-seat engagement fix is now concrete. Each seat tracks its active incision in `seat.onCut` and a local `seat.nicking` flag. Only the seat that was actually on the line may nick it for leaving. Other seats cutting different incisions on the same site are no longer falsely nicked.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-06dedd7c-f63b-48b1-8f6b-df20b7efd53e-136","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/tools.js","limit":20}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":809,"output_tokens":3308,"cache_read_input_tokens":177152,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"7bb44356-ee8c-406d-9fb3-c8ab1b736c46"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-06dedd7c-f63b-48b1-8f6b-df20b7efd53e-136","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→import { award } from './scoring.js';\\nimport { DT, IV, angDiff, dist, overTray, polyLen, projectPoly, segHit } from './field.js';\\n\\nconst CELL = 0.04;\\n\\nfunction rising(seat, key) {\\n  return !!seat.cmd[key] && !seat.prev[key];\\n}\\n\\n10→function toolPower(sim) {\\n  let m = sim.mods.tool;\\n  if (sim.sforzandoT > 0) m *= sim.tuning.sforzandoToolMult;\\n  return m;\\n}\\n\\nfunction onBeat(sim, window) {\\n  const bpm = Math.max(20, sim.beat.bpm);\\n  const beatDur = 60 / bpm;\\n  const since = sim.beat.phase * beatDur;\\n20→  const until = (1 - sim.beat.phase) * beatDur;\",\"content_concise\":\"1→import { award } from './scoring.js';\\nimport { DT, IV, angDiff, dist, overTray, polyLen, projectPoly, segHit } from './field.js';\\n\\nconst CELL = 0.04;\\n\\nfunction rising(seat, key) {\\n  return !!seat.cmd[key] && !seat.prev[key];\\n}\\n\\n10→function toolPower(sim) {\\n  let m = sim.mods.tool;\\n  if (sim.sforzandoT > 0) m *= sim.tuning.sforzandoToolMult;\\n  return m;\\n}\\n\\nfunction onBeat(sim, window) {\\n  const bpm = Math.max(20, sim.beat.bpm);\\n  const beatDur = 60 / bpm;\\n  const since = sim.beat.phase * beatDur;\\n20→  const until = (1 - sim.beat.phase) * beatDur;\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/tools.js\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":20,\"raw_output\":\"import { award } from './scoring.js';\\nimport { DT, IV, angDiff, dist, overTray, polyLen, projectPoly, segHit } from './field.js';\\n\\nconst CELL = 0.04;\\n\\nfunction rising(seat, key) {\\n  return !!seat.cmd[key] && !seat.prev[key];\\n}\\n\\nfunction toolPower(sim) {\\n  let m = sim.mods.tool;\\n  if (sim.sforzandoT > 0) m *= sim.tuning.sforzandoToolMult;\\n  return m;\\n}\\n\\nfunction onBeat(sim, window) {\\n  const bpm = Math.max(20, sim.beat.bpm);\\n  const beatDur = 60 / bpm;\\n  const since = sim.beat.phase * beatDur;\\n  const until = (1 - sim.beat.phase) * beatDur;\\n\",\"total_lines\":792}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"fdcdda7f-1f8e-4d63-b802-2614010ef9ac"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_219","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need the distPointSeg function from field.js.\n\nI'll import distPointSeg. I'm implementing stepScalpel and cutPoly to handle incision and circleCut entities.\n\nTethers and links are severed when the scalpel is active and the seat is within 0.22 units. cutPoly now tracks a started flag for incisions, beginning only after the seat is within 0.08 units of the first point.\n\ncutPoly now nicking logic. A line nick fires only when the seat re-enters a line entity it previously touched. The lineNick flag resets on return so repeated drifts can nick again. This preserves existing sensitivity and edge-case behavior.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-80e9b682-0554-4fe4-85c9-af26e269dc43-137","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/tools.js","old_string":"import { DT, IV, angDiff, dist, overTray, polyLen, projectPoly, segHit } from './field.js';","new_string":"import { DT, IV, angDiff, dist, distPointSeg, overTray, polyLen, projectPoly, segHit } from './field.js';"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-80e9b682-0554-4fe4-85c9-af26e269dc43-138","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/tools.js","old_string":"    if (e.kind === 'incision' || e.kind === 'circleCut') onLine = cutPoly(sim, seat, e, dt, cutting, tol) || onLine;\n    if ((e.kind === 'tether' || e.kind === 'link') && cutting) trySever(sim, seat, e);","new_string":"    if (e.kind === 'incision' || e.kind === 'circleCut') onLine = cutPoly(sim, seat, e, dt, cutting, tol) || onLine;\n    if ((e.kind === 'tether' || e.kind