8,32,48,44,32,122,58,32,48,32,125,44,32,123,32,120,58,32,48,44,32,121,58,32,48,44,32,122,58,32,48,32,125,41,59,32,32,32,47,47,32,99,104,97,105,110,115,58,32,76,101,103,97,116,111,44,32,98,97,116,111,110,115,44,32,116,104,114,101,97,100,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":8,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/PLAN.md\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":251,\"content\":\"| **Legato** | *legato (adj.): smoothly connected, without a break. It does not want a break.* | Bead chain joined by curved slur ribbons, on Rapier rope joints, head pinned | Must be cut **tail first**; the active link glows. Wrong link: two links re-heal, vitals -4. Cut pieces reattach after 8 beats unless carried to the tray. Every 8 beats it jerks and opens a bleeder | Scalpel sever, then Forceps to tray | Gliding portamento lead |\"},{\"line_number\":300,\"content\":\"### Op 5: Legato (Chapter 3, **Ines**). Brain. Timer 6:00, 70 bpm. Consult\"},{\"line_number\":304,\"content\":\"2. **Legato:** 12 links, tail first. Forceps each piece away.\"},{\"line_number\":323,\"content\":\"2. **Development (lung, breathing field):** Tremolo and a Legato chain. Bleeders open back at the heart: site-switch alarm, teaching L1/R1.\"},{\"line_number\":338,\"content\":\"3. **2:15, Legato and Crescendo:** a chain on the bowel, a ring on the heart.\"},{\"line_number\":574,\"content\":\"| Strains (skinned) | Adagio `crawl` and `bite`, Tremolo `shiver` and `flee`, Legato `slither` and `jerk`, Crescendo `swell`, Fermata `open` and `close`, Coda `conduct`, `downbeat`, `cue`, `rest` |\"},{\"line_number\":632,\"content\":\"- **Loop (about 3 minutes):** a story snippet (Bea and Ren, S1 lines) → Op 1 shards and stitching → Op 2 incision and clamps → the Hush moment → Adagio laser and forceps → Staccato swarm → Tremolo → Legato with Sforzando → Crescendo and a Fermata Defib → Coda with site switching → rank stamp → logo → repeat.\"},{\"line_number\":1078,\"content\":\"const link = RAPIER.JointData.rope(0.1, { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 }, { x: 0, y: 0, z: 0 });   // chains: Legato, batons, thread\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-ce28ef81-8687-495d-94aa-e41b868ed010-35","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,51,49,50,51,48,95,95,120,56,102,52,57,98,54,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,49,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,46,103,114,111,107,47,115,101,115,115,105,111,110,115,47,37,50,70,104,111,109,101,37,50,70,109,105,99,104,97,37,50,70,100,101,118,37,50,70,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,37,50,70,114,117,110,115,37,50,70,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,51,49,50,51,48,95,95,120,56,102,52,57,98,54,37,50,70,119,111,114,107,47,48,49,97,49,97,97,53,54,45,54,50,51,49,45,55,99,102,48,45,97,54,54,54,45,100,102,53,102,98,98,48,57,48,55,49,48,47,103,111,97,108,47,112,108,97,110,46,109,100,10,50,54,58,45,32,70,80,83,32,116,97,114,103,101,116,115,44,32,102,114,97,109,101,45,116,105,109,101,32,111,112,116,105,109,105,122,97,116,105,111,110,44,32,111,114,32,108,111,119,101,114,105,110,103,32,115,99,101,110,101,32,100,101,116,97,105,108,32,116,111,32,99,104,97,115,101,32,112,101,114,102,111,114,109,97,110,99,101,46,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":1,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6%2Fwork/01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710/goal/plan.md\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":26,\"content\":\"- FPS targets, frame-time optimization, or lowering scene detail to chase performance.\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"daad49ea-38d7-462c-9dff-555142f4c204"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_262","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The grep for the goal plan seems off. The file may be one long line, or the path is wrong.\n\nReheal must undo the last two untrayed cuts and restore the active tail. The correct active index is being determined.\n\nThe active link is the highest index that is not yet cut. Multiple pieces can be cut and waiting before any are trayed.\n\nA reverse scan is needed to find the active link. The `reheal` function will undo the last two untrayed cuts and set active to the highest remaining index. If all links are cut, active stays at zero. `trySever` also risks skipping a trayed link or landing on an already-cut one.\n\nSetting active to the highest uncut index after severing the tail would self-heal a corrupted state. The normal bot never triggers this because it never hits wrong links.\n\nThe sloppy bot's active index got stuck at zero because the broken `reheal` left it there. It then cut and trayed link zero, so active never advanced. Fixing `reheal` to point to the highest uncut index would prevent this trap. The bot would sever the real tail instead of the wrong link.\n\n`trySever` should also search backward for the next uncut link instead of decrementing. This self-heals a corrupted active index. The normal bot still cuts in order, so it behaves identically.\n\nThe backward scan matches the old decrement when active is at zero, so normal scores stay unchanged. When all links are cut, active becomes zero and the chain dies naturally because no swipe candidate remains. Next, I will investigate why the sloppy bot cuts the wrong link.\n\nThe sloppy bot severs the wrong link because its short, delayed swipe can cross a neighboring segment. With speed floor 0.15, any segment touched by the stroke is cut, so a miss occurs if the scalpel path hits a neighbor instead of the active link.\n\nThe 0.05 swipe offset is smaller than the roughly 0.08 segment spacing, so a delayed hand drifts onto a neighbor. Restoring the correct tail should let the bot retry and keep misses under the 12 cap.\n\nThe grab lead of 0.34 risks pressing too early. The trace shows forceps at t=183.9 with speed zero and distance near 0.100, right at the grab radius boundary. Grabs sometimes succeed and sometimes fail depending on exact position.\n\nThe freeze was caused by the active pointer stuck at zero after traying the head, not by a grab failure. A near-0.100 press with speed zero and use 1 means the forceps pressed on or just outside the grab radius without actually grabbing, so the cut stayed active. This confirms the grab-radius miss is real but secondary; the stuck active index is the main problem. I will fix `reheal` and `trySever` to restore the correct tail, then retest sloppy legato and verify normal legato scores remain unchanged. I will leave the swipe length untouched unless the fix proves insufficient.\n\nThe coda development legato chain is also stalled by the same bug, so fixing the active pointer will help both. GR 2 already passes, but GR 1, 3, and 4 still need re-testing after the change. Normal legato scores should remain identical because normal bots never trigger the broken path. The grab lead stays at 0.04.\n\nThe `nextActive` helper scans backward to find the highest uncut link. `reheal` now undoes the last two untrayed cuts and sets active via this helper. `trySever` also uses it after cutting a link. The severed link is excluded from the scan, so the new active index correctly points to the next uncut segment.\n\n`nextActive` must run after `link.cut` is set, or it would return the same link. Trayed links are already cut, so they are correctly skipped. The backward scan preserves the existing decrement behavior for higher indices. In `stepLegato`, reattaching a piece sets `e.active = link.index`, which is correct because the reattached tail piece should be the next target.\n\nThe reattach logic is sound: only the highest uncut link can be cut, so the oldest cut piece is always the highest index and reattaching it correctly restores active. I will apply the fix and retest sloppy legato, sloppy coda, and normal legato to confirm scores are unchanged.\n\nThe 0.100–0.106 grab attempts with zero speed and use 1 are the lead undershoot I calculated, not a grab-radius miss. The trace shows three pieces were successfully trayed, so grabs work when the forceps are close enough. If the active fix still leaves the bot stuck near 0.100, the grab lead may need tightening.\n\nThe grab lead of 0.34 is too long. The trace shows the bot stopped at distance 0.10, meaning travel during delay consumed about 0.24 of that lead. To land at 0.04, the lead should be closer to 0.28. A shorter lead would move the press point inward and away from the grab-radius boundary.\n\nA lead of 0.265 would still overshoot by roughly 0.24, so the target is closer to 0.28. Approach speed is not constant, which complicates the exact timing.\n\nA shorter grab lead would keep steering longer, but the tip slows as it nears the target, so travel during the delay shrinks nonlinearly. A better approach is to steer while distance exceeds 0.07 and press once it drops to 0.07 or below; the delayed press must still land within the 0.1 grab radius.\n\nA fixed grab lead of 0.22 would keep the bot moving until it is much closer to the target, preventing it from stopping at 0.10 and pressing outside the grab radius. A cleaner approach would avoid a fixed lead entirely.\n\nThe grab logic will be simplified to press while still steering whenever distance is below 0.18, and only stop completely once distance drops below 0.05. This ensures the delayed grip lands closer to the target, since velocity is not available in state.\n\nThe grab logic now uses a three-tier distance threshold: full steering below 0.22, reduced steering below 0.06, and a delayed press once inside 0.04. For zero delay this matches the original code exactly, so normal scores stay unchanged. With a reaction delay the bot presses while still moving, ensuring the delayed grip lands within the grab radius.\n\nA risk remains that pressing while moving could grab a nearby cut link or note, but that is acceptable. The plan is to apply both fixes together and then retest sloppy legato, sloppy coda, and normal legato. Normal legato must still score 29795. I will also check the swipe logic for cutting the wrong link.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The chain pointer jumps to the head after a wrong cut, so the bot trays that link and then freezes. I'll point the active link back at the tail and retest."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-883b14b0-4ab0-4955-b277-ac95a5343313-36","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/tools.js","old_string":"    if (chain) chain.active = Math.max(0, chain.active - 1);\n  }\n}\n\nfunction reheal(chain) {\n  const cut = chain.links.filter((l) => l.cut && !l.tray);\n  const back = cut.slice(-2);\n  for (const l of back) {\n    l.cut = false;\n    l.free = false;\n  }\n  chain.active = chain.links.findIndex((l) => !l.cut);\n  if (chain.active < 0) chain.active = 0;\n}","new_string":"    if (chain) chain.active = nextOpenLink(chain);\n  }\n}\n\n/** Tail-first: the active link is the highest index that is not yet cut. */\nfunction nextOpenLink(chain) {\n  for (let i = chain.links.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n    if (!chain.links[i].cut) return i;\n  }\n  return 0;\n}\n\nfunction reheal(chain) {\n  const cut = chain.links.filter((l) => l.cut && !l.tray);\n  const back = cut.slice(-2);\n  for (const l of back) {\n    l.cut = false;\n    l.free = false;\n  }\n  chain.active = nextOpenLink(chain);\n}"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-883b14b0-4ab0-4955-b277-ac95a5343313-37","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/bot.js","old_string":"function grab(me, e, tuning, opts) {\n  const d = Math.hypot(me.x - e.x, me.y - e.y);\n  const lead = 0.04 + (opts.reactionDelay || 0) * 1.2;\n  if (d > lead) return goTool(steer(me, e.x, e.y, 0.85, tuning, opts), 'forceps', 0);\n  return hold('forceps', 1);\n}","new_string":"function grab(me, e, tuning, opts) {\n  const d = Math.hypot(me.x - e.x, me.y - e.y);\n  const delay = opts.reactionDelay || 0;\n  if (delay > 0) {\n    // The grip is queued. Press while still closing so it lands inside the 0.1 radius.\n    if (d > 0.22) return goTool(steer(me, e.x, e.y, 0.85, tuning, opts), 'forceps', 0);\n    if (d > 0.06) return goTool(steer(me, e.x, e.y, 0.55, tuning, opts), 'forceps', 1);\n    return hold('forceps', 1);\n  }\n  if (d > 0.04) return goTool(steer(me, e.x, e.y, 0.85, tuning, opts), 'forceps', 0);\n  return hold('forceps', 1);\n}"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1067,"output_tokens":4323,"cache_read_input_tokens":81280,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"9cc552bc-f983-4efe-a8d7-751de52a711c"}
