2,32,32,32,105,102,32,40,119,104,101,110,46,98,97,114,32,33,61,32,110,117,108,108,32,38,38,32,116,104,105,115,46,98,101,97,116,46,98,97,114,32,62,61,32,119,104,101,110,46,98,97,114,41,32,114,101,116,117,114,110,32,116,114,117,101,59,10,55,57,56,58,32,32,32,32,98,101,97,116,73,110,66,97,114,58,32,116,104,105,115,46,98,101,97,116,46,99,111,117,110,116,32,62,32,48,32,63,32,40,40,116,104,105,115,46,98,101,97,116,46,99,111,117,110,116,32,45,32,49,41,32,37,32,52,41,32,43,32,49,32,58,32,48,44,10,56,48,48,58,32,32,32,32,98,97,114,58,32,116,104,105,115,46,98,101,97,116,46,98,97,114,44,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":6,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/sim.js\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":195,\"content\":\"      const beatInBar = ((this.beat.count - 1) % 4) + 1;\"},{\"line_number\":196,\"content\":\"      if (beatInBar === 1) this.beat.bar += 1;\"},{\"line_number\":197,\"content\":\"      this.beatEvents.push({ beat: this.beat.count, beatInBar, bar: this.beat.bar });\"},{\"line_number\":257,\"content\":\"    if (when.bar != null && this.beat.bar >= when.bar) return true;\"},{\"line_number\":798,\"content\":\"    beatInBar: this.beat.count > 0 ? ((this.beat.count - 1) % 4) + 1 : 0,\"},{\"line_number\":800,\"content\":\"    bar: this.beat.bar,\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"4f36c131-0d1e-41db-8e34-2f377d98288e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_297","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The bar increments on every beatInBar === 1.\n\nCore opens every 16 beats at 92 bpm, roughly every 10.4 seconds. It stays open for about two beats. The first opening is delayed during hush because the beat scale drops to 0.25.\n\nEach core opening deals roughly 117 HP at full damage. Three openings would kill the player, so survival requires avoiding every downbeat. The trace shows the player missed all six downbeats in eighteen seconds and died at two HP while running to the IV.\n\nCommitting to one baton kills the player in about 0.3 seconds, but that is acceptable because it stops downbeats and allows survival through core openings. Blocking downbeats keeps vitality near thirty, so the healAt sixteen trigger never fires. At t=110 the gel targets a scratch instead of the wounded baton; the baton had two HP but the windup ended, and damage resets to nine after beat one.\n\nPartial damage is wiped every downbeat, so the player must kill within a single windup beat. Switching bats loses all progress. The bot should pick one baton and beam it for the entire beat four window. The early-fire and delay may miscenter the laser. Two HP remaining equals only 0.067 seconds of damage, which is almost enough to finish the kill. Splitting the window between two bats is likely why the bot only reaches two HP.\n\nThe trace shows the laser position shifting from the right baton to center and then to the left baton. Both bats have equal priority, so the switch is driven by distance. The bot moved toward the right baton, then centered, then switched to the left. This confirms the laser splits its damage window between two bats instead of committing to one.\n\nThe two HP remaining is a single window shortfall, not damage split across bats. The reaction delay consumes the first 0.25 seconds of the 0.65 beat, leaving only 0.40 seconds of effective laser time. That yields about twelve HP, which should be enough to finish a nine-HP baton.\n\nThe laser may not hit for the full 0.40 seconds. Tip drift from ±0.03 stick noise is below the 0.12 deadzone, so it does not displace the tip during a hold command. A steer command adds only 0.03 noise, which should still land. Other factors like use or power reduction could also reduce damage.\n\nAt t=116.6 the laser tip sits at 0.23, 0.04 while the right baton is at 0.28, 0.05. That distance is 0.051, just outside the 0.05 hit radius. The tip never lands inside the radius, so it only grazes and cannot finish the kill. Aim assist may be pulling the hold position away from the baton center.\n\nThe beam command steers at 0.7 use 1 until the distance drops to 0.04, then holds use 1. The hold is delayed by 0.25 seconds, so the tip drifts during that window. If the steer frames are slow, the tip stops exactly 0.05 away from the baton center, landing just outside the 0.05 hit radius and missing the kill.\n\nThe beat three approach command steers at 0.9 when the distance exceeds 0.2, then drops to 0.2 once the bot is close. The slowdown radius is only 0.074, so the tip should stop within 0.02 of the baton center. The observed trace still shows a consistent 0.05 gap, meaning the delayed stop command arrives too late and the tip overshoots.\n\nAim assist pulls toward guides like incisions and wounds rather than batons, which could push the tip away from the target. The plan is to adjust the delayed beam so it drives directly onto the baton center and holds use 1 within 0.08 distance. Alternatively, the hold radius should be widened to account for lag so the resting point remains centered.\n\nThe delayed stop command arrives when the tip is still far from the baton, so it cannot prevent an overshoot. The new plan is to keep steering at a minimum speed until the distance drops below 0.02, rather than issuing a zero-stick command too early. A draft of the updated beam function uses a dynamic steer multiplier and a tighter hold radius.\n\nA small aim lead was considered but set aside because velocity is unavailable. The revised plan lowers the delayed beam hold threshold to 0.015 and raises the close-in speed so the tip no longer crawls. The hit radius cannot be changed without affecting normal play. The new draft uses a 0.9 steer multiplier when distance exceeds 0.12, a 0.55 multiplier within 0.12, and holds use 1 once the distance drops below 0.02.\n\nThe steer function returns zero stick when the desired speed drops below 0.13, which would park the tip outside the hit radius before the delayed hold arrives. However, the slowdown floor is 0.08, so the tip should still move until distance falls below 0.012. The earlier assumption that steer stops too early appears incorrect.\n\nThe beat three approach speed of 0.2 is too slow for the delayed bot. At that speed the tip needs one second to reach the baton, but the beat window is only 0.65 seconds. The approach never finishes before the windup ends, so the beam must start from a farther distance and cannot land inside the hit radius.\n\nThe approach function will use a 1.0 steer multiplier when distance exceeds 0.15 and 0.6 within 0.15, dropping to 0.9 or 0.2 for normal play. The beam will keep a higher close speed so the tip reaches the target before the reaction delay expires. The bot will commit to one baton only. The sloppy coda syringe threshold will be raised to prevent crashes from killing bats. Beat three priority is confirmed at 93, above the 84 scratch priority.\n\nThe gel trace at t=110 is just post-downbeat cleaning, so it is harmless. The plan now includes adding baton stickiness during the windup and approach window to prevent the bot from switching targets mid-beat. Because the candidate list lacks memory, the stickiness check will be handled in the decide function before the candidate list is built.\n\nThe decide function now locks the bot to one baton by checking memory for a stored batonId. If that baton is dead or missing, it sorts the remaining bats by distance and assigns the nearest one. The bot then either beams or approaches that same target for the rest of the beat.\n\nThe baton stickiness check runs after the syringe and hush logic, so the bot still syringes when health is low. Core damage is avoided because the core closes by beat three. Returning early from this block skips scratch tasks during the baton window. The normal delay-zero path also uses this stickiness, but it should not affect normal coda scores since the normal bot already kills bats.\n\nThe baton stickiness and faster approach will be gated behind the `slow` flag so normal play stays byte-identical. The `slow` variable is already defined before the candidate list, so the new block can reference it directly.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-cc2810e3-24c2-4e0d-bfc0-68dda69f78cf-79","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/bot.js","old_string":"function approach(me, e, tuning, opts) {\n  const d = Math.hypot(me.x - e.x, me.y - e.y);\n  const f = steer(me, e.x, e.y, d > 0.2 ? 0.9 : 0.2, tuning, opts);\n  return goTool(f, 'laser', 0);\n}","new_string":"function approach(me, e, tuning, opts) {\n  const d = Math.hypot(me.x - e.x, me.y - e.y);\n  const delay = opts.reactionDelay || 0;\n  // The wind-up is one beat. A delayed hand has to be on the baton before it starts.\n  const want = delay > 0 ? (d > 0.12 ? 1.05 : 0.65) : (d > 0.2 ? 0.9 : 0.2);\n  const f = steer(me, e.x, e.y, want, tuning, opts);\n  return goTool(f, 'laser', 0);\n}"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-cc2810e3-24c2-4e0d-bfc0-68dda69f78cf-80","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/bot.js","old_string":"  if (delay > 0) {\n    // Fire on the way in. The queued beam then meets the target instead of starting late.\n    if (d > 0.18) return goTool(steer(me, e.x, e.y, 0.95, tuning, opts), 'laser', 0);\n    if (d > 0.04) return goTool(steer(me, e.x, e.y, 0.7, tuning, opts), 'laser', 1);\n    return hold('laser', 1);\n  }","new_string":"  if (delay > 0) {\n    // Park inside the 0.05 hit radius. A slower close was stopping on the edge.\n    if (d > 0.1) return goTool(steer(me, e.x, e.y, d > 0.25 ? 1.05 : 0.75, tuning, opts), 'laser', d < 0.14 ? 1 : 0);\n    return hold('laser', 1);\n  }"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-cc2810e3-24c2-4e0d-bfc0-68dda69f78cf-81","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/sim/bot.js","old_string":"  const list = candidates(state, me, opts).filter((c) => !claims.has(c.e.id));","new_string":"  if (slow && codaUp && !coreNow && (state.beatInBar === 3 || state.beatInBar === 4)) {\n    const bats = state.entities.filter((e) => e.kind === 'baton' && e.hp > 0);\n    if (bats.length) {\n      let bat = bats.find((b) => b.id === mem.batonId);\n      if (!bat) {\n        bat = bats.slice().sort((a, b) => {\n          const da = Math.hypot(a.x - me.x, a.y - me.y) + (a.site === me.site ? 0 : 3);\n          const db = Math.hypot(b.x - me.x, b.y - me.y) + (b.site === me.site ? 0 : 3);\n          return da - db;\n        })[0];\n        mem.batonId = bat.id;\n      }\n      return actOn(state, me, state.beatInBar === 3 && !bat.windup ? 'approach' : 'beam', bat, mem, tuning, opts);\n    }\n  } else if (state.beatInBar === 1 || state.beatInBar === 2) {\n    mem.batonId = 0;\n  }\n\n  const list = candidates(state, me, opts).filter((c) => !claims.has(c.e.id));"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1805,"output_tokens":4631,"cache_read_input_tokens":141056,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"e53c8e15-1916-48c8-a39d-bc5d65f3ee36"}
