rains(this, dt);\"},{\"line_number\":185,\"content\":\"  advanceBeat(dt) {\"},{\"line_number\":187,\"content\":\"    const gap = target - this.beat.bpm;\"},{\"line_number\":188,\"content\":\"    this.beat.bpm += gap * Math.min(1, dt / 1);\"},{\"line_number\":189,\"content\":\"    this.beatEvents = [];\"},{\"line_number\":190,\"content\":\"    const scale = this.beatScale();\"},{\"line_number\":191,\"content\":\"    this.beat.phase += dt * scale * this.beat.bpm / 60;\"},{\"line_number\":192,\"content\":\"    while (this.beat.phase >= 1) {\"},{\"line_number\":193,\"content\":\"      this.beat.phase -= 1;\"},{\"line_number\":194,\"content\":\"      this.beat.count += 1;\"},{\"line_number\":195,\"content\":\"      const beatInBar = ((this.beat.count - 1) % 4) + 1;\"},{\"line_number\":196,\"content\":\"      if (beatInBar === 1) this.beat.bar += 1;\"},{\"line_number\":197,\"content\":\"      this.beatEvents.push({ beat: this.beat.count, beatInBar, bar: this.beat.bar });\"},{\"line_number\":256,\"content\":\"    if (when.beat != null && this.beat.count >= when.beat) return true;\"},{\"line_number\":257,\"content\":\"    if (when.bar != null && this.beat.bar >= when.bar) return true;\"},{\"line_number\":466,\"content\":\"        extra.born = spec.born ?? this.beat.count;\"},{\"line_number\":796,\"content\":\"    bpm: num(this.beat.bpm),\"},{\"line_number\":797,\"content\":\"    beat: this.beat.count,\"},{\"line_number\":798,\"content\":\"    beatInBar: this.beat.count > 0 ? ((this.beat.count - 1) % 4) + 1 : 0,\"},{\"line_number\":799,\"content\":\"    beatPhase: num(this.beat.phase),\"},{\"line_number\":800,\"content\":\"    bar: this.beat.bar,\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"06c1ec92-f8d2-485d-bd3d-cb93ebb06bd3"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_354","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"advanceBeat runs before stepStrains.\n\nNotes spawn at count=N and step on the next frame. They burst after exactly 5 beats. At 92 BPM that is about 3.26 seconds. The singing window requires 0.25 seconds of laser while a primary hit stays within 0.14 of the note.\n\nThe 0.3-second beam at beat 1 overlaps the note's lifetime. The note spawns at beat 3 and bursts at beat 4, so the beam window covers beat 1 where the note is alive.\n\nThe baton tip is 0.044 to 0.144 units from the note center. The 0.14 hit radius is breached only in the worst-case position. The beamBaton threshold of 0.025 sits well inside that range.\n\nThe tip-to-note distance stays between 0.069 and 0.119 units, safely inside the 0.14 hit radius.\n\nAfter the move, the baton sits at coda.y while the note is at coda.y+0.2, a distance of only 0.08 units.\n\nBeaming on the dead +x baton while HP remains above zero should singe every note during the 0.3-second kill window after each beat 1.\n\nHowever, the core opens every 16 beats (4 bars). On those bars the prelude lands at beat 4, so the baton is already parked on the core when the note spawns at beat 3. The laser cannot reach the note in that case.\n\nThe prelude note bursts without ever being singed. The only beam window is the core opening at beat 1, but the baton resets there and does not fire. This happens once every four bars, roughly once per ten seconds. That yields about 0.18 spores per second plus a burst damage of 1.2. It is far more survivable than eight bursts.\n\nDuring non-opening cycles the note is singed two beats after it spawns, well inside its five-beat lifetime.\n\nDuring core openings the dead baton blocks the downbeat damage. HP resets to nine while the player remains at the core, and the next downbeat is four beats later. The timeline of arms versus downbeats is being checked.\n\nThe downbeat check runs before the HP reset. The arm is already dead when the beat 1 check occurs, so the downbeat is blocked. The 0.3-second kill leaves the arm dead for the next three beats, and the following beat 1 sees zero HP and blocks again. A full beat of grace time is available.\n\nThe core timer decrements immediately during the same stepCoda call that opens it. It drops from 3 to 2 on beat 1, then to 1 on beat 2, and closes at the start of beat 3.\n\nThe baton returns to beat 3 with nine HP. The freshly reset arm is killed during that beat. The note spawned there is singed immediately. The next downbeat remains blocked. The only bursted note is the one abandoned on the prelude bar.\n\nThe conductor has 255 HP. Without hush, each core opening lasts about 10.4 seconds. During hush the beat clock slows to 0.25x speed, so a 16-beat cycle would take roughly 41.7 seconds of wall time, though hush itself only lasts seven seconds. The measured damage per full opening is -58.\n\nThe measured -58 damage assumes the baton tip was already in place. The full cycle lasts about 42 seconds of wall time plus travel between the two site moves. Vitals start at 34.7 and drain at roughly 0.72 per second from four spores. Downbeats are blocked, so no additional scratch damage occurs.\n\nTotal damage over forty seconds is roughly nineteen. Vitals start at thirty-five and end near sixteen, so a syringe near the end should keep the bot alive. Hush begins immediately because vitals are thirty-five and composure is one hundred. The previous failure happened because the baton was left on the core.\n\nThe prior failure saw eight bursts in twenty-five seconds because the baton was left on the core. Staying on the active baton should pop most notes before they expire, leaving only the prelude-cycle bursts. The plan is to implement this and measure. When HP is at or below zero, park with laser off; otherwise beam the baton.\n\nWhen the remembered baton is missing, the bot selects a new one from the live pool and prefers the +x side. The code filters for alive bats, falls back to all bats if none are healthy, sorts the pool, and stores the chosen baton ID.\n\nThe code branches on baton health: zero or less triggers parkOn, otherwise beamBaton. Dead bats are kept in the pool but ignored during fresh selection because new bats spawn at full HP while old ones become dead. After a move, if the next baton sits on a different site, the bot switches sites.\n\nThe prelude on beat 4 of every third bar intentionally leaves the dead baton for the core. If the coda moves during that opening, old bats die and new ones spawn; the stale baton ID is then discarded and a fresh +x baton is chosen. The delay-0 path is gated by `if (slow && ...)`, so normal bots remain unaffected and the coda score should stay at 37227.\n\nA subtle bug was spotted: if the bot parks on a dead baton, a note within 0.05 of the tip might be hit as a primary target by stepLaser because notes carry HP. Dead bats remain stationary. The next step is to apply the edit and run tests.\n\nThe suspected primary-hit bug is resolved. A note at 0.094 units from the baton tip sits outside the 0.05 radius, so it cannot be targeted as a primary hit while the laser is off. When beaming a living baton, the baton itself is closer than the note and becomes the primary target, allowing side-burn to singe the note instead. Scratches spawn at the coda center rather than the baton, so they do not interfere with targeting either. The edit appears sound.\n\nA 0.25-second reaction delay means the laser stays armed for 0.25 seconds after the baton dies. Because dead bats are not valid targets, the off-time accumulates during that window. That accumulated off-time might reach the 0.25-second scorch threshold and trigger a scorch.\n\nThe 0.25-second scorch risk is resolved. When the laser is turned off, the use threshold resets laserTime to zero and returns early, so the off-time accumulator never reaches the scorch threshold. The accumulated off-time would be negligible even if it did.\n\nThe 0.25-second reaction delay equals exactly 15 frames, so frame alignment is not a concern. Keeping the dead baton as a heat sink was rejected to avoid altering the normal bot's behavior. Widening side-burn to work without a primary hit while parked near a dead baton was also considered.\n\nWidening side-burn to work while the laser is off would pop the prelude note on beat 3, since that note spawns before the bot leaves the dead baton. The alternative of treating a dead baton as a heat sink was also considered, but that would require a simulation change.\n\nTreating a dead baton as a heat sink would let the bot keep lasering it for the full three beats. Notes would pop during beats 2 through 4, including the prelude note on beat 3, so no bursts would occur. The 0.25-second reaction tail would not scorch. It is unclear whether the normal delay-0 bot ever laser dead bats.\n\nTreating dead bats as heat sinks would alter the normal coda score. The prior side-burn at 0.14 did not change the score, so normal laser hits likely never had extra notes in range. Adding heat-sink behavior would only affect the score if dead bats were already being hit as primary targets.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-2629c158-150b-4abc-b6fb-44cdbf8cb9a8-13","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"hurt\\(","glob":"**/sim.js"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":852,"output_tokens":4385,"cache_read_input_tokens":46464,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"c0da651f-b43b-40d7-bb36-f690cbfc4d03"}
