{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_399","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'll write a compact snapshot module and measure it.\n\nI'm now planning the Three.js visual scene with WebGPURenderer, MeshToonNodeMaterial, and a real ink filter. It will render organs, instruments, strains, and a full HUD. I'll write the remaining game files in batches.\n\nThe snapshot format will use short JSON keys. It stores tick, vitals, timer, beat, phase, hush code, arrest clock, tips, and moving entities. Seat data should stay under 160 bytes. Moving entities could reach roughly 600 bytes. The full keyframe includes all entities but strips long arrays like wound gates.\n\nThe full keyframe could reach 8–20 KB, so I'll switch to a packed array format with single-character kind codes and bitset flags. I'll measure the actual size by running the four-player GR simulation to its busiest point.\n\nDecoded snapshots must match the shape of `sim.state` so the view can read them directly without branching. The `s` message will be a delta applied to the last keyframe. I'll verify that the round-trip preserves all view data and that the snapshot stays under 1,100 bytes during the busiest four-seat Grand Rounds.\n\nI'll define a `VIEW_KEYS` list to assert that every field the view reads is present after decoding. The snapshot module will export `keyframe`, `unreliable`, and `decode` functions, with tuning stripped from the packed state. I'm also deciding whether to measure message size with `JSON.stringify` or `TextEncoder`.\n\nI've mapped the exact entity fields the view needs, including position, health, flags, geometry arrays, and seat color. Points and gates are the heaviest fields; a ten-point incision alone consumes roughly 150 bytes. I'll use JSON.stringify to measure UTF-8 byte length.\n\nKeyframe coordinates will be quantized to two decimals and null fields omitted. A script will measure raw state size before writing the story and snapshot modules. PROGRESS.md and deviations need updating, and the simulation API surface must be checked first. The story file will contain 250–350 dialogue objects across scenes S0 through S16, with must-have lines kept verbatim.\n\nBea only says \"good\" in S16. The story will be an array of 17 scenes, each with a chapter title and lines keyed by speaker and mood. I'm targeting roughly 306 total lines across the set. Specific must-have quotes are being pulled from the table for S1 and S2.\n\nThe remaining must-have quotes for scenes S4 through S13 are now being extracted from the table.\n\nThe remaining must-have quotes for scenes S14 through S16 are now extracted. I'll write barks.js and scenes.js next. The Three.js field renderer will use THREE.MeshToonNodeMaterial from three/webgpu.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-871217bf-96e4-4487-a2de-279539af6572-84","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"\"addons\"","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/node_modules/three/package.json"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-871217bf-96e4-4487-a2de-279539af6572-85","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/PROGRESS.md","limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1742,"output_tokens":1802,"cache_read_input_tokens":148736,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"7f6960c9-68c4-4c21-a9c3-b1164f916b93"}
