{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_563","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I have a clear picture. I will write director.js and main.js comprehensively.\n\nNext up are CSS additions, build, and tests. Key design decisions include the createApp API, pending keyboard edges, a 10-second attract wall clock, story flow, and state fields. I also need to check rank shapes from scoring.js, event types for audio mapping, and how to edge siteDelta since tools.js treats it as held every frame.\n\nDirector must edge siteDelta on rising edges for both keyboard and pad inputs before passing them to sim.input. test.step polls synchronously, steps the simulation, and runs shell post-step logic; in test mode the animation frame loop must not poll during this cycle. On the title screen any key sets a pending confirm that stepOnce consumes to enter the menu. The standalone hub check uses Enter to reach the main menu and Space to confirm the first story item.\n\nThe standalone hub check presses keys without calling test.step. The animation frame loop must poll and step in non-test mode. Tests use test.step instead.\n\nPlaywright releases keys before test.step, so the poll sees no held input and only the pending edge fires. This prevents double confirmation.\n\nA real-play bug was found: if a key is held, the next rAF poll sees the key still down and risingMenu would double-activate alongside the pending edge. The fix is to have stepOnce prefer the pending edge and skip the risingMenu check for that same action.\n\nThe fix is to merge the pending edge into the menu object inside stepOnce, then activate from that single merged state. This guarantees exactly one activation per step, even if the animation frame loop runs multiple times while a key is held.\n\nAttract mode will swallow keyboard and mouse input. Any real menu edge or pad activity exits attract, clears pending state, and prevents navigation. Mouse clicks are handled immediately in onActivate to avoid duplicate processing in stepOnce.\n\nSpace on the title screen will also call preventDefault. Story confirm repeats roughly every three steps while held, using a held counter. Tab skips the current scene. The Chart Begin menu shows consult options when available and defaults to Begin focused; difficulty changes may adjust without moving focus.\n\nThe Chart Begin menu will keep the focus index on Begin while left and right arrows cycle difficulty. Consult operations will show Careful, Aggressive, and Begin as options. The story chooser will offer Continue or New only when a save exists or cleared scenes remain; otherwise it will skip directly to the Bea question.\n\nThe main menu has five items: Story, Free Play, Grand Rounds, Settings, and About. Story is a single item that branches into either the Continue/New Game chooser or the difficulty question. The hub sequence confirms Story with Space, then Resident with Enter, then advances lines with Space. If the chooser appears, Enter selects Continue or New instead of Resident.\n\nThe default Resident focus is index 1. The new game loop runs S0 first, then steps through storyOrder eight times: broken-glass, growth, adagio, staccato, legato, fermata, coda, and last-patient. Each pair of scenes is followed by a Chart, Operation, Report, and after-scene step. After the final last-patient scene the credits roll and the menu returns.\n\nThe story flow is now fully sequenced. Each operation opens its before-scene, then Chart, then Operation, then Report, then the after-scene. After the last scene it shows credits and returns to the menu.\n\nSave state will use `intro` to track whether S0 was seen, and `storyPos` to index the next operation. Continuing from a save will resume at that position after the intro.\n\nThe fail screen will offer Retry Phase, Restart, Quit, and Bea's Notes when notes are available.\n\nThe pause menu will show Resume, Retry Phase, Restart Operation, Settings, Dev Mode, and Quit outside story mode. In story mode it will show Resume, Skip Scene, Settings, Dev Mode, and Quit. The page pump will not pause when the tab is hidden.\n\nThe dev screen will list buttons from DEV_IDS and sliders from TUNING_PAGES. Each button carries a data-item id matching its DEV_ID so tests can click them.\n\nSettings will have three pages: Audio, Controls, and Display. Changes persist. It controls aimAssist, tipSpeed, glyph toggle, and music gain.\n\nThe About screen will display four headings.\n\nThe About screen will show four always-visible tab labels plus the current page content, with a Credits page included. Q and E keys cycle through all pages including Credits.\n\nThe touch notice will display the exact string \"Controller or keyboard required\" and include a Settings link. Attract mode sits behind it. Any key press or pad input returns to the Title screen. Tapping Settings works via onActivate.\n\nTapping outside the touch notice returns from attract mode. Tapping the notice itself keeps the user on it. A key press exits the notice and returns to the Title screen.\n\nAttract mode activates after ten seconds. It cycles through Title, Main Menu, Free Play, and Touch notice. Operations run for two to two and a half seconds each. Cheats are enabled. The bot drives the simulation. No save occurs. A 250 millisecond interval and an animation frame check trigger attract updates.\n\nThe origin screen is preserved during attract mode. An overlay operation view is also possible.\n\nThe test requires three distinct operation states during attract mode. The simulation will advance operations underneath while the visible screen stays on Title, so state().op changes without changing state().screen.\n\nThe attract mode will paint the operation HUD with a DEMO mark over the origin screen. The test only checks that state().op changes during attract, so state().screen can remain on the origin menu.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-7eb3d016