4,101,32,111,110,101,32,121,111,117,32,104,97,116,101,46,39,41,44,10,53,50,45,32,32,93,44,10,53,51,45,32,32,115,117,99,99,101,115,115,58,32,91,10,53,52,45,32,32,32,32,112,97,99,107,40,39,67,108,111,115,101,100,46,32,67,111,117,110,116,32,116,104,101,32,115,112,111,110,103,101,115,32,108,105,107,101,32,121,111,117,32,109,101,97,110,32,105,116,46,39,41,44,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":1,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/data/barks.js\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":48,\"content\":\"  fail: [\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"f2b94721-df2a-4cac-8364-db70d3804c26"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_569","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I will write the director file. It is going to be long.\n\nI am designing the animation loop to avoid double polling. `paint` will not poll. `stepOnce` polls once per frame. The `frame` function will run `checkAttract`, step the simulation, and then draw. I am also starting the `draw` function.\n\nThe `draw` function uses a `painting` flag to prevent overlapping renders. It syncs the view with simulation state when appropriate, then calls `model.view.render`. The loop will also draw during test mode so screenshots work, but without polling.\n\nstepOnce will also sync the view, so the painting flag prevents double rendering with the animation loop. In test mode, stepOnce skips rendering when norender is set but still syncs the camera. Audio scheduling and playback events will run inside stepOnce. A hold counter for story confirmations is being added.\n\nStory lines advance on the rising confirm edge and again every third held frame. The menu logic will branch to handle story lines separately, returning early to avoid double-advancing. Skip input will use `menu.skip` or `pending.skip`.\n\nTab input merges into a single skip event. On the title screen, any key resumes audio and resets the last input timer. If attract mode is active, it exits immediately. During a session on the Card screen, Enter or Space keys trigger an AIHub ready signal.\n\nThe Card screen also maps the M key to an AIHub \"more\" signal. The Touch notice screen triggers a pending leave on any keypress. On the Title screen, any keypress sets a pending confirm. In other screens, Enter and Space trigger confirm, Escape and Backspace trigger back, Tab triggers skip, and arrow or WASD keys set directional movement. During Operation mode, WASD controls camera movement rather than the menu.\n\nWASD and arrow keys will not set pending menu directions during Operation mode. They only move the camera tip. These inputs only trigger menu navigation when an overlay is open.\n\nThe `inPlay` flag is defined as Operation mode with no overlay and no pause. During this state, Space and Enter will not set pending confirmations, leaving those keys for use and skip. The H key still triggers the Bea assistant during play. Escape during Operation mode opens the pause menu instead of acting as a menu navigation input.\n\nEscape and Backspace will set `pending.pause` during Operation or Story mode, and `pending.back` otherwise. In `stepOnce`, a pause event opens the pause menu and clears pending inputs. The plan is to treat Backspace as a back action in menus rather than a pause key.\n\nDuring Operation mode, Circle on the pad will act as the alt tool instead of pausing. Only Escape and the pad Options button will open the pause menu. The pad back edge is reserved for menu navigation.\n\nDuring menus, both the Escape key and the pad back edge trigger a return to the previous screen. In the Lobby, the pad confirm button joins a session before the menu confirm action.\n\nPad confirm during menus activates the focused item. It is mapped to the Cross button on the pad, while keyboard confirm remains separate. The Lobby screen checks pad confirm before the generic menu confirm logic.\n\nPad confirm joins a seat before the menu confirm action handles the focused item. If both pad and keyboard confirm arrive in one poll, the pad joins while the keyboard confirms the focused option; tests keep these on separate steps.\n\nTwo pads join seats in sequence, and Enter starts the session on the next press. In a keyboard-only lobby, pressing Enter joins seat zero without starting immediately, so the next Enter begins the session.\n\nThe generic menu logic will activate the focused item on confirm, move focus up or down, and switch pages with left and right. Pad up and down also shift focus.\n\nFocus will be clamped to valid bounds. Pending inputs merge into the menu state, but pause is handled separately since it is not part of the sample menu. Touch mode exits to the Title screen when a leave event occurs or a pad button is pressed; interval polls without input will not trigger this exit. The attract check may run at the start of stepOnce.\n\nDuring attract mode, any input will immediately exit attract. The exit function is idempotent, so a single press cannot trigger it twice. Pointer clicks remain in the poll state until takeClick is called, which could retrigger on subsequent polls; stepOnce will call takeClick to clear them.\n\nThe `lastInput` timer will update only when real input is detected, specifically from pad edges, pending flags, or active menu states. It will not count held keyboard keys.\n\nMouse hover will not reset the idle timer. Only pointer movement updates `lastInput`, so a stationary pointer lets attract mode start after ten seconds.\n\nPad sticks past 0.2 will count as real input for the idle timer, so drift does not prevent attract mode from starting.\n\nThe keyboard frame for seat zero is fixed at origin with zero use. Idle pad analog noise is out of scope.\n\n`checkAttract` depends on `lastInput`, so that timer must be set when the Title screen first appears.\n\nA 250 ms interval will run `checkAttract` in non-test mode and step the simulation four times per second during test attract mode. The interval only rotates operations, so it does not need to call `stepOnce`. There is a question about whether `stepOnce` should also invoke `checkAttract` during attract mode.\n\nCalling `checkAttract` from both the interval and `stepOnce` is safe. During attract mode in test, the interval may consume a key meant for `test.step`, but that is correct because any input exits attract. In normal test mode the interval does not step, so `test.step` remains the sole stepper. There is a concern that `checkAttract` inside `stepOnce` could start attract mid-menu during slow tests.\n\nCalling `test.step` without input does not reset the idle timer, so tests that wait eleven seconds will correctly trigger attract mode. The interval must be started in `startStandalone` so that wall-clock waits alone can activate attract. Playwright uses real wall-clock time, making the ten-second idle threshold reliable.\n\nOperation rotation is driven by wall-clock time every 250 ms, independent of frame rate. The attract mode will cycle through three operations at two-second intervals. It will use simulation cheats and will not persist state when started. Exiting attract mode restores the bot flag to false and clears the simulation reference.\n\nThe exit function resets the idle timer and clears pending inputs so attract mode cannot restart immediately. Pointer movement will reset the idle timer on every move event, not just large ones.\n\nThe interval will not recurse into heavy work during attract mode, since the simulation steps at sixteen per second and operation rotation is driven by a timer. In test mode without rendering, state updates still occur even though no draw happens. The design is complete and ready to be written. The dev overlay flag is set when the overlay is 'dev' or the session never existed. During the session card screen, state().screen returns 'Card', but the hub and standalone checks do not rely on that specific name.\n\nThe API state returns null for operation, phase, vitals, timer, and site when the simulation is inactive. Rank resolves from an object ID or string, falling back to null. FocusId maps to the focused item identifier. Glyphs default to 'playstation'. Music gain, tuning, render backend, and attract mode are read directly from audio settings, model state, and view configuration.\n\nThe exact screen names are defined: About, Fail, Report, Chart, Lobby, Settings, Operation, Story, Title, Main Menu, Touch notice, and Credits roll. The reached array is copied with spread syntax. The initial boot screen appears before standalone mode begins.\n\n`startStandalone` sets the Title screen synchronously before awaiting the view. The `__game` global is assigned immediately when the app loads, so tests can read state right after startup without waiting for the view. The Title appears before the first await resolves.\n\nA test that awaits `window.__game` may read the screen before the module finishes initializing. The Title screen will be set synchronously in `createApp` before the connect promise resolves, so standalone tests see it immediately. A brief flash to Title is acceptable because a session card will replace it quickly.\n\nTests will wait for `window.__game?.state().screen === 'Title'` rather than relying on the global alone. The Title screen flashes briefly before a session card replaces it, but this is harmless. A hung connect call is unlikely. Playwright iPhone devices report fine pointers, so touch mode detection should work correctly.\n\nThe touch-mode guard uses `window.matchMedia?.('(any-pointer: fine)')` with a null-safe check. If the API is missing, it defaults to treating the device as having a fine pointer and skips the touch notice. The seen-pad flag starts false at boot.\n\nA body dataset will expose game state for tests. The dev menu splits nine operation jump items into two pages of five and four. Other controls are grouped in chunks of six, with slider pages included.\n\nSlider controls use a `data-slider` attribute and adjust left or right. A reset-page button is included. Activating `op:broken-glass` runs `runDev`, which closes the dev overlay and paints the Operation screen. The `scene-next` operation calls `openScene`. `complete-phase` may set the Report screen directly inside `runDev`, bypassing `setScreen` and potentially leaving music or focus in an inconsistent state.\n\n`syncMusic()` will run at the end of `paint` whenever the track changes, keeping audio state consistent after `runDev`. Focus is clamped to valid range if it falls out of bounds. Slider labels display each tuning value alongside its default. Step sizes are computed as `(max-min)/20` or integer increments. The music slider spans 0 to 1 with a step of 0.01 and defaults to 0.1.\n\nThe `applySlider` function routes music, effects, and voices to their respective audio nodes, updates aimAssist and tipSpeed in both settings and tuning, and writes other tuning keys directly. It skips persistence during attract mode. The glyph toggle switches between playstation and xbox and persists the choice; the next poll refreshes the prompt set if a pad is connected.\n\nRumble, shake, boil, swap, and fullscreen toggles are added to the dev menu. Boil state syncs to the view. Fullscreen requests or exits with error handling. Text speed cycles through 1, 2, and 4. Difficulty is stored in both save and settings objects. Chart left and right buttons change difficulty and persist it without affecting focus. Page index resets on every screen change. Free play, About, Dev, and Settings each have dedicated pages.\n\nSession pause displays settings items as an overlay while the screen remains Operation. Standalone pause keeps the same screen and hides dev controls. The applySettings function omits quit.\n\n`setScreen` will not change the active screen during pause; it only sets the overlay. A `setOverlay` helper resets focus and page index before painting. Back from pause clears the overlay. A `pushScreen` helper saves the current screen to a stack before switching, and `goBack` restores the previous screen or falls back to Main Menu.\n\nSettings opened from pause will return to the pause overlay rather than the previous screen. The back button restores the pause state. Opening settings from the touch notice pushes it onto the stack, so back returns there instead of Title. About opens directly from the menu. Credits roll is reached from the story screen; its skip button returns to Main Menu and clears the stack.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-55a2f2c2-c6e0-4ed1-9257-a974c770045e-44","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"TRACK_IDS","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/audio/music.js","-A":25}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1145,"output_tokens":6250,"cache_read_input_tokens":115328,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"90a1544f-1165-4ed6-83fb-0656395b6450"}
