/app/session.js has been updated.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/app/session.js\",\"edits\":{\"details\":[{\"old_string\":\"    if (!info.server && model.view) {\\n      if (info.isHost && model.sim) model.syncFromSim?.();\\n      else if (model.netState) {\\n        const st = model.netState;\\n        const me = st.seats?.find((s) => s.id === AIHub.me);\\n        if (me) { me.x = local.x; me.y = local.y; me.tool = local.tool; }\\n        try { model.view.sync(st, dt); } catch { /* view catches up next frame */ }\\n        model.view.render?.()?.catch?.(() => {});\\n      }\\n    }\\n    model.paint?.();\",\"old_line\":291,\"new_string\":\"    model.tickAudio?.();\\n    if (!info.server && model.view && !model.painting) {\\n      if (info.isHost && model.sim) model.syncFromSim?.();\\n      else if (model.netState) {\\n        const st = model.netState;\\n        const me = st.seats?.find((s) => s.id === AIHub.me);\\n        if (me) { me.x = local.x; me.y = local.y; me.tool = local.tool; }\\n        model.painting = true;\\n        try { model.view.sync(st, dt); } catch { /* view catches up next frame */ }\\n        const drawn = model.view.render?.();\\n        if (drawn?.then) drawn.then(() => { model.painting = false; }, () => { model.painting = false; });\\n        else model.painting = false;\\n      }\\n    }\\n    model.paint?.();\",\"new_line\":291,\"context_before\":\"    } else if (model.sessionPause) {\\n      model.applySettings?.(sample);\\n    }\\n\",\"context_after\":\"    setTimeout(pump, 16);\\n  })();\\n}\\n\",\"line_prefix\":\"\"}]}}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"c02225f3-f658-40fd-93aa-730c1be496e1"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_572","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"A bug was found: `syncFromSim` on the host is called inside an incorrect conditional.\n\nThe host conditional is actually correct. syncFromSim sets painting internally, and the server path is properly excluded.\n\nThe server may still have an audio context because `createAudio` runs before the server flag is known. I will skip sound output on the server by checking `model.sessionInfo?.server` in `playEvent`.\n\nI will also guard `playTrack` and `tickAudio` against server execution. Now I am writing the director.js file.\n\nI will wrap syncFromSim in try/finally to prevent the painting flag from getting stuck if it throws. I am also updating CSS and structuring director.js carefully.\n\nI verified the input handling logic for pending operations and menu states. Key presses before the step loop correctly set pending flags. Escape pause toggles work properly with held keys. Touch leave events are handled safely. Title and session card inputs use their own dedicated paths.\n\nI checked the card input path for double answers. The pump only polls pad edges, so it cannot double-trigger menu.confirm from a held Enter key. I also added repeat guards to card keydown handlers so Enter and M do not answer repeatedly.\n\nI will keep director.js under 900 lines and write main.js next. About data will load from the tag. I am designing a signature function for paint to avoid rebuilding innerHTML every frame, which would destroy card click targets and cause flicker.\n\nThe paint signature now covers screen state, card readiness, flow, phase ID, and session flags. When the signature matches, only live patches for vitals, timer, score, combo, bark, wheel, and ECG are applied. Phase changes update the objective and include the phase ID in the signature.\n\nThe wheel highlight changes too often to include in the paint signature, so it will be patched live instead. The patch function updates vitals text and toggles tool button classes based on the current tool state. Bark text is also included in the signature so the speech bubble updates correctly.\n\nVitals will be excluded from the paint signature to avoid constant rebuilds. Card seconds, countdown numbers, and attract operation index are included so they update each second. The focus class is also in the signature so hover states rebuild correctly. The first paint always runs because the initial signature is null.\n\nI will implement `currentItems()` as a pure function. `setScreen` now resets focus via `defaultFocus()`, and `setOverlay` preserves the screen while resetting focus and page. Story ask mode sets the screen to Story, enables storyMode, and initializes focus to 1.\n\n`openAsk` sets `storyMode` to `'ask'` and `flow` to `'story'` before calling `setScreen`, so `defaultFocus` correctly returns 1. `openScene` loads a scene, resets the line counter, sets `storyMode` to `'lines'`, and begins playback from the first line.\n\n`openScene` now triggers the scene set and the first line. `advanceLine` steps to the next line, updates the speaker mouth, toggles shouting, plays audio, and repaints. I am now writing `finishScene`.\n\n`finishScene` calls the scene completion callback and clears it. `beginStory` resets the save state and opens the difficulty ask screen. `confirmDifficulty` saves the difficulty setting, then either opens the intro scene or resumes the story from the saved position. `afterS0` marks the intro as complete and persists the save.\n\n`openOpStory` advances through the story order. It opens the intro scene first, then each operation's chart screen. `openChart` sets the operation ID and consult choice, then switches to the Chart screen. `startOperation` begins an operation by setting its ID.\n\n`startOperation` resets the result display and overlay. It builds a new `Sim` instance using the operation ID, seed, physics flag, tuning, difficulty, consult choice, and player settings. The boot function then attempts to initialize physics unless testing is active or explicitly disabled.\n\nThe boot function now frees any existing physics instance before creating a new simulation. It then switches to the Operation screen, marks it reached, clears the overlay, syncs music, shows the OR view, and paints.\n\nA race condition is acceptable: if the user confirms the operation while physics is still initializing, steps simply do nothing until the simulation appears.\n\nTest mode forces physics off, but the boot function remains async because the await sits inside a conditional block.\n\nThe async boot function in `startOperation` causes a race: test mode still yields a microtask, so `test.step` can run before the simulation exists. The fix is to mount the simulation synchronously when testing or physics is disabled.\n\nConfirmed the test step ordering: `feedSim` runs after `handleMenu`, so the simulation is ready for the next step. Now writing the Report continue handler.\n\n`continueReport` routes the player based on flow state. It returns to Lobby in couch mode, Free Play in free mode, or opens the operation's post-scene. `afterScene` advances through the story order or shows credits. The report screen will also record data.\n\n`recordResult` saves the operation rank and marks the operation complete in story mode. It updates the story position and pushes the operation ID to the cleared list. The function exits early if attract mode, session mode, or a missing simulation is detected. A notes item appears when `sim.notesOffer` is true.\n\nAccepting notes triggers retryPhase and returns to the operation screen. Retrying a phase also calls retryPhase. Restarting reopens startOperation with the same flow. Quitting returns to the Main Menu while keeping the simulation alive, though this may leave an operation ID visible on the menu.\n\nQuitting to the menu now frees physics and clears the simulation so the operation ID disappears from the menu. Physics will not be freed during a session. The attract mode replacement function first frees any existing simulation's physics, then creates a new simulation with physics disabled.\n\nAttract mode now uses a cheat list that locks vitals, freezes the timer, and grants infinite composure and clamps. The attract cycle rotates through story operations plus a few extras every two seconds. Exiting attract mode disables the bot, frees any active physics, and clears both the simulation and operation ID.\n\n`startAttract` initializes the attract state, mounts the first story operation, and begins playback. `checkAttract` exits early when a session is active.\n\n`checkAttract` exits early when an overlay is active or the current screen is not idle. It starts attract mode after ten seconds of inactivity. During attract playback, it advances to the next story operation every two seconds and mounts it. Activity during attract mode causes `stepOnce` to exit before feeding the simulation.\n\nDouble rotation from the interval and stepOnce is harmless because the two-second cycle is idempotent. Calling checkAttract at the start of stepOnce is safe since the Operation screen is not an idle state. The ten-second attract threshold measures real time, not simulation time.\n\nA busy lock prevents the attract interval from re-entering stepOnce while a test.step is already running. In non-test mode, only the animation frame loop steps the simulation.\n\nThe busy lock prevents the interval from stepping while a test warp is active. During a warp, stepOnce will not render at all; only the animation frame loop will paint. This avoids thousands of redundant draws.\n\n`api.test.step` now runs `stepOnce` in a loop. View synchronization is skipped during norender tests. Real play will sync and draw inside `draw()`, letting the animation frame handle both. `pointerToField` needs the camera updated on resize.\n\nThe HUD ECG will be drawn as a div instead of a canvas. The `data-prompt-set` attribute is read by tests from the document element, and `input.poll` updates it. To prevent tests from seeing an empty value before any input occurs, this attribute is initialized to `'keyboard'` in `createApp`. The glyph set defaults to PlayStation.\n\nThe lobby will show a visual join indicator for each seat. Seat colors are defined as red, blue, green, and yellow. A joined set tracks which indices have joined. The couch start function sorts the joined indices and begins the grand-rounds operation.\n\nThe couch start function maps joined seat indices to player objects with names and colors, then passes them to `startOperation` as the `players` array. The simulation handles this list automatically.\n\nDuring couch playback, `feedSim` iterates over the sample seats. It skips any seat whose index is not in the joined set, finds the matching seat in the simulation, and feeds it a prepared frame.\n\nA keyboard sample for seat 0 is valid even when no pad is connected, because the joined set can include that index while the device remains keyboard.\n\n`prepFrame` copies the input frame and applies `pendingTool` to seat 0 only. It also suppresses redundant site deltas by comparing against a held value per seat. The override logic targets the first simulation seat.\n\nOverride input is fed to the first simulation seat and persists across steps until cleared. A helper function maps operation IDs to readable titles for dev tool labels, while raw IDs are used for shorter labels. Slider pages will be placed after button pages in the dev menu.\n\nDev tool pages will group all IDs into chunks of six, including operations alongside tuning controls. Slider rows will not trigger `runDev`; only action items do. The reset-page button must receive the current tuning page's argument keys.\n\nThe reset-page button will pass its current tuning page keys to `runDev` so the correct arguments are restored. The Settings menu is being structured as a two-row grid with volume sliders for music, effects, and voices, plus toggle switches for glyphs and rumble.\n\nThe Settings menu now includes aim assist, swap sticks, tip speed, fullscreen, screen shake, line boil, and text speed. A nudge function is being added to adjust the currently focused item.\n\nThe nudge function cycles the difficulty setting through intern, resident, and attending when the Chart screen is active. For slider items, it adjusts the value by the step amount and clamps it to the item's range.\n\nThe nudge function now toggles and cycles settings. Glyphs switch between Xbox and PlayStation. Fullscreen toggles the display mode. Line boil enables or disables the effect via the view. Text speed cycles through 1, 2, and 4. Aim assist writes tuning values. Music gain is set directly. Button labels include the current value so tests can read it.\n\nThe Chart screen will show patient name, age, job, note, difficulty, consult marks, and a begin button. Difficulty is text-only and does not count as an item, keeping the total at seven or fewer.\n\nFree play mode will list unlocked story operations in pages of four. Difficulty and doctor cycle are shown as header text rather than menu items to stay under the limit.\n\nThe `unlocked` helper returns true for any operation whose story index is at or before the current `storyPos`, so the first operation appears immediately. Free Play cards are labeled with their title and rank stamp. The Q and E keys will cycle pages on Free Play, About, Settings, and overlay dev screens.\n\nThe About screen will have five pages: four content blocks plus credits. It will use tabs as focusable items so up and down keys navigate them, while Q and E still cycle the page. The heading itself will act as the button for each tab.\n\nThe Credits tab will list backend contributors. The Source Information page will show a few short JSON fields per view to avoid a dense wall, since the full source has fifteen fields.\n\nAll source fields will be kept in the DOM for the test that requires every required field to be on screen. Hidden subpages are not acceptable because Playwright textContent can still read hidden elements, and getByText may fail to locate them.\n\nThe Source Information page will list all fifteen fields in a wrapping column. At 1080p with 2rem text, the height is about six hundred pixels and fits comfortably under the tabs. The test will verify that the four headings are visible and that `#about-data` parses correctly. The Credits tab will include the backend renderer name alongside the contributor list.\n\nA helper function will parse the `#about-data` element into a cached model object. If that element is missing, the five About headings will fall back to hardcoded constants mapped to their keys. Source information will display as `Date: ...` lines.\n\nThe session card DOM is being built with a title heading, tagline paragraph, goal text, and a seats list container.\n\nThe session card loop creates a colored seat div for each player, showing their name and a ready indicator. A vitals paragraph displays the remaining seconds.\n\nThe session card now includes a controls note and Ready and More Time buttons. A button helper creates item elements with focus styling. The countdown will use large text. Round over displays a simple message. During Operation the pause overlay paints the HUD and settings together.\n\nThe Operation HUD will use HTML strings for menus and DOM builders for the session card and HUD seats. Sim seat names are hub names passed through info.players, so they are escaped before insertion to prevent XSS.\n\nSeat names will be inserted as plain text via `esc`, never as attributes, to avoid quote-related XSS. Colors from the hub will be validated against a hex pattern before being applied as inline styles. The next step is writing the main JavaScript file, CSS, and build configuration.\n\nThe fail bark omits the word \"good\". A malpractice display is being added. During operation, pressing the D-pad down edge will trigger a Bea question. Stick-down events were considered but rejected because they would spam the prompt while navigating menus.\n\nThe D-pad down edge is being narrowed to pad button 13 only, since the input system does not expose that button separately from stick-down events. This prevents stick-down from falsely triggering Bea during tool selection.\n\nThe Bea prompt will fire once per rising D-pad down edge during operation, provided the up edge is not held and no overlay is active. A mapping object is being defined to connect simulation actions such as scalpel cuts, defibrillation, and suturing to their corresponding audio effects.\n\nThe audio effect map now covers gates, lasers, alarms, flatlines, stamps, and UI sounds. `say` will play a named voice line and set the bark bubble. `bark` will call `say` with Bea as the default speaker. Phase changes may be handled as an optional event.\n\n`setHush` will be wired to the simulation's hush phase. A `musicFor` helper will select the correct background track based on the current mode and screen, falling back to generic titles or rounds for sessions and operations.\n\nThe `musicFor` helper now routes Chart, Report, and Fail screens to the tense track. A `quiet()` guard will skip audio playback on the server by returning early from `playTrack` and `playEvent`. `tickAudio` will still run there as a no-op. The `setHush` filter will duck volume without restarting the music track. `scheduleAhead` will be called every tick.\n\nThe server will not create the view, leaving that to session.js. A `syncFromSim` function will push simulation state into the renderer at sixty frames per second, setting a painting flag around the sync and render calls and clearing it afterward. A `presented` helper is being defined to prepare the simulation state for presentation.\n\nA `withOrgan` helper is being added to fill in the missing `organ` field on network state. It looks up the operation and site by ID, defaults to the first site or 'forearm' if none match, and catches lookup errors to ensure the value is always present.\n\nThe session client branch will call `model.decorateState?.(st)` before syncing state to the renderer. The host path already uses `presented()` for this. In `startStandalone`, the nogfx branch now creates a pointer camera and sets the backend to none.\n\nThe nogfx fallback now paints a friendly screen overlay that can be dismissed with confirm so menus remain usable. The text stays in the DOM for test visibility.\n\nThe nogfx overlay will block input until a Continue button clears the notice. Session failure is handled by session.js, not the director, so standalone mode will skip calling fail directly. A `wantsTouch` helper is being drafted to detect touch input at startup.\n\nA `wantsTouch` helper detects touch devices by checking for a coarse pointer and whether no pad has been seen yet. If touch is detected, the startup screen becomes a Touch notice while attract mode remains active. The key button returns to Title instead of settings. Once a pad is polled, the screen advances to Title on the next step. This transition might conflict with attract mode.\n\nrequestAnimationFrame and the main interval are started inside startStandalone. A single visibility listener is registered in createApp. Fullscreen changes will sync settings. An api object is being exposed for tests, providing state, audio, devRun, and test helpers like step, warp, input override, bot control, and skipTo.\n\nThe test API now includes a `skipTo` helper that advances the simulation to a specific phase while preserving flow state and clearing result display. A `hash()` method returns the simulation hash. The `state()` function will return a rank as an ID string or null. Tip data is mapped from seat objects with id, x, y, tool, and score. FocusId comes from current menu items and is null during operation unless paused. The dev overlay is active when overlay equals 'dev'. A reached array tracks completed objectives.\n\nThe state helper now reads screen, operation, phase, vitals, timer, score, BPM, beat, site, and tool from the simulation object. When the simulation is absent during a network client session, it falls back to model.netState. It also exposes musicGain and tuning.\n\nA menu back map is being defined. Settings, About, Free Play, Lobby, Chart, Story ask, and Credits will return to the previous screen or main menu. Story lines and Fail will open pause instead of exiting. Touch settings back returns to the touch notice. A stack helper pushes the current screen before switching. The back function is being drafted.\n\nThe back function now handles overlays and the screen stack. It closes the dev overlay to pause, settings, or the previous overlay. It exits pause and chooser. On Chart it returns to Free Play or Main Menu. Otherwise it pops the stack or falls back to Main Menu.\n\nOpening settings from the pause overlay will switch the screen to Settings instead of using an overlay. This keeps the simulation paused and allows back navigation to return to Operation.\n\nSession mode will pause via a sessionPause overlay instead of changing screens. Standalone mode will use an openSettings helper that stores the current screen and overlay, clears the overlay, and sets the screen to Settings. A closeSettings helper restores the saved state.\n\nThe closeSettings helper restores the saved overlay and screen, or falls back to Main Menu. setScreen clears overlays, so the overlay must be reapplied after the screen change.\n\nPause resume clears the overlay. Opening dev from pause keeps the Operation screen; feedSim pauses when the dev overlay is active, and state().devOpen becomes true for tests.\n\nQuitting to menu from pause resets the simulation and clears both the overlay and screen.\n\nSingle-stepping and bot behavior both freeze during pause because feedSim is not called. The Story lines menu item will use the ID \"advance\" so that confirm advances the line instead of activating. Hold logic for Story lines will advance every third step and on the rising frame, but it must avoid double-advancing on that same frame.\n\nThe Story lines hold logic now advances the line on the rising confirm frame and every third held step, resetting the counter after each advance. Clicking the advance button also triggers the line advance directly, but this does not double-fire because pointerup only calls onActivate once. Tab still skips the scene.\n\nKey bindings are being wired. Any key on the title screen triggers a pending confirm that activates the start item. Space on the menu confirms the story item. Activating story opens the chooser overlay if the player has prior save data, otherwise it opens the ask screen. Choosing continue in the chooser starts scene S0 after the intro.\n\nChoosing continue in the story chooser resumes from the saved position. It opens the next operation's pre-scene if that operation is complete, or restarts the current one if the player quit early. The New option resets all progress. Resident mode defaults focus to 1 and activates on Enter. The title screen shows a single \"Press any button\" item that confirms with Enter.\n\nThe title screen will treat any key press as a confirm, including letters and arrows. Because stepOnce returns immediately after activating the start item, the same pending confirm cannot also trigger a menu item on the next screen in that same step.\n\nThe hub sequence does not need explicit test.step calls because requestAnimationFrame runs stepOnce, and keydown events are consumed within 16 milliseconds. The 500 millisecond gaps between keys keep them as separate steps. The animation frame must start before the view await so that title rendering and input processing continue during slow scrubbing.\n\nTab will be intercepted in onKey to preventDefault and keep focus on the window. Key repeat is ignored so held Enter does not fast-forward; held.confirm from polling handles that instead. Space held during story lines still triggers confirm for fast-forward.\n\nAbout page index now tracks focus so the body matches the selected tab. Q and E switch pages in About, Settings, and Dev. Up and down arrows move focus within each page. A live slider input listener will handle range element events.\n\nThe live slider input listener will patch the readout label directly instead of triggering a full paint rebuild. This prevents the slider element from being destroyed mid-drag. writeSetting accepts an optional silent flag to skip the label update when called from continuous input events.\n\nThe dev tuning slider will use its key as the setting ID and check against TUNING_DEFAULTS. Readout labels will show both the current value and its default. The session client decorateState patch is being applied, and syncFromSim correctly skips painting when model.painting is already true.\n\nStandalone mode avoids double rendering because the session pump is not running. The draw() animation frame now skips rendering when the mode is session, the view is missing, or painting is already in progress. It only renders during Operation, Story, Report, Chart, or attract mode. Story scenes still update the canvas so the lamp swings, but they skip entity synchronization every frame.\n\nThe draw() function now branches by screen. Story scenes render the current line's character via showStory. Operation and attract mode render via showOr and sync. The Chart screen will also call showOr to avoid a blank canvas.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-31a7096e-0f67-4973-b848-e5c15774b108-50","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/app/session.js","old_string":"        const st = model.netState;\n        const me = st.seats?.find((s) => s.id === AIHub.me);\n        if (me) { me.x = local.x; me.y = local.y; me.tool = local.tool; }\n        model.painting = true;","new_string":"        const st = model.netState;\n        const me = st.seats?.find((s) => s.id === AIHub.me);\n        if (me) { me.x = local.x; me.y = local.y; me.tool = local.tool; }\n        model.decorateState?.(st);\n        model.painting = true;"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-31a7096e-0f67-4973-b848-e5c15774b108-51","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/ui/ui.css","old_string":".who { font-size: 1.6rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }","new_string":".who { font-size: 2.2rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-31a7096e-0f67-4973-b848-e5c15774b108-52","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/ui/ui.css","old_string":".pages { display: flex; gap: 1rem; font-size: 1.8rem; }\n.page { display: none; font-size: 2rem; line-height: 1.25; }\n.page.on { display: block; }","new_string":".pages, .tabs { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.8rem; font-size: 2.2rem; }\n.page { display: none; font-size: 2.2rem; line-height: 1.25; }\n.page.on { display: block; }\n.tabs button { font-size: 2.2rem; background: transparent; border: 0; border-bottom: 0.2rem solid transparent; padding: 0.3rem 0.4rem; cursor: pointer; }\n.tabs button.on { border-color: #f4efe4; }"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-31a7096e-0f67-4973-b848-e5c15774b108-53","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/ui/ui.css","old_string":".wheel button { width: 4.4rem; height: 3rem; font-size: 1.1rem; background: #10282c; border: 0.15rem solid #f4efe4; border-radius: 0.4rem; }","new_string":".wheel button { width: 7rem; height: 3.4rem; font-size: 2.2rem; background: #10282c; border: 0.15rem solid #f4efe4; border-radius: 0.4rem; }"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-31a7096e-0f67-4973-b848-e5c15774b108-54","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/ui/ui.css","old_string":".sound { position: absolute; bottom: 0; left: 0; font-size: 1.6rem; }","new_string":".sound { position: absolute; bottom: 0; left: 0; font-size: 2.2rem; }\n.card { gap: 0.8rem; }\n.touch-copy { font-size: 3.2rem; max-width: 40rem; }\n.row { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; font-size: 2.4rem; }\n.pick { outline: 0.2rem solid #f5b700; }\n.nogfx p, .about-line { font-size: 2.2rem; margin: 0.2rem 0; }\nbody[data-screen=\"Title\"], body[data-screen=\"Boot\"] { background: #17707A; }\n.hud .obj, .hud .vitals, .hud .timer { text-shadow: 0 0.08rem 0 #0B2529; }"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8140,"output_tokens":14444,"cache_read_input_tokens":124544,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"6a48c42c-6911-4706-b2ae-0344da654c6e"}
