{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_732","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Hub tests passed with zero failures and 23 successes.\n\nBrowser tests passed 80 and simulation tests passed 51. Remaining tasks include updating about.json counts, writing exit-interview.json and PROGRESS.md, launching the root HTML with WebGPU flags, and capturing screenshots and logs.\n\nLaunches and package.log are confirmed. 1920×1080 shots will be attempted if browser time allows.\n\nAbout.json will gain a sentence with the real test counts: 51 simulation, 80 browser, and 23 hub. Then the project rebuilds.\n\nThe browser suite will be re-run after the about.json edit and rebuild. This ensures the log matches the final HTML bytes. Hub tests are unaffected.\n\nThe browser suite will be re-run after the rebuild so its log matches the final HTML. Hub tests are skipped because the about.json change does not alter session code. The exit interview can be prepared in parallel with that browser run.\n\nThe about.json update will read: \"The sim suite (51 checks) and the hub harness (23 checks) caught the issues that would have shipped. The browser suite (80 checks) caught UI timing: a pause confirm was swallowed for 350 ms after attract, and a reload cleared the list of screens the playthrough had already visited.\"\n\nThe about.json update will be minimal. It adds the test counts to the first testing answer without rewriting the existing personality text. The second answer about critical issues remains unchanged. The sourceInformation testing field may also mention the counts.\n\nThe exit interview will mirror the about.json testing feedback. Both files will be updated to the same final text, then rebuilt so the HTML inlines the about data.\n\nExit-interview.json will contain the game identity block. It includes the title \"Alive in Common Time\", tagline, description, type, genre, source, series, player count, controls, and tags.\n\nThe identity description will be copied exactly for the exit interview. A script will validate the exit interview, confirm names, client hash, banner, banned names, Math.random in simulation, and TODOs to generate package.log. Two root HTML launches with five flags each will verify zero page errors, correct canvas size, high painted fraction, driven input changes to tip or HUD, and a second capture.\n\nLaunches will be saved as launch-1.png, launch-2.png, and launch.log. The script must confirm window.__game exists before trusting the page.\n\nLaunch.log will record canvas size, painted fraction, page errors, and tip delta. The two PNGs will be the post-input frames showing the game drawn. Measuring painted fraction via WebGPU canvas readback is possible but risks a blank readback.\n\nCanvas drawing buffer size will be checked via canvas.width and canvas.height, with non-background pixels counted to measure painted fraction. The viewport is likely 1280×720. The norender flag is excluded from launches because it would prevent rendering; test=1 is used instead so the simulation steps and the view draws. loop() still calls draw() in test mode, though it skips sim stepping on requestAnimationFrame.\n\nThe launch script is being drafted. It opens the page at 1280×720, waits for window.__game to exist and reach the Title screen, skips to the broken-glass state, pauses briefly to let a frame draw, then captures the initial canvas frame.\n\nThe launch script will read canvas dimensions and client size. It will move the tip by disabling aim assist and driving a stub pad or keyboard input. The Three.js WebGPU renderer may need preserveDrawingBuffer enabled so toDataURL does not return blank.\n\nIf the canvas readback is blank, the Playwright screenshot itself will serve as proof. A small script can sample non-background pixels from that PNG to measure painted fraction.\n\nI am weighing Python PIL, ffmpeg, and pngjs to analyze the PNG if canvas readback fails. I will check for these tools when writing the launch script. I also need to decide whether to re-run the full browser suite after rebuilding.\n\nThe browser suite will run in the background while exit-interview and package checks are written. Package.log validation can run immediately after exit-interview is ready, since it needs no browser.\n\nPROGRESS.md will list the completed suites and the remaining launch captures. It will omit the title string.\n\nThe hand-in folder will omit the title string. The goal plan checklist boxes will be flipped to true only after launches and package.log are complete. The identity description is being checked for the exit interview.\n\ndocs/AUTHORING.md must exist. The about.json update will keep apostrophes only in JSON fields, not in meta content. The exit interview will mirror about.json exactly for the four feedback arrays and source information. The description is \"A rain-soaked surgical melodrama at St. Alder.\"\n\nThe about.json description will read: \"A rain-soaked surgical melodrama at St. Alder. Cut, clamp and keep time with an engineered parasite while the head nurse judges your hands. One to four surgeons, one patient.\" Player count uses an en dash. PROGRESS.md will be replaced entirely. The launch script is drafted for execution after the browser suite.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The hub harness passed: 0 failed, 23 passed. I'll record the real test counts, rebuild the page, and finish the hand-in files."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e9cdd6c1-a3ba-46b6-8386-abccecb082fb-101","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/data/about.json","old_string":"    \"testing\": \"Headless sim suite, Playwright Chromium and WebKit, hub harness. No physical controller, no second human, no real iOS device.\",","new_string":"    \"testing\": \"Headless sim suite 51 checks, Playwright browser suite 80 checks on Chromium and WebKit, hub harness 23 checks. No physical controller, no second human, no real iOS device.\","}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e9cdd6c1-a3ba-46b6-8386-abccecb082fb-102","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/data/about.json","old_string":"    \"The sim suite and the hub harness caught the issues that would have shipped. UI timing and prompt-set bugs showed up only when a real page drove the sim.\",","new_string":"    \"The sim suite (51 checks) and the hub harness (23 checks) caught the issues that would have shipped. The browser suite (80 checks) caught UI timing: attract was swallowing the next confirm, and a reload was dropping screens the playthrough had already opened.\","}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e9cdd6c1-a3ba-46b6-8386-abccecb082fb-103","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/PROGRESS.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e9cdd6c1-a3ba-46b6-8386-abccecb082fb-104","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/config/identity.js"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":268,"output_tokens":4024,"cache_read_input_tokens":162432,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"a6de7630-7e87-44cb-aa89-aa071f704c54"}
