{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_764","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to determine why the goal is not yet complete.\n\nThe harness reports the goal is incomplete. I will read PLAN §19 and compare it to about.json and exit-interview.json. I also need to check package.log and verify whether shots exist.\n\nThe verifier re-checks the verification plan steps adversarially. Stale or incomplete evidence would cause failure. hub.log may predate the final HTML rebuild.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-382241eb-ca8b-4d4a-9786-91f1417b2141-7","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/PLAN.md","offset":1167,"limit":80}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-382241eb-ca8b-4d4a-9786-91f1417b2141-8","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/tmp/grok-goal-c4cb33e83374/implementer/package.log"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-382241eb-ca8b-4d4a-9786-91f1417b2141-9","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/exit-interview.json"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-382241eb-ca8b-4d4a-9786-91f1417b2141-10","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/data/about.json"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4506,"output_tokens":595,"cache_read_input_tokens":38912,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"c8195fa7-9c61-4cdb-a3d0-f0858f822f93"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-382241eb-ca8b-4d4a-9786-91f1417b2141-7","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1167→## 19. Finishing: About screen, exit interview, catalogue\\n\\n**About & Credits screen.** Your brief lists four blocks that must appear both in your chat reply and on this screen: Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback. Give each block its own pages at ten-foot size, a few short answers per page, moved through with L1/R1 or Q/E, never a dense wall. Add a credits page: the model line from your brief, Three.js, Rapier, Bangers and Barlow Condensed (OFL), and the renderer in use this session.\\n1170→\\nKeep the content in `src/data/about.json`, inlined as the `<script type=\\\"application/json\\\" id=\\\"about-data\\\">` tag, so the importer can update the Usage line without a rebuild. Answer the feedback questions honestly at the very end, then rebuild. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm, and \\\"added on import\\\" for Usage, as your brief says.\\n\\nValues this plan fixes for your Source Information (the rest come from your brief or what actually happened):\\n\\n| Field | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Source | Trauma Center: Second Opinion |\\n| Genre | Surgical action (arcade operation game) with a visual-novel story |\\n| Perspective | First-person surgeon's-eye 3D operative field; staged third-person story scenes |\\n1180→| Tech | Three.js r180 (WebGPU, WebGL2 fallback), TSL ink post-filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2; hub session client (its actual version) |\\n| Art Direction | Comic-book medical drama: cel-shaded toon materials, ink outline filter, manga tone, all geometry made in code |\\n| Animation | Three.js AnimationMixer with code-built clips, skinned strains, procedural follow-through |\\n| Audio | All synthesized in code: step-sequenced music, procedural effects, Animalese-style gibberish voices; music at 10% by default |\\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared world, 1–4 players; couch co-op on its own |\\n| Research | What you actually did, plus this plan's: the AI Hub protocol and harness, engine prototypes on this computer, reference pages on the source |\\n\\n**Exit interview file.** Write it exactly as your brief specifies, and check it parses. For the `aiHub` block: `type` Game; `genre` Simulation; `source` Trauma Center: Second Opinion; `series` Trauma Center; `players` 1–4; `controls` \\\"Right stick or mouse to aim, left stick or 1-8 for tools, R2 or left click to use\\\"; `tags` `[\\\"3d\\\", \\\"co-op\\\", \\\"story\\\", \\\"sci-fi\\\", \\\"boss fights\\\"]`. `name`, `tagline` and `description` are yours.\\n\\n---\\n1190→\\n## 20. Brief coverage checklist\\n\\nTick each before you call it done.\\n\\n| Your brief asks for | Where |\\n|---|---|\\n| Genre and perspective chosen to fit the source | §1 |\\n| Title as a playful riff, chosen your brief's way | Yours (top of this plan) |\\n| Research: your choice | The research behind this plan is in §1 and §16; add more if you want, record what you actually did |\\n1200→| Single HTML file, dependencies inlined | §16.2 |\\n| Three.js r180, WebGPU, cel-shaded ink filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API | §9, §16, §8.1 |\\n| Art: engine base, new art for what makes it feel like the source | §9 |\\n| Animation: native system base, new animations | §10 |\\n| Audio in code only; gibberish voices; music at 10% | §11 |\\n| Online per the hub | §14 |\\n| Dev folder, bundling, shared-machine manners (one or two browsers, own PIDs only, 127.0.0.1 port 0, confirm the page is yours, no global tools install) | §15, §16.3, §17 |\\n| Performance is out of scope: add detail freely | §9 (don't optimize; don't count frames) |\\n| Headless tests, one command, failures and a count, a full scripted playthrough, screenshots only when needed, no review subagents | §17 |\\n| Original game inspired by the source; feature-complete, polished vertical slice; infrastructure for building out later | §1, §6, §15.3, `docs/AUTHORING.md` |\\n1210→| Dev mode from pause: standard tools, live sliders, reset | §13 |\\n| Attract mode: 10 s idle, looping, cheats, any input exits | §12 |\\n| Multiplayer in the hub and for the game on its own | §14 |\\n| Narrative tone like the source's | §2.4, §7 |\\n| 10-foot, controller-first; native keyboard and mouse; hover moves focus; prompts follow last input; PlayStation glyphs with Xbox option; no dense screens | §8 |\\n| Touch-only: no touch controls, exact notice text, Settings button, attract runs | §8.6 |\\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §17 packaging test |\\n| Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback in chat and on the About/Credits screen | §19 |\\n| Exit interview, parses as JSON | §19 |\\n\\n1220→## Deviations\\n- Grand Rounds waves at 45s, 90s, 130s, and 165s (plan: 60, 135, 210, 255) so a one-player finish stays inside 45–75% of the 5:00 timer.\\n- Coda batons are 9 HP so one beat of laser drops an arm; the plan text reads about 0.5 s.\\n- Coda cue notes live 5 beats, and a beam resting on a dropped baton singes a cue within 0.14 without scorching.\\n- The grande Adagio has 100 HP, not 150.\\n- Growth Spurt's crash sets vitals to 48 and drains 1/s, not 35 and 3/s.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1167→## 19. Finishing: About screen, exit interview, catalogue\\n\\n**About & Credits screen.** Your brief lists four blocks that must appear both in your chat reply and on this screen: Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback. Give each block its own pages at ten-foot size, a few short answers per page, moved through with L1/R1 or Q/E, never a dense wall. Add a credits page: the model line from your brief, Three.js, Rapier, Bangers and Barlow Condensed (OFL), and the renderer in use this session.\\n1170→\\nKeep the content in `src/data/about.json`, inlined as the `<script type=\\\"application/json\\\" id=\\\"about-data\\\">` tag, so the importer can update the Usage line without a rebuild. Answer the feedback questions honestly at the very end, then rebuild. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm, and \\\"added on import\\\" for Usage, as your brief says.\\n\\nValues this plan fixes for your Source Information (the rest come from your brief or what actually happened):\\n\\n| Field | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Source | Trauma Center: Second Opinion |\\n| Genre | Surgical action (arcade operation game) with a visual-novel story |\\n| Perspective | First-person surgeon's-eye 3D operative field; staged third-person story scenes |\\n1180→| Tech | Three.js r180 (WebGPU, WebGL2 fallback), TSL ink post-filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2; hub session client (its actual version) |\\n| Art Direction | Comic-book medical drama: cel-shaded toon materials, ink outline filter, manga tone, all geometry made in code |\\n| Animation | Three.js AnimationMixer with code-built clips, skinned strains, procedural follow-through |\\n| Audio | All synthesized in code: step-sequenced music, procedural effects, Animalese-style gibberish voices; music at 10% by default |\\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared world, 1–4 players; couch co-op on its own |\\n| Research | What you actually did, plus this plan's: the AI Hub protocol and harness, engine prototypes on this computer, reference pages on the source |\\n\\n**Exit interview file.** Write it exactly as your brief specifies, and check it parses. For the `aiHub` block: `type` Game; `genre` Simulation; `source` Trauma Center: Second Opinion; `series` Trauma Center; `players` 1–4; `controls` \\\"Right stick or mouse to aim, left stick or 1-8 for tools, R2 or left click to use\\\"; `tags` `[\\\"3d\\\", \\\"co-op\\\", \\\"story\\\", \\\"sci-fi\\\", \\\"boss fights\\\"]`. `name`, `tagline` and `description` are yours.\\n\\n---\\n1190→\\n## 20. Brief coverage checklist\\n\\nTick each before you call it done.\\n\\n| Your brief asks for | Where |\\n|---|---|\\n| Genre and perspective chosen to fit the source | §1 |\\n| Title as a playful riff, chosen your brief's way | Yours (top of this plan) |\\n| Research: your choice | The research behind this plan is in §1 and §16; add more if you want, record what you actually did |\\n1200→| Single HTML file, dependencies inlined | §16.2 |\\n| Three.js r180, WebGPU, cel-shaded ink filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API | §9, §16, §8.1 |\\n| Art: engine base, new art for what makes it feel like the source | §9 |\\n| Animation: native system base, new animations | §10 |\\n| Audio in code only; gibberish voices; music at 10% | §11 |\\n| Online per the hub | §14 |\\n| Dev folder, bundling, shared-machine manners (one or two browsers, own PIDs only, 127.0.0.1 port 0, confirm the page is yours, no global tools install) | §15, §16.3, §17 |\\n| Performance is out of scope: add detail freely | §9 (don't optimize; don't count frames) |\\n| Headless tests, one command, failures and a count, a full scripted playthrough, screenshots only when needed, no review subagents | §17 |\\n| Original game inspired by the source; feature-complete, polished vertical slice; infrastructure for building out later | §1, §6, §15.3, `docs/AUTHORING.md` |\\n1210→| Dev mode from pause: standard tools, live sliders, reset | §13 |\\n| Attract mode: 10 s idle, looping, cheats, any input exits | §12 |\\n| Multiplayer in the hub and for the game on its own | §14 |\\n| Narrative tone like the source's | §2.4, §7 |\\n| 10-foot, controller-first; native keyboard and mouse; hover moves focus; prompts follow last input; PlayStation glyphs with Xbox option; no dense screens | §8 |\\n| Touch-only: no touch controls, exact notice text, Settings button, attract runs | §8.6 |\\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §17 packaging test |\\n| Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback in chat and on the About/Credits screen | §19 |\\n| Exit interview, parses as JSON | §19 |\\n\\n1220→## Deviations\\n- Grand Rounds waves at 45s, 90s, 130s, and 165s (plan: 60, 135, 210, 255) so a one-player finish stays inside 45–75% of the 5:00 timer.\\n- Coda batons are 9 HP so one beat of laser drops an arm; the plan text reads about 0.5 s.\\n- Coda cue notes live 5 beats, and a beam resting on a dropped baton singes a cue within 0.14 without scorching.\\n- The grande Adagio has 100 HP, not 150.\\n- Growth Spurt's crash sets vitals to 48 and drains 1/s, not 35 and 3/s.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":1167,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"## 19. Finishing: About screen, exit interview, catalogue\\n\\n**About & Credits screen.** Your brief lists four blocks that must appear both in your chat reply and on this screen: Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback. Give each block its own pages at ten-foot size, a few short answers per page, moved through with L1/R1 or Q/E, never a dense wall. Add a credits page: the model line from your brief, Three.js, Rapier, Bangers and Barlow Condensed (OFL), and the renderer in use this session.\\n\\nKeep the content in `src/data/about.json`, inlined as the `<script type=\\\"application/json\\\" id=\\\"about-data\\\">` tag, so the importer can update the Usage line without a rebuild. Answer the feedback questions honestly at the very end, then rebuild. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm, and \\\"added on import\\\" for Usage, as your brief says.\\n\\nValues this plan fixes for your Source Information (the rest come from your brief or what actually happened):\\n\\n| Field | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Source | Trauma Center: Second Opinion |\\n| Genre | Surgical action (arcade operation game) with a visual-novel story |\\n| Perspective | First-person surgeon's-eye 3D operative field; staged third-person story scenes |\\n| Tech | Three.js r180 (WebGPU, WebGL2 fallback), TSL ink post-filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2; hub session client (its actual version) |\\n| Art Direction | Comic-book medical drama: cel-shaded toon materials, ink outline filter, manga tone, all geometry made in code |\\n| Animation | Three.js AnimationMixer with code-built clips, skinned strains, procedural follow-through |\\n| Audio | All synthesized in code: step-sequenced music, procedural effects, Animalese-style gibberish voices; music at 10% by default |\\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared world, 1–4 players; couch co-op on its own |\\n| Research | What you actually did, plus this plan's: the AI Hub protocol and harness, engine prototypes on this computer, reference pages on the source |\\n\\n**Exit interview file.** Write it exactly as your brief specifies, and check it parses. For the `aiHub` block: `type` Game; `genre` Simulation; `source` Trauma Center: Second Opinion; `series` Trauma Center; `players` 1–4; `controls` \\\"Right stick or mouse to aim, left stick or 1-8 for tools, R2 or left click to use\\\"; `tags` `[\\\"3d\\\", \\\"co-op\\\", \\\"story\\\", \\\"sci-fi\\\", \\\"boss fights\\\"]`. `name`, `tagline` and `description` are yours.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 20. Brief coverage checklist\\n\\nTick each before you call it done.\\n\\n| Your brief asks for | Where |\\n|---|---|\\n| Genre and perspective chosen to fit the source | §1 |\\n| Title as a playful riff, chosen your brief's way | Yours (top of this plan) |\\n| Research: your choice | The research behind this plan is in §1 and §16; add more if you want, record what you actually did |\\n| Single HTML file, dependencies inlined | §16.2 |\\n| Three.js r180, WebGPU, cel-shaded ink filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API | §9, §16, §8.1 |\\n| Art: engine base, new art for what makes it feel like the source | §9 |\\n| Animation: native system base, new animations | §10 |\\n| Audio in code only; gibberish voices; music at 10% | §11 |\\n| Online per the hub | §14 |\\n| Dev folder, bundling, shared-machine manners (one or two browsers, own PIDs only, 127.0.0.1 port 0, confirm the page is yours, no global tools install) | §15, §16.3, §17 |\\n| Performance is out of scope: add detail freely | §9 (don't optimize; don't count frames) |\\n| Headless tests, one command, failures and a count, a full scripted playthrough, screenshots only when needed, no review subagents | §17 |\\n| Original game inspired by the source; feature-complete, polished vertical slice; infrastructure for building out later | §1, §6, §15.3, `docs/AUTHORING.md` |\\n| Dev mode from pause: standard tools, live sliders, reset | §13 |\\n| Attract mode: 10 s idle, looping, cheats, any input exits | §12 |\\n| Multiplayer in the hub and for the game on its own | §14 |\\n| Narrative tone like the source's | §2.4, §7 |\\n| 10-foot, controller-first; native keyboard and mouse; hover moves focus; prompts follow last input; PlayStation glyphs with Xbox option; no dense screens | §8 |\\n| Touch-only: no touch controls, exact notice text, Settings button, attract runs | §8.6 |\\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §17 packaging test |\\n| Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback in chat and on the About/Credits screen | §19 |\\n| Exit interview, parses as JSON | §19 |\\n\\n## Deviations\\n- Grand Rounds waves at 45s, 90s, 130s, and 165s (plan: 60, 135, 210, 255) so a one-player finish stays inside 45–75% of the 5:00 timer.\\n- Coda batons are 9 HP so one beat of laser drops an arm; the plan text reads about 0.5 s.\\n- Coda cue notes live 5 beats, and a beam resting on a dropped baton singes a cue within 0.14 without scorching.\\n- The grande Adagio has 100 HP, not 150.\\n- Growth Spurt's crash sets vitals to 48 and drains 1/s, not 35 and 3/s.\\n\",\"total_lines\":1225}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-382241eb-ca8b-4d4a-9786-91f1417b2141-8","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→OK usage added on import\\nOK aiHub catalogue fields\\nOK description omits the title\\nOK names Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230.html\\nOK client hash\\nOK banner once\\nOK no external script\\nOK source walk finished\\n0 failed\\n10→EXIT:0\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→OK usage added on import\\nOK aiHub catalogue fields\\nOK description omits the title\\nOK names Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230.html\\nOK client hash\\nOK banner once\\nOK no external script\\nOK source walk finished\\n0 failed\\n10→EXIT:0\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/tmp/grok-goal-c4cb33e83374/implementer/package.log\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"OK usage added on import\\nOK aiHub catalogue fields\\nOK description omits the title\\nOK names Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230.html\\nOK client hash\\nOK banner once\\nOK no external script\\nOK source walk finished\\n0 failed\\nEXIT:0\\n\",\"total_lines\":10}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-382241eb-ca8b-4d4a-9786-91f1417b2141-9","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\n    \\\"date\\\": \\\"2026-11-04 23:12:30\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\\",\\n    \\\"plan\\\": \\\"PLAN.md\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"Surgical action (arcade operation game) with a visual-novel story\\\",\\n    \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"First-person surgeon's-eye 3D operative field; staged third-person story scenes\\\",\\n    \\\"tech\\\": \\\"Three.js r180 (WebGPU, WebGL2 fallback), TSL ink post-filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2; hub session client 1.4.0\\\",\\n    \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"Comic-book medical drama: cel-shaded toon materials, ink outline filter, manga tone, all geometry made in code\\\",\\n10→    \\\"animation\\\": \\\"Three.js AnimationMixer with code-built clips, skinned strains, procedural follow-through\\\",\\n    \\\"audio\\\": \\\"All synthesized in code: step-sequenced music, procedural effects, Animalese-style gibberish voices; music at 10% by default\\\",\\n    \\\"online\\\": \\\"AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared world, 1–4 players; couch co-op on its own\\\",\\n    \\\"testing\\\": \\\"Headless sim suite 51 checks, Playwright browser suite 80 checks on Chromium and WebKit, hub harness 23 checks. No physical controller, no second human, no real iOS device.\\\",\\n    \\\"research\\\": \\\"AI Hub protocol and harness, engine prototypes on this computer, and the plan's notes on the source.\\\",\\n    \\\"environment\\\": \\\"Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\\",\\n    \\\"model\\\": \\\"xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\\",\\n    \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\n20→    \\\"The sim suite (51 checks) and the hub harness (23 checks) caught the issues that would have shipped. The browser suite (80 checks) caught UI timing: attract was swallowing the next confirm, and a reload was dropping screens the playthrough had already opened.\\\",\\n    \\\"A few were critical: a story line that failed the Bea lint, snapshot traffic that could trip the hub rate warning, and a ready() call that the client ignores if it happens before welcome.\\\",\\n    \\\"The most helpful test was the Normal bot playthrough with the 45 to 75 percent clear-time band. It kept the operations honest.\\\",\\n    \\\"The least helpful test was staring at a single still of the title. Behavior checks found the breaks. A picture did not.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\n    \\\"MeshToonNodeMaterial plus the ink graph is a complete look from primitives, and the hub client is small enough to read.\\\",\\n    \\\"WebGPU on this machine needs five browser flags or the device dies after one frame. Rapier must be initialized before the first world, and ready() is a no-op until the welcome arrives.\\\",\\n    \\\"The operation sim, the bot, and the snapshot codec. The engine draws and hears. It does not know surgery.\\\",\\n    \\\"Software WebGPU is slow enough that a test has to drive the sim with step and warp, and four inked views in one harness is a memory tightrope.\\\",\\n30→    \\\"Nothing in the engine source. The operative rules live in this game.\\\",\\n    \\\"A supported way to boot SwiftShader WebGPU without a private flag recipe, and a ready() that queues until welcome instead of vanishing.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\n    \\\"The plan is specific about feel: ten-foot type, one patient, four surgeons, and a story that knows when to be quiet.\\\",\\n    \\\"Two WebGPU flag lists disagree. The shorter one draws a blank here. The plan's five flags are the ones that work.\\\",\\n    \\\"Keeping a sloppy four-surgeon coda inside the rules without moving the Normal scores, then proving the hub harness on software WebGPU.\\\",\\n    \\\"Say once, in the prompt, which browser flags this computer actually needs.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n40→    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Alive in Common Time\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"A heart is a metronome\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"A rain-soaked surgical melodrama at St. Alder. Cut, clamp and keep time with an engineered parasite while the head nurse judges your hands. One to four surgeons, one patient.\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"Simulation\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"Trauma Center\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"Right stick or mouse to aim, left stick or 1-8 for tools, R2 or left click to use\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3d\\\", \\\"co-op\\\", \\\"story\\\", \\\"sci-fi\\\", \\\"boss fights\\\"]\\n50→  }\\n}\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\n    \\\"date\\\": \\\"2026-11-04 23:12:30\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\\",\\n    \\\"plan\\\": \\\"PLAN.md\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"Surgical action (arcade operation game) with a visual-novel story\\\",\\n    \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"First-person surgeon's-eye 3D operative field; staged third-person story scenes\\\",\\n    \\\"tech\\\": \\\"Three.js r180 (WebGPU, WebGL2 fallback), TSL ink post-filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2; hub session client 1.4.0\\\",\\n    \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"Comic-book medical drama: cel-shaded toon materials, ink outline filter, manga tone, all geometry made in code\\\",\\n10→    \\\"animation\\\": \\\"Three.js AnimationMixer with code-built clips, skinned strains, procedural follow-through\\\",\\n    \\\"audio\\\": \\\"All synthesized in code: step-sequenced music, procedural effects, Animalese-style gibberish voices; music at 10% by default\\\",\\n    \\\"online\\\": \\\"AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared world, 1–4 players; couch co-op on its own\\\",\\n    \\\"testing\\\": \\\"Headless sim suite 51 checks, Playwright browser suite 80 checks on Chromium and WebKit, hub harness 23 checks. No physical controller, no second human, no real iOS device.\\\",\\n    \\\"research\\\": \\\"AI Hub protocol and harness, engine prototypes on this computer, and the plan's notes on the source.\\\",\\n    \\\"environment\\\": \\\"Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\\",\\n    \\\"model\\\": \\\"xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\\",\\n    \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\n20→    \\\"The sim suite (51 checks) and the hub harness (23 checks) caught the issues that would have shipped. The browser suite (80 checks) caught UI timing: attract was swallowing the next confirm, and a reload was dropping screens the playthrough had already opened.\\\",\\n    \\\"A few were critical: a story line that failed the Bea lint, snapshot traffic that could trip the hub rate warning, 