{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_799","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Focus navigation works when pressing the down arrow four times.\n\nThree down arrows from the main menu landed on Lobby instead of Settings. The title flow is being re-examined to find the focus offset.\n\nThe screenshot during the title flow captures the attract HUD. The main menu is confirmed correct. The suspicion is that the first ArrowDown press hits while blockMenu is still active from exiting attract mode.\n\nThe first Enter press exits attract mode and sets blockMenu. Because the keyup already happened, blockMenu clears in the same step. pending.confirm then activates the start screen. This explains why the title flow lands on Main Menu instead of the title screen.\n\nThe stepOnce logic confirms the first Enter press exits attract mode and activates the start screen. The main menu focus is now at zero. The remaining puzzle is why three down arrows from this position land on Lobby instead of Settings.\n\nThe input.js key mapping is being checked to confirm ArrowDown maps to down. A new theory suggests menu hover may be interfering: if Playwright's mouse cursor sits over a menu item, the hover logic could snap focus back to that item every step and prevent the down arrow from moving focus correctly.\n\nThe About screen was reached successfully with four down arrows. This suggests the mouse cursor was not hovering over a menu item during that test, or hover does not override focus after movement. The Escape sequence from Lobby is now being examined.\n\nThe Escape sequence from Lobby returned to Main Menu with focus on Story. This confirms the menu indices are correct: four downs from zero reach About, so three downs should reach Settings. The suspicion is now that the first down arrow press was lost, or that focus was not actually at zero after the main menu shot.\n\nA new helper function is being drafted to navigate directly to a menu item by pressing down until the focus ID matches the target. This avoids relying on fixed arrow counts that may have been miscounted.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-39cbc57b-0102-463b-8e2b-f857bd421b26-84","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/tmp/grok-goal-c4cb33e83374/implementer/shots/title.png"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-39cbc57b-0102-463b-8e2b-f857bd421b26-85","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/tmp/grok-goal-c4cb33e83374/implementer/shots/main-menu.png"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-39cbc57b-0102-463b-8e2b-f857bd421b26-86","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/tmp/grok-goal-c4cb33e83374/implementer/shots/about.png"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-39cbc57b-0102-463b-8e2b-f857bd421b26-87","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/tmp/grok-goal-c4cb33e83374/implementer/shots/story-shout.png"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2366,"output_tokens":1555,"cache_read_input_tokens":135040,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"216da67f-845d-4b92-a5b7-2acf30f1fbfc"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-39cbc57b-0102-463b-8e2b-f857bd421b26-84","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"ImageContent\":{\"data\":\"\",\"mime_type\":\"image/jpeg\"}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-39cbc57b-0102-463b-8e2b-f857bd421b26-85","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"ImageContent\":{\"data\":\"\",\"mime_type\":\"image/jpeg\"}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-39cbc57b-0102-463b-8e2b-f857bd421b26-86","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"ImageContent\":{\"data\":\"\",\"mime_type\":\"image/jpeg\"}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-39cbc57b-0102-463b-8e2b-f857bd421b26-87","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"ImageContent\":{\"data\":\"\",\"mime_type\":\"image/jpeg\"}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"2a5b24de-1757-4cf8-96da-1eb4cf7cba10"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_800","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The HUD portrait is a giant upside-down or cropped head filling the top of the screen. It appears as a huge pale sphere, likely the top of a head or chin, dominating the operative field.\n\nThe portrait plane is positioned in front of the camera but with incorrect UV orientation and scale. The camera's lookAt rotation may have misaligned local Y with screen-up, causing the portrait to render at the top.\n\nCamera local Y should be screen-up, so y=-0.55 ought to sit below center. The blob at the top may instead be the patient's body viewed from above, with the torso filling the frame because the camera is too close or angled incorrectly.\n\nThe pale mass at the top may be the patient's abdominal skin organ geometry positioned as a giant sphere. The title shot could be the growth-spurt phase, while the main menu shows the broken-glass gel phase.\n\nThe pale dome appears in every OR shot, so it is not organ-specific. It likely comes from the portrait bust plane, a lamp, or a shared body part. The portrait plane may be rendering the crown or blank top of the face texture because it sits too close to the camera.\n\nThe plane is 0.7 units wide at distance 1.4. The camera's vertical field of view is 34 degrees, so the visible half-height at that distance is about 0.428. Only the top strip of the plane falls within the camera's viewable range. That top edge should appear at the bottom of the screen, not the top.\n\nThe vitals number \"100\" sits at the top center of the HUD. The pale shape appears to be behind that number, confirming it rests at the top of the 3D view.\n\nThe camera sits at positive Z and Y, looking toward the origin. A large organ mesh at the near edge of the table would appear at the bottom of the screen, not the top. This makes the patient's body an unlikely source for the pale dome.\n\nObjects farther from the camera appear higher on screen. The pale shape at the top may be the patient's head mesh sticking into the frame from the far side of the table. This would explain why it appears as a chin or neck.\n\nThe head may be oversized and overlapping the operative field. I will examine the actual image and the organ or body construction to verify.\n\nThe portrait faces appear upside down or on the underside of the head spheres. The hair cap sits on top, while the face texture lands in the lower hemisphere of the sphere. This is a classic Three.js sphere UV problem: the canvas paints the face in the vertical middle-lower area of a 1024x512 texture, which maps to the bottom half of the sphere.\n\nThe hair cap placement is correct. The face texture is landing on the chin and may be inverted. I am checking Three.js SphereGeometry UV mapping to see if the front of the sphere corresponds to the center of the 1024x512 canvas.\n\nThe left bust faces the camera, but the right bust is rotated to face left toward the other character. The story camera looks at the busts from +Z, so only the left face should be visible. This suggests the portrait plane may be rendering the wrong bust or the UV mapping is misaligned.\n\nThe canvas-to-UV math shows the face texture should map to the upper hemisphere. Canvas y=232 corresponds to v=0.55, which is slightly above the equator. This contradicts the story description that places the face on the bottom of the head.\n\nThe hair cap is a sphere geometry covering the upper hemisphere. It hides the painted face texture beneath it, leaving only the mouth visible below. This explains why the face appears on the lower portion of the head.\n\nThe fix is to shrink the hair cap so it only covers the top third of the head, or paint the face lower on the texture. The OR portrait is a rendered bust texture on a plane. If the face is hidden under the hair cap, the portrait will show a bald dome.\n\nThe bust camera looks slightly downward at the head. If the face texture is on the bottom or back of the sphere, the camera sees the pale skin-colored rear of the head. That back-of-head view is the pale dome in the portrait.\n\nThe portrait plane is too large and sits at the top of the screen, covering the surgical field. It should be small and positioned at the bottom.\n\nI will fix both the face UV mapping and the portrait placement in the geometry code.\n\nI also need to scale the HUD fonts up: the `.where` text should be at least 2.125rem, and the timer, score, and BPM numbers should be 3.5rem instead of 38px.\n\nThe About page text sits at y=990, so its bottom edge lands at 1034. That clears the 1026 safe-area limit, though it still risks clipping by the system tray. The audit flagged this as unsafe because the bottom edge exceeds the 5% margin. The page also forms a dense wall of source fields, which violates the plan to keep answers short and avoid dense walls.\n\nThe About screen leaves the 3D view active behind it, so the giant head and syringe remain visible through the text. The source fields form a dense wall that overlaps the 3D head. The tabs at the top also overlap the head geometry.\n\nThe main menu image was actually the attract-mode operation HUD, not the main menu screen. The log recorded screen=Main Menu, but the visible UI showed \"Press any button\" and \"Disinfect the wound\" and DEMO. This means the main menu was never captured; only the attract mode was.\n\nThe 3D view should show a clean backdrop or title scene when the user is not in an active operation or story. A leftover attract-mode portrait with surgical props behind the text is wrong.\n\nThe log claims main-menu.png shows the main menu, yet the image clearly displays an operation HUD. The two descriptions differ: one shows abdomen and bowel tabs with Bea, while the other shows wound disinfection and Ines without site tabs. They are separate operations, not a hallucination.\n\nThe screenshot is taken after state sampling, so the log accurately records the main menu. The operation HUD visible in that image must be a later frame captured during the 1.8-second wait.\n\nThe main menu HTML may be transparent, letting the attract-mode 3D canvas show through behind it. The vision model could also have focused on the 3D background and missed the menu overlay. If the menu text is actually present but the vision model ignored it, that would explain why the log records Main Menu while the image shows an operation HUD.\n\nThe state snapshot is taken before paint, so model.screen should already reflect the main menu. The vision model may have missed the menu because its logo blends into the background or sits off-screen.\n\nThe audit found zero issues for the main menu, confirming the text is on screen and large enough. The vision model still described the 3D background instead of the menu. This suggests the menu may be hidden behind the 3D canvas or the screenshot truly captured the operation HUD.\n\nThe main menu HTML replaces the entire UI, so the operation HUD should not exist in that screenshot. The vision model described HUD elements anyway, which is impossible if the state was truly Main Menu. The image files may be stale or confused by the image tool.\n\nThe two screenshots are clearly different operations, not a stale file. The 1800ms wait occurs before state sampling, so attract mode cannot start during that interval. The vision model likely described the 3D canvas because the menu text is present but invisible to it.\n\nThe audit found zero text issues, confirming the main menu is fully visible and correctly positioned. The CSS places the menu inside an absolutely positioned container with pointer-events disabled, so it should stack above the 3D canvas. The vision model’s description of the 3D background instead of the menu overlay is therefore unexpected.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-3915e213-323c-49f7-86ce-ab7bd8d60c30-88","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"id=\\\"view\\\"|id=\\\"ui\\\"|id=\\\"app\\\"","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src","glob":"*.{js,css,html}"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-3915e213-323c-49f7-86ce-ab7bd8d60c30-89","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"id=\\\"view\\\"|id=\\\"ui\\\"","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/tools/build.mjs"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6178,"output_tokens":4264,"cache_read_input_tokens":137344,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"bcad908f-7392-4e76-ad53-e87ab5ceb08e"}
