{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_907","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"A crash occurred at line 212, the first `page` reference.\n\nThe crash likely stems from paint(false) in the finally block. Rebuilding the HUD may trigger input events that recursively call stepOnce, causing infinite recursion.\n\nTracing the recursion hypothesis shows no direct loop: paint does not call stepOnce. Setting innerHTML might fire a synchronous input event, but that would trigger onKey rather than stepOnce. Thirty paint calls should not crash the tab.\n\nThe prior successful run processed 1000 steps without per-step paint, confirming the crash is caused by paint. A full HUD rebuild is not heavy enough to cause an OOM in 30 calls. The real issue may be a pre-existing loop: draw() calls showOr(), which calls resize(), which calls renderer.setSize() every frame.\n\nA target crash means the renderer process died, not an uncaught exception. Thirty paints should not OOM. The current theory is that paint(false) triggers an infinite loop that hangs until the tab is killed.\n\nThe infinite loop theory is ruled out. paint() does not call stepOnce, and patchLive does not trigger stepping. paint() only updates its own signature variable, so it cannot cause a recursive hang.\n\nA DOM event firing paint during innerHTML assignment is unlikely. The 250ms interval cannot re-enter stepOnce because JavaScript is single-threaded; it queues until the evaluate returns. Thirty synchronous steps should not crash the tab. The cause of the crash remains unclear.\n\nThe crash may be a memory exhaustion issue. Prior webgpu contexts could have left the page near its limit, and 30 extra paint calls pushed it over. The same failure point in both runs confirms paint-per-step is the trigger. I am now re-examining the paint function for hidden recursion.\n\nThe 250ms timer and rAF loop are inactive during operation, so only test.step drives the 30 synchronous stepOnce calls. Each call triggers paint, but the HUD rebuild is small and should not exhaust memory. The GPU workload from bleeders and particles was handled fine in the prior successful run, so the crash likely has a different cause.\n\nA WebGPU device loss might explain the target crash when combined with DOM rebuilds. I will inspect shots.mjs around line 200 to verify the exact failure point. Since paint(false) already skips redundant rebuilds, the crash may instead come from paint throwing an exception that kills the renderer. I plan to add a guard and logging to isolate the cause.\n\nA smaller reproduction is impractical given the time limit. The safer fix is to move the HUD update from stepOnce into draw(). draw() already runs every animation frame and only patches text, so it avoids the 30 synchronous paint calls during the evaluate burst. Bark text will still need to be handled in patchLive or draw(), since draw() does not update the bark DOM.\n\nMoving paint(false) into draw() fixes the crash. During test.step(30), the animation frame loop is blocked, so draw() never runs and paint does not fire. After the evaluate returns, draw() executes once and paints once. The HUD will show the latest numbers and bark text without rebuilding the DOM thirty times.\n\nThe implementation will remove paint from the stepOnce finally block and add it at the end of draw(). The session pump already calls model.paint(), so draw() returns early for sessions to avoid double updates. The order will be sync scene, paint HUD, then render. A model.painting flag will guard against overlapping paints.\n\nThe `model.painting` guard is set at the start of `draw()` and never checked inside `paint()`, so overlapping paints are prevented. The updated `draw()` function now runs `sync` and `present()` before calling `paint(false)` for Operation or Attract screens.\n\nThe empty catch block in draw() ensures the painting flag is always cleared even if paint or showOr throws. The test.step flow now waits for two animation frames before screenshotting, so draw() and paint() run once after the evaluate returns. Bark text updates correctly during hush.\n\nThe next step is to revert the paint-in-finally change and rebuild. Menu shots from the crashed run already reflect the new build, but Operation shots of calm and hush were captured with the old code. After moving paint into draw(), the calm shot should look identical since the animation frame will paint before the screenshot. Rebuilding and rerunning is required to verify.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-3d329422-0ae3-4aae-9839-f147645c1406-145","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/app/director.js","old_string":"      tickAudio();\n    } finally {\n      busy = false;\n      if (model.screen === 'Operation' || model.attract.on) paint(false);\n    }\n  }","new_string":"      tickAudio();\n    } finally {\n      busy = false;\n    }\n  }"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-3d329422-0ae3-4aae-9839-f147645c1406-146","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src/app/director.js","old_string":"      if (model.sim && (model.attract.on || model.screen === 'Operation')) {\n        model.view.showOr?.();\n        model.view.sync(decorateState(model.sim.state), 1 / 60);\n      } else if (model.screen === 'Story') present();\n    } catch { /* draw again next frame */ }","new_string":"      if (model.sim && (model.attract.on || model.screen === 'Operation')) {\n        model.view.showOr?.();\n        model.view.sync(decorateState(model.sim.state), 1 / 60);\n      } else if (model.screen === 'Story') present();\n      if (model.screen === 'Operation' || mo