the Normal bot playthrough with the 45 to 75 percent clear-time band. It kept the operations honest.\\\",\\n    \\\"The least helpful test was staring at a single still of the title. Behavior checks found the breaks. A picture did not.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\n    \\\"MeshToonNodeMaterial plus the ink graph is a complete look from primitives, and the hub client is small enough to read.\\\",\\n    \\\"WebGPU on this machine needs five browser flags or the device dies after one frame. Rapier must be initialized before the first world, and ready() is a no-op until the welcome arrives.\\\",\\n    \\\"The operation sim, the bot, and the snapshot codec. The engine draws and hears. It does not know surgery.\\\",\\n    \\\"Software WebGPU is slow enough that a test has to drive the sim with step and warp, and four inked views in one harness is a memory tightrope.\\\",\\n30→    \\\"Nothing in the engine source. The operative rules live in this game.\\\",\\n    \\\"A supported way to boot SwiftShader WebGPU without a private flag recipe, and a ready() that queues until welcome instead of vanishing.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\n    \\\"The plan is specific about feel: ten-foot type, one patient, four surgeons, and a story that knows when to be quiet.\\\",\\n    \\\"Two WebGPU flag lists disagree. The shorter one draws a blank here. The plan's five flags are the ones that work.\\\",\\n    \\\"Keeping a sloppy four-surgeon coda inside the rules without moving the Normal scores, then proving the hub harness on software WebGPU.\\\",\\n    \\\"Say once, in the prompt, which browser flags this computer actually needs.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n40→    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Alive in Common Time\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"A heart is a metronome\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"A rain-soaked surgical melodrama at St. Alder. Cut, clamp and keep time with an engineered parasite while the head nurse judges your hands. One to four surgeons, one patient.\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"Simulation\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"Trauma Center\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"Right stick or mouse to aim, left stick or 1-8 for tools, R2 or left click to use\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3d\\\", \\\"co-op\\\", \\\"story\\\", \\\"sci-fi\\\", \\\"boss fights\\\"]\\n50→  }\\n}\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/exit-interview.json\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\n    \\\"date\\\": \\\"2026-11-04 23:12:30\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\\",\\n    \\\"plan\\\": \\\"PLAN.md\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"Surgical action (arcade operation game) with a visual-novel story\\\",\\n    \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"First-person surgeon's-eye 3D operative field; staged third-person story scenes\\\",\\n    \\\"tech\\\": \\\"Three.js r180 (WebGPU, WebGL2 fallback), TSL ink post-filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2; hub session client 1.4.0\\\",\\n    \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"Comic-book medical drama: cel-shaded toon materials, ink outline filter, manga tone, all geometry made in code\\\",\\n    \\\"animation\\\": \\\"Three.js AnimationMixer with code-built clips, skinned strains, procedural follow-through\\\",\\n    \\\"audio\\\": \\\"All synthesized in code: step-sequenced music, procedural effects, Animalese-style gibberish voices; music at 10% by default\\\",\\n    \\\"online\\\": \\\"AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared world, 1–4 players; couch co-op on its own\\\",\\n    \\\"testing\\\": \\\"Headless sim suite 51 checks, Playwright browser suite 80 checks on Chromium and WebKit, hub harness 23 checks. No physical controller, no second human, no real iOS device.\\\",\\n    \\\"research\\\": \\\"AI Hub protocol and harness, engine prototypes on this computer, and the plan's notes on the source.\\\",\\n    \\\"environment\\\": \\\"Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\\",\\n    \\\"model\\\": \\\"xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\\",\\n    \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\n    \\\"The sim suite (51 checks) and the hub harness (23 checks) caught the issues that would have shipped. The browser suite (80 checks) caught UI timing: attract was swallowing the next confirm, and a reload was dropping screens the playthrough had already opened.\\\",\\n    \\\"A few were critical: a story line that failed the Bea lint, snapshot traffic that could trip the hub rate warning, and a ready() call that the client ignores if it happens before welcome.\\\",\\n    \\\"The most helpful test was the Normal bot playthrough with the 45 to 75 percent clear-time band. It kept the operations honest.\\\",\\n    \\\"The least helpful test was staring at a single still of the title. Behavior checks found the breaks. A picture did not.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\n    \\\"MeshToonNodeMaterial plus the ink graph is a complete look from primitives, and the hub client is small enough to read.\\\",\\n    \\\"WebGPU on this machine needs five browser flags or the device dies after one frame. Rapier must be initialized before the first world, and ready() is a no-op until the welcome arrives.\\\",\\n    \\\"The operation sim, the bot, and the snapshot codec. The engine draws and hears. It does not know surgery.\\\",\\n    \\\"Software WebGPU is slow enough that a test has to drive the sim with step and warp, and four inked views in one harness is a memory tightrope.\\\",\\n    \\\"Nothing in the engine source. The operative rules live in this game.\\\",\\n    \\\"A supported way to boot SwiftShader WebGPU without a private flag recipe, and a ready() that queues until welcome instead of vanishing.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\n    \\\"The plan is specific about feel: ten-foot type, one patient, four surgeons, and a story that knows when to be quiet.\\\",\\n    \\\"Two WebGPU flag lists disagree. The shorter one draws a blank here. The plan's five flags are the ones that work.\\\",\\n    \\\"Keeping a sloppy four-surgeon coda inside the rules without moving the Normal scores, then proving the hub harness on software WebGPU.\\\",\\n    \\\"Say once, in the prompt, which browser flags this computer actually needs.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Alive in Common Time\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"A heart is a metronome\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"A rain-soaked surgical melodrama at St. Alder. Cut, clamp and keep time with an engineered parasite while the head nurse judges your hands. One to four surgeons, one patient.\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"Simulation\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"Trauma Center\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"Right stick or mouse to aim, left stick or 1-8 for tools, R2 or left click to use\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3d\\\", \\\"co-op\\\", \\\"story\\\", \\\"sci-fi\\\", \\\"boss fights\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\n\",\"total_lines\":51}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa56-6231-7cf0-a666-df5fbb090710","uuid":"2f02753a-7621-4090-a8a3-eaf3b87e4001"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_978","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Significant deviations exist from PLAN.md's explicit operation scripts.\n\nThe Growth Spurt crash was tuned to 48 vitals and 1/s drain. PLAN §17 requires min vitals to hold at 35 or above. The original 35/3 setting risks dropping below that threshold before Hush activates.\n\nThe testing section explicitly permits tuning data to prevent difficulty spikes. This overrides the narrative values for crash vitals, Grande Adagio HP, wave times, and coda baton HP. Core behaviors remain intact: the crash still occurs, Hush still triggers, Grande still has two tails and bleeders, and coda batons can still be lasered.\n\nThe goal plan itself documents these numerical deviations as accepted. Refuting them would invent requirements beyond the contract.\n\nThe deviations were appended as a separate note rather than weakening the acceptance criteria. Criterion 3 still requires PLAN §5 behavior, so an exact match is required. Criterion 4 specifies Normal clear times between 45% and 75%. These strict numerical targets may be unmet.\n\nThe parScore invariant is the higher-priority target. Tuning numbers to meet it is explicitly permitted by §17, so deviations should not be refuted.\n\nI will now hunt for real bugs by carefully reviewing the tests. I need to inspect rules.mjs, verify whether tests actually import the simulation, and spot-check critical mechanics in browser and hub theater tests.\n\nThe session card displays 6166 instead of a countdown. PLAN requires a 3-2-1 countdown from AIHub.time(). This suggests AIHub.time() returns milliseconds rather than seconds. The agent's final response places the countdown with the seat list.\n\nThe hub suite passed 23 checks including the card. Its own validation likely only confirms round completion and button responses, not the countdown display format. PLAN §14.4 explicitly requires a 3-2-1 countdown from `AIHub.time()`. The raw millisecond value of 6166 would be a genuine visual bug.\n\nThe scalpel overlapping the title is a ten-foot type defect. The verification plan and goal plan both state that type, ink, and overlap issues do not gate refutes. Criterion 5 covers multiplayer, hub, and couch behavior.\n\nCriterion 5 requires a shared-world session with seats 1–4. The own card enters Grand Rounds directly, skipping title, menus, attract, story, dev, and saves. Pause is Settings-only while the simulation keeps running. Network traffic flows only through the hub. The host alone runs the simulation on a timer independent of animation frames, including a viewless server copy. Clients render snapshots. Local tips update instantly. `finish()` is host-only and reports standings, scores, won status, stats, and awards. In couch mode up to four seats share one screen; two pads join, move two tips independently, and both score.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-175f5617-c5c6-489f-b3e0-eeb46f98d590-19","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"countdown|cardTime|seconds left|More Time|6166|answer\\('ready'\\)|data-countdown","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/src","glob":"*.{js,css,html}"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-175f5617-c5c6-489f-b3e0-eeb46f98d590-20","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"test\\(|ok\\(|check\\(|assert","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/tests","glob":"*.mjs","head_limit":100}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-175f5617-c5c6-489f-b3e0-eeb46f98d590-21","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-231230__x8f49b6/work/Alive in Common Time-Trauma Center: Second Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-231230/tests/run-all.mjs","li